        <p>While - the British Treasury are about to find funds for the *erection of a statue to Cromwell in, orin the neighbourhood 'äf, the Houses of Parlia- ment, the Dutch are ahotiiitn unveil one to his con- temporary, 'De Ruyter, at Flushing. Though De Ruyter's genius as a naval commander had not fully developed itself duiing the maritime war which was carried on'betvieen the( English and the Dutch during he closing days of the Long Parliament and the opening ones of the Protectorate, he had given promise of the greater deeds which he was to alcomplish in the time of the-Second Charles,when he daringly forced his way up the Thames and burned Chatham. De Ruyter, too, was the servant of a Government': hostile ,to .the princely house which aftermards ascended —the throne of the ' Netherlands, and it. is significant of the extent to which old animnsities have died out that it is the Queen of Netherlands who is to perform the cere- mony. No one could reasonably expect the statue of _Cromwell tb:leceive a similar honour at the hands of any member of our Royal House. De R_uyter was pot a- politicimandifiokno part in the inTrignes at The Pague, and therefcite On that side of his character did not resemble Crcmwell. The true parallel would be netween Cromwell and John de Witrt, and nO soiereign of the Netherlands is ever likely-to unveil a Statue of the greatest enemy of the reigning house. ' • •</p>
        <p>The self-dispersion throughout the country of the LonciAnarchists was the .necessary sequence of the strictness with which they have been watched by the metropolitan police. But it must be only the inore active and enterprising members of the gang who are seeking in the seclusion of the pro- vinces'the privacy which they cannot find in Lon- don. The lower and less dangerous grade of them will still haunt!the open spaces of the .metropolis and preach itheir gospel, when their audience per- tnits.thettik' . The street audiences, however, while they listen patiently to any quantity of good ortho- dox Radicalism presented to them inithe old ortho- dox way; give the shortest's.,hiift to an,One profess- ing Anarchism. One orator, who, Acknowledged _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ himself-to. have been. lately a member-Of the Social Democratic Federation,find apparently has detached Illinself from that; body to loin the Anarchists, met /with soe rough.- treatment on• Sunday night- on jjanipstetid Heath, whllo' he was denouncing the Royal Family and was oiag 11A, nent. preaching the doctrine of assassination as the drE,Ad of the police permitted him to do. But there was an amount of wholesome. common sf-nise, in the crowd which ;would have nothing to do with bomb-throwing and 'the assassinig (Mager, and ibey huStled the foOlish fellow from his perch, nod he and his co-Anarchists •barlto take to their lietds:</p>
        <p>The .developin' ent tl:roubout the country of a system of technical eduction is revealing our un- preparedness for. it Scrverreffectually has the necessity of technical inqtruction taken hold of the public,pind that schools fir e being established far in excess of the sitpl-I.ly 4.competent teachers. The -subjects which are now being introduced are so varied and numero'us thqt makeshifts in the form of university men have to be employed in place of _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>,iractical technical- instructors. The demand has come with great suddenness, and the growth of the system is in danger of being much retarded, or its 'Cost-ninch increased, in cOnsequence of the want of filly-qualified teachers. '.-Artife i-echnical school is •,. , . • ,now ,doniestieated' among ,Us as,, a permanent and Aighly-valued institutionosome machinery for sup- plyingthis need will toPtionte -necessary, whether • I?y.the eatablishinent of',a)training'college or 'expariSion of s'on3e of "exiting educational es- tablishments, or by a remodelling and cheapening of the Science and Art Departinent. And NOatever L,conflict ti nifty rage at Westnitinster, the matter will not brook-delay."' • " The Chairman of the ChurCh Association lately sent tv memorial to the Bistop of London complain- big that th%.' -Saviour as in the ct dSting on the Cross, With attendant images ,afpersOnhad beervereaed, in the Church of St. Peter's, Staines (built hy•nSir Edward Clarke), and be appealed to the Bishop •Ari refuse to consecrate church "-unlegg and 'until the objectionable idols be removed." ~The Bishop/however, proceeded with the C.,OilieCration of ihe,church, and a letter has been received from his Lordship stating that he ' made inquiries on receipt, of, the complaint and .satis,fied .himself,that as Ole. .scuip.tors represented a historical event they stood on the same footing as the reredos in Exeter-Cathedral, sanctioned by the PriVy Council in the cliSeiertP!Oltiotts T. Boyd, and were, not "C onfrarY tb hiw. 'whOreupdn the Church Association declares that " with the laity will rest. the sin of following unfaithful leadors and of supply- ing them for such purposes with- rllocean funds."</p>
