      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>Since somebody's blacking was advertised on the Pyramids publicity was never more grotesquely ob- tained than in Trafalgar Square at present. After nightfall a powerful electric light streams from -the top of a building opposite the National Gallery, flashes across the front of that edifice, throws a</p>
        <p>-monemtary glory round the figure of Nelson perched high above the roar and bustle of the town, wanders over the United Service Club, darts across the square to St. Martin's Chutch and Morley's Hotel, blazes on the fountains, and finally collects a curious crowd. When this is done the cunning; gentleman in charge of the light, suddenly concen- trating it on the shaft of the Nelson Column, dis- plays to the astonished eyes around a well-focussed puff of somebody's soap. Above is the hero's statue; below, Landt-eer's lions, 'with the National Gallery for a background ; behind the spectator is White- hall, with the _Government Offices, the Houses of Parliament, and Westminster Abbey, and in the midst of all shines the advertisement of soap. It is "real smart."</p>
        <p>For some years strangers and even Londoners in many cases have been puzzled by an immense building which Tfill'es-74*erything on the Thames Embankment, particularly its neighbour the Savoy Hotel. This building is " Hobbs 's Folly," one of the assets in connection with the Liberator societies Always interesting, it promises to be more so, for the Official Receiver, finding it impossible to sell or let the place on-satisfactory terms,has entered upon the experiment, perhaps unprecedented in the annals of the Bankruptcy Court, ot converting it into an hotel. The Receiver is doing the work on a colossal scale. From first to last nearly a million will have been spent on the building. With 750 bedrooms, it will_be one of the largest hotels in the world. Big as the edifice is, it is being made bigger. The additicins are planned to enclose a great court- yard. Salisbury-street, Strand, has been demolished to make way for an entrance, and another will overlook the Embankment.</p>
        <p>The Trinity House is charged with working lighthouse men seventeen hours daily. ' When workmen in the dockyards and oranance factories, to say nothing of other people, have got ar eieht- hour day, the accusation, it true, is astounding. Lest, however,anyboay should blame Her Majesty's Ministers, let it be remembered that the Trinity House is not a Government department. For these prodigiously long hours the pay is small—only £4 lls.Bd. a month for the chief keeper. Of course no beacon burns seventeen hours out of the twenty- four,but the men are compelled to fill up their time at painting, japanning, repairing, and generally keeping in that marvellously spick and span condi- tioa familiar to seaside visitors both the light- houses_ and their cottages, besides trimming the lawns and cultivating the gardens attached to most lighthouSes. To Make matters worse, it is almost impossible for them to.get a holiday even for half a day. As this is the season when people go to the seaside; they can investigate the case for them- selves-. t• • ----- There is no truth in the assertion made that the police in 1-13de Parks on Sunday were instructed to prevent the champions of the House of Lords from holding a meeting. Mir order was simply the usual one to protect the demonstration from dis- turba,nce..- if any persons present were burning to show their love for the peers they could have done so at,their_ease by assembling at sufficient dis- tance from the platforms 'where the abolition of the House of Lords -wa4 , advocated: That such was the case is Proved by the holding ot the Social Democrapic gathering, not only without interference from 'the polibe, but also with their protection when the, crowd grew angry at Social Democratic discussion. The truth is that the crowd, almost without -,exception, was heartily in favour of abolishing the Lords, and the statement referred to is meant to throw dust irr the eyes of the public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>_ r r-Vgatv""'..."'</head>
        <p>_ r r-Vgatv""'..."'</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATUROAY, SEPTEMBER 1, 1894.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Just now the minds of the feminine portion of the population are on holiday thoughts intent, and from seaside, mtor and mountain reach me reports from friends of the enjoyment which they are deriving from their annual outings. By the way, as I write rain falls heavily and leads one to the philosophical reflection that some of us who are, perforce, postponing our tours, will prob- ably be better off for weather than those who are now away. Of course the question of costumes for the holidays is of intense importance, and I would like to tell you of one which struck me as being paticularly suitable for seaside wear. It was of zavy•blue canvas lined throughout with light selrlet silk, with the open coat displaying a full vest and large collar of cream spotted muslin edged with a frill.</p>
        <p>Large sleeves are happily disappearing from the tailor-made dream Slowly, but very surely, the tailors are coming round, and all the best amongst them are now limiting the fulness of theirs leeves to a vast extent, setting the sleeves in a few gathers at the top, or in three small boxpleats ; these just give a measure of width, but are quite innocent of the voluminous folds which erstwhile disgraced the gowns. I want to tell you of a very stylish hat, which, though particularly smart and up to date, owns nothing that will spoil with sun or rain. The shape itself is of fine black straw, while the trim- mings are shot black wings and wired lace bows. A delightful note of colour is obtained by a bunch of yellow orchids which cluster at the back and under the brim, and thia brim is finished with a piping of black velvet serail round the edge. The effect is really excellent.</p>
        <p>efr-4----4'7i ,v:i-,.'r:•::':_':',-- V 1 ......,E,'TY:,, ::-.....• 1 \-!•-.. --•-;\ ( \ ' I J-. 4. • . '')\ , \ / l A . , , , \ , II . :. li q , 4 - i , , . .. ._ . , _ .... , •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>One cannot contemplate the holidays calmly without a tweed cape—a garment which is wrap, waterproof, and railway-rug all in one, and which everyone of us may afford, hr Some material or ether. There is, in addition to tweed, the ever- useful black or brown serge, and, again, the home- spun of renown; whilevongh and fAced•oloths are just now having all their,own way. And to the very economically inclinedl may even Mention the checked and• crosti-barred flAnniilettes which 'to-day are so cleverly made and so_wonderfully designed in the likeness of the very best tweeds as to deceive; at first sight, even the expert in such matters.</p>
        <p>You will not require to be told what a useful addition a well-fitting blouse makes to a drossy woman's toilet, or what charming effects may be produced in their tabricatitn. A friend has.shown me a beAutiful example made of pink silk with tiny blatk-spots, while at parts 'appear pink rosebudt and green leaves ,rtised from the surface. •The blouse is full over the bust, and neatly draped with green chiffon. It is intended, -I should, say, to be worn with a coat and skirt oi w.ecn.cloga.</p>
        <p>It always amuses me when I hear people that they, are afraid to have their bedroom win- dows open at night, owing to the night air. Wcat other kind of air can we breathe at night but night air? The choice is between pure air Irani with,ut and foul air from within. It has been proved that a large number of the diseases we sutlr from are occasioned by people sleeping with their windows shut. An open window most., nights in the year can never hurt anyone.</p>
        <p>.B,e.lievyeomures, .)-t•tc</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
