      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>Mr. Gladstone has very pronounced views upon the subject of female beauty. In conversation with one of the ladies of the Court during a journey to Balmoral he expressed an emphatic opinion that beauty of feature was nothing in comparison with beauty of expression. It may safely be opined that the charms of Mr. Glad- stone's companion lay rather in mobility than regularity of feature.</p>
        <p>The Queen will shortly be possessed of one of the most remarkable articles ever made in prison. The superintendent of Agra Gaol some months back received an order to weave a carpet of special design for her Majesty. This is now complete. On it twenty-eight of the deftest convicts of the establishment have been engaged, and the texture measures seventy-seven feet by forty feet, and is estimated to contain no fewer than fifty nine million stitches. Most people have an idea that the Queen's consent must be obtained when British Royalties intend to get married, but her consent is only necessary from the respect and affection with which her relatives regard her in common with all her subjects. The real " stern par- ent" is the Duke of Cumberland, who is chief of the family. The Duke declined to sanction the marriage of the Duke and Duchess of Teck, but as might be expected nobody paid much attention to the Duke of Cumber- land when Queen Victoria had approved of the match. Miss Mary Ansell, the clever young actress who has entered into a life partnership with Mr. J. M. Barrie,has many talents in addition to her theatrical gifts. She is an accomplished horsewoman, and shows equal taste and skill in music and art alike. Mrs. Barrie has oneeccen- tricity which should be viewed with considerable satis- faction by her husband. She likes to make all her own dresses and bonnets. It may readily be believed that Mr. Barrie will not attempt to thwart her in this harm- less taste. One other peculiarity of this lady's is a strong dislike to smoking, and Mr. Barrie has heroically jilted his first love, " My Lady Nicotine," for this later and sweeter affection.</p>
        <p>Princess Mix of Hesse, although not strictly beautiful, has a most pleasing and expressive face, which is seen to most advantage when animated. She is a brilliant con- versationalist, and is said to be rather advanced in her ideas. Like many of the Queen's grand-daughters, she is an excellent needlewoman. Few people know how much interest Her Majesty takes in this department of a young princess's education. The Duchess of Saxe- Coburg, whose children have always been great favour- ites with the Queen, is herself almost an entire stranger to the needle, and Her Majesty has been all the more particular to watch over the progress which the young princesses made under the care of their governesses.</p>
        <p>A Plantation Proposal. Among the slaves on the American plantations in the old days there were regular forms of courtship, and al- most every large plantation had an experienced old slave who instructed young gallants in the way in which they should go in the delicate matter of winning the girls of their choice. " Uncle Gilbert," was the shoemaker on a plantation where there were a hundred slaves. He was very learned in the art of courtship ; and it was to his shop the slave lads went for instruction. The American slave's courtship words and forms are the result of his attempt at imitating the gushingly elegant manners and speech of his master. Uncle Gilbert's rule of courtship was that a " young man mus' tes' an' prove a gal befor' offerin' her his ban'. Ef er gal gives a man as good answer as he giver her question,den she's all riht in min'. Ef she can look him squar in de face when she talks to him, den she kin be trusted : an' ef her patches is on straight, and her close clean, den she je gwine ter keep de house straight, an' yer britches mended. Sich er ooman is wuth havin'."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>NEW FANATICISM.</head>
        <p>A NEW FANATICISM.</p>
        <p>THE DOUBLE LIFE:</p>
        <p>OR, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY. By the Author of " DARKWOOD.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXX. A FRIEND IN NEEL. It is my soul, that calls upon my name; How silver sweet sound lovers' tongues by night, Like softest music to attending ears! SHAKESPEARE'S Romeo and Juliet. Lord Grosvenor came nearer and nearer, in his fleet little pleasure boat, to the bluff cottage where- in Giralda was imprisoned. He did not speak. He looked up once or twice with a keen and sweep- ing glance, and if to assure himself that his move- ments were not watched by hostile eyes. Giralda listened, trying to catch the sound of his coming, under the loud murmer of the waves. At length she heard a low, grating sound, as of the boat touching against the rocks. "He is landing!" she murmured, pressing her face closer against the bars in the wild hope of seeing him. "Ah 1 Is not that the sound of his steps on the rocks? Or do I mistake the waves for his footsteps? It's he! It is he!" She listened still more intently, straining her bearing to its utmost capacity. Surely that was the gentle patter of footsteps, as some one climbed the rocks! It grew plainer and more distinct. The climber had reached the top of the bluff now, and must be resting. Again Giralda waved her arm through the bars to guide him to her window. There was a brief silence. Then something crashed softly against the cottage wall just under the window- It was the upper extremity of a lad- der, which the young Lord Grosvenor had found lying in the garden. Giralda's heart thrilled now with expectancy. There came a soft rush, as of some one mounting gently, and then the face of her morning's rescuer appeared before the barred window. "Giralda I" he whispered; softly. "Miss Are- valo !" Giralda reached out both her hands through the bars, laughing and sobbing in a breath. "It is I, Lord Grosvenor," she said, in a broken voice full of glad pulses. "Save me I Save me ! " The young lord took her hands in a warm, en- couraging clasp, stroking them softly. "You are safe from this moment, Miss Arevalo !" he said, with an unexpectant tenderness that brought the quick blushes to her cheeks, and a strange and happy thrill to her heart. "You are a prisoner here ?" " Yes, yes, my lord. The door is locked ; the windows are barred. These people are my enemies ! " "Your enemies? I should not dream that you had an enemy in the world !" was Lord Grosvenor's impassionate response. "I never saw him till to-night. You know Lord Acllowe, Lord Trevelyan's nephew ?" "I know of him, but I have never seen him." "He—.be asked me to-day to become his wife," said Giralda, after a moment's hesitation. "I refused him; and to-night he had me brought here, to be kept a close prisoner until I shall change my mind." "Why, this_is barbarous—infamous- ! exclaimed Lord Grosvenor, with indignant emphasis. "It is a proceeding worthy of feudal times." "And every minute I remain here is an injury to those I love !" moaned Giralda, in sudden and vivid remembrance of her father's peril. "Lord Adlowe has gone to work trouble upon my parents! Oh, my lord, can you get me out of this prison?" "Yes, and instantly ! " responded our hero, ardently. "I could cut these bars with my knife. No; the wood is too hard. Bittly is a hard and desperate kind of man, but I had better face him and demand your release. A straightforward directness is generally best. All he is astir. I have awakened him. Have no fears, Giralda. I , will protect you. ' He listened a moment, hearing movements in the roolm (,t t-e,;t,ttage. At the same moment ,(v..l4,eted a tiung boat, manned by three stout ws, creep.ng around the bluff, well into the fore.</p>
        <p>1 "I don't doubt it," responded the young noble- man, in tender sympathy. "That Adlowe must be made to suffer for this high-handed outrage, if there is any law in the kingdom ! " "Oh, no. I cannot make any formal complaint against him, my lord," said Giralda, sorrowfully and wearily. "I must bear it in silence ! " "Call me Paul," said his lordship, with an in- genuous blush. "I am alone in the world, and have no one to call me by that name. "My lord! sounds so formal from your lips !" "You must call me Giralda, then," was the low response." "Agreed. And now tell me, Giralda, why must you bear this wrong, this insult, this outrage, in silence ?" The maiden looked at her preserver, her soul in her eyes. He looked so manly, so honourable, so noble, that she was tempted to tell him her secret —her father's secret. She felt as if she must con- fide in some one, and he, who had twice saved her life, seemed no stranger, but a true and warm friend. "My lord," she said, "that is Paul," and she blushed vividly, as he could see in the soft moon- light that fell full on her lovely young face—" I have a great secret—one that concerns, those who are dearer to me than life itself. I feel as if this secret would be safe with you. I feel as if I must have some one to talk to to-night or my heart will break !" "Talk to me as freely and unreservedly as you would talk to a brother," said the young lord, with unobtrusive sympathy. "If you want my counsel I will give it. At any rate, your secret will be safe with me." "I know it. My lord—Paul—l told you this morning that I was the ward of Lord Trevelyan— his adopted niece! I am more—his own great- niece? lam a Trevelyan !" Lord Grosvenor looked surprised and mysti- fied. "I thought his lordship had but two nephews," he said— Geoffrey Treveylan and Lord Ad- lowe." "He had but the two. lam the daughter of Geoffrey Trevelyan !" The young lord's look of surprise deepened. "Did he not die in Brazil eighteen years ago!" he asked. "No, he is living to-day. The secret is known to but few. Lord Trbvelyan does not know that I am really his relative. He does not dream that his hated nephew lives. But Lord Adlowe knows all. He threatens to strike me through my dear parents if I refuse to marry him ?" "The wretch 1' said the young lord, vOth energy. ' "Paul," said Giralda, with increasing earnest- ness, "you have heard of Geoffrey Trevelyan----my dear, wronged, innocent father! You have heard the ;tory which stained his spotless name, covering it with infamy? Let me tell you the truth !" In an impassioned tone, with her truth and love beaming in every feature, she told him the version of the sad story as she had received it from the lips of the Lady Beatrice. She painted the vile char- acter of Lord Ad lowe ; she told of her father's years of hope deferred, of her mother's trusting, clinging love, and of her own resolve to clear her father's name of every stain. Her voice broke down in sobs before she concluded, and it was no detriment to the unsullied, noble manhood of the young lord that his tears fell with hers. Her manner and her words had carried convic- tion to his soul. "It was Lord Adlowe who contrived that scene ! " he exclaimed. "How it was done I cannot tell! but that assault and robbery were the result of a foul conspiracy against the honour of Geoffrey Trevelyan—perhaps against his life! Had the marquis's wound been serious, and had he died Geoffrey Trevelyan's life would have been forfeited, and Adlowe stepped into possession of the estates. How strange thet Lord Trevelyan never suspected the truth !' "Oh, Paul, he trusts Lord Adlowe, although he affects a contempt for him. He is not fond of him. He does not love him, but it would be impossible to convince him of the truth as I have convinced you. I have thought of telling him all the truth, but he would only cast me off, and hasten to have papa arrested. He will never be convinced of papa's innocence save by the most complete and 'Positive evidence. There was a man who could</p>
      </div>
