        <p>He leaned forward on the ladder, catching at one have thrown light on the matter—who could have of the bars of tue window, waving his hat, and cleared papa's name. He was Lord Adlowe's valet, shouting loudly : Ho, there, boys ! This way ! Come up here, and his name was Negwyn. I have heard that he " went to Australia; but he may have died there." quicaly ! I have need of you !" "You think it would not do to tell Lord The fishermen looked up, and shouted back a Trevelyan the whole truth as you have told it to loud balirmative, pulling in for the shore. The use?" suggested our hero. young Lord Grosvenor was a great favourite "I know it would not. Listen while I tell you with these rough e:iildren of the sea, his daring how I happened to come to the Park." and courage, Ins geuerosity to them and the widows She told of her hidden home, her conviction that and children of their drowned comrades causing she ought to support herself, her journey to the them to regard him not only as their benefactor, Park in search of an situation, and her utter igno- ho. . ryr.r,a, 4- Iv ,i liol i ,50 fiwin,PlvoQ • rance that she was going to her rightful home and Seeing them coming, Lord Grosvenor rapidly to the presence of her kinsman. She told how she descended the ladder to the ground. had won his lordship's love from her resemblance And at the same moment the cottage door opened to Geoffrey Trevelyan, some of the particulars of and Bittly, a red, braWny, and savage-locking man, her journey to London, and finally of the will the of Herculean proportions, appeared, half dressed, marquis had made in her favour. upon his threshold. "Who is this _making such a row, and waking "I can never tell him now, you see," she said, wpple at this time o' night 1" he growled, in a deep, sorrowfully. "Lord Adlowe insinuated that I had oarse bass voice, glaring at our hero. been sent by my parents to the Park to win Lord! h, "It is I—Lord Grosvenor was the quick Trevelyan's savings. The marq