alda roused herself from her reverie as the .me the -second time! How can I ever thank boat entered the shadow of the tall bluff on which . you?" - , was perched the Eyrie. She noticed now a little Lord Grosvenor took her fluttering hand and rocky landing and stairs cut in the rocks, leading i lifted it reverently to his lips. by a winding route to the top of a steep cliff. Her "I will tell you sometime, how you can thank companion ran the boat into a little cove at the me acceptably," he whispered. foot of these rude stairs, and secured it to an iron Giralda drew away her hand in a sweet confusion. ring which was there for the purpose. .-Somehow his words and manner strangely pleased Then he gave her his hand with tender gallantry I her. and assisted her up the stairs. • . "Put on your hat and we will go," said his lord- "I fear you are ill," he said, anxiously, as they ship. "I will take you in my boat to the Eyrie, slowly mounted hand in hand. "Your hand is like •, and from there you can go by carriage wherever ice. Your face is flushed with fever, and you • you wish." tremble like a leaf. All this excitement has been _Giralda attired herself hastily, giving her shawl too much for you !" andlkavelling bag to his lordship, who conducted "I am only very tired, I think," said her downstairs. , Giralda, in a faltering voice, as a strange sense- The fishermen greeted her with a cheer, having tion of weakness came over her. "I should like made a shrewd guess at the facts. Her late gaolers to rest." looked at her with glances of hatred, and the young She paused a moment, leaning against the rock. girl hurried out, not breathing freely until she had The young lord hesitated an instant, and then drew gained the open air, her lovely drooping head to his breast, gently They paused a minute or more on the bluff, compelling her to lean against him, his soul thrill- while Lord Grosvenor briefly explained to the men ing with delight at her gentle dependence upon the cause of hi