in Parliament, was seized with a c 0 plaint which gave more concern to his friends than to himself. "How do you treat your mation, Mr. Van Homrigh ? ". said a sca,a.7, member for one of the midland counties., " answered Peter, "1 trate it with sovereign en-- timpt.• "THERE is good deal of religion in nature,,, solemnly remarked a young clergyman while olit, ing upon a lady of his congregation the otle evening. "There is, was the quiet reply.. should never forget that there is a sermon in blade of grass.' "Quite true. We shouli. remember that grass is cut very short at tiu• season of the year." PEEL is said to have once inquired of a,".ar. • admirer what he thought was -Ulu chief qualiile- cation of a Prime Minister. " I think," Was trbo ready answer, "that he should have i!•'e,CCO :/c3,-;. • "No ! no ! " expostulated the wealthy Prev"e.'.; "I don't mean that." " I think,'' was equally ready rejoiner," he should ha live ice tt-1</p>
