        <p>tion, went on to say that his client's premises adjoined those occupied by M the late r. Speakman. • S'ome time ago Mr. Hughes had purchased the good will of the premises he now occupied and at present carried ors busines: as a confectioner Of 'wine and spirit merchant. His (Mr. Allen's) client held that there'was no lieces.ity for a win e license such as that required by the applicant,. The needs oh the neighbourhood for wine were amply provided for. However, from what had been said that morning he (Mns Allen) concluded that these objec- tions on the part of his client would not hold good as it appeared to be. only medicated wine which the applicants desired to sell. - Mr.-Burton remarked that his clients did not wish to interfere with the trade of the community, but he thought they would not care to be restricted to selling medicated wine. • Mr.. Allen said he could not object on the grounds cited with reference to character. He could only contend that such a license was•unnecessary as there was ample provision in the district to meet the requirements of the inhabitants. Mr.-Btirton replied that to successfully oppose the application Mr. Allen muist prove that the applicants did not come withino he meaning of the 3rd section of the " Refreshment Houses Act, 1860," as given above. The Chairman drew the attention cf the Clerk to the fact that the license was applied for by two persons and ; asked if two persons could jointly hold a license. The Clerk answered in the negative : The license ; could only be granted to one-responsible person. Mr. Burton did not think that it wan absolutely necessary to grant a license to one person only. How- ever, if the magistrates would not grant it to both he asked them to grant it. to Edmund Marston. The Chairman thought that the notice was forfeited as they could not grant a license to two persons as con- tained in the application. . Mr. Burton thought it was merely a technical barrier and would tnake no difference if the alteration he sug- gested were made. .• The Chairman considered that the best course for Mr. Burton to adopt was t consent to the matter being adjourned until September 24th, when the adjourned sessions would take place, and in the meantime fresh notices could be issued. This was agreed to.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. ktrat,lr•—Before Messrs. John Brundrit (in the chair), T "11,1 P Speakman, C.C. James Handley, Samuel aJ t , or , and Thomas Handley. LARGE DECREASE OF DRUNKENNESS. SUPERINTENDENT'S ANNUAL REPORT. 81 . tal h Perintendent Atherton read his annual report which • as follows :—" I have the honour to submit my 9al report with reference to the conduct of licensed ta.l4lallers and beer retailers in this division during the kir year. There are 44 licensed victuallers, 53 beer ers, and four others who sell off the premises, making 4 total 1 to . 0. 101, giving an average of 251 of the population eeell'a:_ili license. No licensed victuallers have been pro- proe" against during the year bring a decrease of one y4e_eeded agabast and none convicted in the preceding 11011 Two beer sellers have been proceeded against and -„, 4lt F convicted, being an equal number proceeded altist last year. One hundred and seventy-four Iserna have iieert summoned for drunk-nneas of whoa" ,'svere convicted as compared with 371 persons pro- d against and 355 lonvicted in the preceding year; , .. • acrease ‘4el 202 of 197 persons summoned for drunkenness, terria , convicted." Continuing, the Superintendent zie rod that he attributed the decrease in 'drunken- Cam in a great measure to the completion of the Snip th:al• In addition to the two beersellers mentioned in 1 report as having been summoned, another had been Lltorn °nod that month for permitting drunkenness but dismissed. This case had not been included in the ;Volt as it occurred after the end of the licensing year, 31st. Chairman said it was very pleasing to listen to a good report as that just read by the Superinten- : t4t• It was exceedingly gratifying that such a large ". taiktease had taken place in drunkenness, but he would .oe Sr have heard that the town took the credit of the pietrhease than it should be attributed to the completion las "e Shit) Canal. Although there was undoubtedly a .ft , at,e Z 1 amount of truth in Superintendent Atherton's , va:nlPrit yet he would rather hope that the decrease bia -: °wing not solely to the completion of the canal but kt', the inhabitants were learning to be more temperate drnrieir habits. He did not think that the decrease in thell,kentess was owing to any neglect on the part of 4184liee of their duty. The licensed victuallers might ; beet:„a" some credit to themselves as none of them had to ti Proceeded against. The facts in the report would, tesi°.4bt, have the effect of silencing the remarks of a orli:In Member of the Standing Joint Committee who , 1114 tDo often characterized the town as "Drunken tieticall.” Such remarks should not be made by gentle- towp, "a were not acquainted with the real state of the He was pleased to state, on behalf of his brother tt)_kilisttrates that tl rough the improved condition of the w hei ' 1 The ~ r magisterial duties were much ligtitenud. tileinirsQuperintend h ent remarked that e should have at an earlier stage of the proceedings, that 'ait:Pucation would be made for a new license to sell ktio. sne might also state that the back premises of Polk of the houses in the town had been regarded by the ktowe 4,s objectionable. Their views had been made alter..?.nowever, to the right persons, and the requisite i'lons had been made. There was now no objec- °any of the licenses being renewed. LICENSES TRANSFERRED. Itkii old licenses were then all renewed and the follow- Were transferred :—Castle Hotel, Halton from AlilMPson to John Simpson ;Royal Oak,Weston, from rt,,TGreen to Peter Riley; Navigation Inn, Canal- shritimrieorn, from James Linton, to William Faulkner ; 1403, tnPr's Rotel, Percival's-lane. Runcorn, from Samuel 'rola 'vi, Joseph Hall, Wheat Sheaf Inn, Bridge-street, illiarn Faulkner to S imuel Houghton ; Swan Inn, AibiZ-street / , from John Evans to George Joynson ; b.t3,er 4. r.,410, Nelson-street, from Henry Hill to Thomas ?cab Jam and Devonshire Arms, Church-street, kto, Lames Thomas Taylor, to William Young; Sloop ' Itid,w°eh-street, from the late William Jones, to his -' Sarah Ann Jones. . T APPLICATIO FOR A WINE LICENSE. ' ' "• N Burton, solicitor, Runcorn, applie lon behalf • 012,1:13: Edmund Marston and Wm. Edgar Lee, execu- -sll ~.• 'us late Mr. Isaac Speakman, for a new license to litli.te-01,,nne ofl the premises at 27 and 29, Briclffe;,,s, treet, ,„41r a• „, -PPel; vv• Allen, solicitor, of Runcorn and Widnes, behalf of Mr. William Hughes, of Bridge- vo 0 . ~ kr „PPose the application. 'kap,' siurton said that his clients applied for the 411,De tovnable them to sell medicated wine, and the :Thieh`atio,o Was made under 23 Vic. ch. 27, sec. 3. t.11,8a sa'di that " Every person who skill keep a shop . eof any goods or commodities other than h'▪ I'aler • wine, or who shall have taken out a license as a , `4l 41 iv' Act\ me (except persons expressly disqualified by ,Ir-etia, / shall without producing or having any other blr)lder t'il'r. authority be entitled to take out a license ihttleti.ora Act to sell by retail in reputed quart or pint' tkeeN,el._.:Uch shop foreign wines not to be consumed otedn where sold." The applicants, if gran ' ;4thokrnse, would be the only persons in the town vlta 1, ;Jed to sell this wine. . They had given the requi- kict _Nees under the Wine and Beerhouses Act of 1869, Ia terwere entitled to a license. The grounds upon which ''`ltittlafa!,‘',otild have to be based were that the appli- Z)4l.4ctLed tO produce satisfactory evidence of good that the shop or adjoining premises were of a lit,usrlV Character; that the applicant having held itOplit'lBo Previously had been disqualified ;or that the itLtlire,i4ll,t or the house were not duly qualified as re- -"itiat '3" law• Be presumed that there was nothing th. kr..J the characr te of the applicants. „ . G , l• • 1-• Simpson, manager fear the executors, said IFense was required simply to enable them to estse"i:pcated wines. The applicants had not held a u, brip. ~, ore, .`r• W01'e,