      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NEWTON.</head>
        <p>NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The Newton Licensing Sessidits were held at the Newton Town Hall, on aturday. There was not much interest taken in the proceedings as there were no fresh applications or any objections to existing licenses. The magistrates present were Captain Witbington (chairman), and Messrs. T. J. Gillespie, B. Stone,, W. Mitchell, and T. D. Grimke- Drayton. Superintendent Higginbotham and Brivaington presented their reports for the districts under their charge. The following is</p>
        <p>SUPERINTENDENT HIGGINBOTHAM'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Gentlemen, I have the hemour to lay before ynu my report and returns relative to the business in connection with the general annual licensing session. The number of licensed persons is the same as last year :—Licensed victuallers 45, beer sellers (on premises) 16, beer sellers, (off premises) 5, grocers and wine licenses 2. There are no applications for new licenses. There is a decrease of 75 proceedings for drunkenness compared with last year, and the number is the lowest for ten years. This decrease I consider, is principally due to the colliers' strike last year. Generally the licensed persons have conducted their houses satisfactorily, and there has been few com- plaint'," He also reported that the number of persons apprehended and summoned during the past year was 176 males, and 19 females, total 195. The corresponding figures last year were 240, 20, 26, showing a decrease for the year of 15. •</p>
        <p>SUPERINTENDENT BRASSINGTON REPORTED, " Gentlemen, I have the honour to lay before you the usual returns relative to the business in connection with the general annual licensing sessions, and to state that there are within the townships of Go'borne and Haydock, which form the Wigan portion of Warrington Petty Sessional Division, 32 licensed persons, viz : 13 inn- keepers, 1 spirit grocer, and 18 beer sellers (13 on and 5 off the premises), being the same as compared with last year. 71 pereons have been proceeded against for drunkenness, being a decrease of 105 as compared with last year. The houses generally have been well con- ducted, and I have no objection to the renewal of any of the licenses." He also reported that during the year 38 persons were apprehended, 183 summoned, total 221. The corresponding figures last year were 61, 267, 328. Mr. Greenall (clerk) then asked whether there were any objections or otherwise. Mr. Stuart said that he would like to mention the matter of the " Charles Napier Inn," in Golborne. The premises were about to be enlarged according to plans he was going to put in. The present house is an old house and has not. sufficient accommodation for its business. He, therefore, hoped the bench would pass the plans and grant the license again to the house.—Superintendent Brassington said that he had only received information of the proposed enlargement that morning. He, therefore, considered that the license should be adjourned for a proper exam- ination to be made.—Mr. Stuart said that he had no objection.—The case was accordingly adjourned until September 29th, and Captain Withington in announc- ing the decision f the bench to grant the remaining licenses, congratulated the licensed victuallers upon the manner their houses had been kept, their not being a single conviction upon any publican during the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ST. HELENS. DECREASE IN DRUNKENNESS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS. DECREASE IN DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, the general annual licensing sessions for the borough of St. Helens was held. The Mayor (Alderman Sinclair) presided,and there were also present on the bench Colnnel Gamble,. C. 8., Dr. Gaskell, Alder- man Cook, Messrs. W. Gamble, H. Martin, C. Sharpies, B. A. Dromgoole, and T. Glover. Chief-constable Wood submitted his report to the bench, showing that during the year ended 31st July, 1894, 795 persons had been apprehended and summoned for drunkenness, against 1052 last year, 1268 in 1892, and 1354 in 1891. One publican had been proceeded against, as compared with 5 last year, 12 in 1892, and 8 in 1891. There was no conviction. Two beersellers had been summoned and one convicted. There were now in the borough 149 public houses,l37 beerhouses,and seven grocers' licences, each number showing a reduction of two in the last four years. The population in 1893 was 76,800, being 515 to every public house and 560 to each beerhouse. There had been only one conviction during the year, a beerseller, W. H. Glover (present tenant William Kay), Horse Shoe, Higher Parr-street, fined 20s. and costs for supplying beer to a drunken man. There were no new applications. The Mayor said that was the day for the renewal of the licenses which expired in October. Following the usual custom they would adjourn the case which ap- peared in the black list, where a beerhouse keeper was fined for selling drink to a drunken man, until the ad- journed sessions on the 11th September. The other licenses they proposed to renew.—Chief-constable Wood said a beerhouse called the Crooked Billet,in Higher Parr- street, belonging to Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co., had heed sold on the condition that the license should not be renewed.—The Mayor : There is no application for it.—Chief-constable Wood : No, sir. The license was not renewed. The Mayor referred to the diminution in cases of drunkenness as shown by the Chief-constable's report. They knew, of course„ that probably a great many per- sons deserved to be summoned who had escaped, but still there was no doubt tere had been a considerable diminution of drunkenness in the borough during the last few years. On the application of Mr. J. 0. Swift, the magistrates renewed the provisional orders for the two new buildings far the Havanna!' Inn, Parr, and the Stanley Inn, Peas- ley Cross, as the new houses were not yet completed. Mr. Swift also referred to the house mentioned at the last transfer sessions, formerlyoccupied by James Pilkington, but now in the occupation of James Cunnington, and f which a temporary authority had been granted to sell until that day. If the removal wasgranted it would be in the name of Cunningham; but the bench said they wished to be satisfied that Cunningham conducted the house well. Another temporary authority was granted until the adjourned sessions. In the case of the New Inn, where the landlord,George Barrow, had died, a temporary authority was granted to the widow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>VOLUNTEER INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>2ND EARL OF CHESTER'S VOL. BAT. CHESHIRE</p>
        <p>REGIMENT. F and G Companies, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Detatchment orders fcr the week ending Saturday, September 15th, 1894 :- 1. —The Annual Prize Meeting will take place during the month of September as follows :—Company Drill Competition on Saturday, September Ist, at 1 p.m. Recruits' Competition on Saturday, September Ist, at 4 p.m. Detatchment Competition on Saturday, Sep- tember Bth, at 1 p.m. Extra Series Competition on Saturday, September 15th. at 2 p.m. Entries can be made on the day of competition. 2.—Members having bayonets, jags, sight protectors, or rifle slings in their possession are requested to return them to the Armoury. Detail : Orderly Officer for the week, Captain G. F. Ashton. Orderly Sergeants for the week, F Company. Seogeant J. W. Woodland; G Company, Sergeant W. H. Harvey. Orderly Bugler for the week, Bugler J. Mort. (Signed) GEO. F. ASHTON, Captain. Commanding Detatchment.</p>
