      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>E li XA INI i NEB , SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 1 1894.</head>
        <p>E li XA INI i NEB , SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 1 1894.</p>
        <p>WITH DI. ON TIIE D!,]E.</p>
        <p>A TALE OF A TUB.</p>
        <p>BY MR. JOHN PEERS. (Concluded from last week.)</p>
        <p>The fading light warned us, as belated dogs, to seek our kennel, and now I found that my berth, though strictly of the narrowest, had the ad Vantage of being dry—a merit which could not be conceded to some parts of the boat, when, as now, the rain came, down in torrents and the wind...blew merrily. This state of things continued all Wednesday morn- ing, but about noon the rain ceased, the clouds cleared off, and the wind lessened to a nice breeze from about south. In the afternoon we saw a flat working up the Swash and speculated as to the probability of our catching her. We had all ready for a start when the tide floated us, and, when this welcome release came we ran down the pool an round the north end of the island, through the race, and into the op-3n waters of the Dee. Here we again sighted our chase, the flat, some miles, Or so ahead and evidently bound for some place on the upper Dee. So we gave our craft her head and she laid down her ears and set herself to the work of winllin2 as though she meant it. Away through the sUidit water,, against the oppw•ing breeze she thrashed, now upon Lime-wharf and now over toward the 11, elsh side, where Mostyn Jaunts her vaporous flog by day—a beaton red by night. Each stretch was an over-lap until finally, off Heswell, we were abreast and spoke him. He was bound for Flint but had not much hope of getting there, for now the wind and the tide were slackening. We had found it necessary to shake our reef out and hod passed Flint, and got a little way inside of the training wall, or "grind,' when the tide was done and the wind, what little of it there was left, was right ahead, and useless as a help towards Chester. So the emblem of Hope sought the Dee sands for the first time that trip and we made preparation to enjoy ourselves, and the scenery. Both these objects were very pleasantly accomplished.. Di., who had now recovered his spirits, was quite enthusiastic about the beauty of the place and the pleasantness of his surroundings. So, man's appreciation of the beauties of nature largely depends upon the, condition of his digestive apparatus. When about half-tide had gone, a black thunder cloud arose above Chester and gradually came down over us and we were treated to a grand downpour of rain accompanied by heavy thunder and vivid lightening. But all this passed over and things became bright and pleasant again. Then the salmon-fishers came down in their boats and hung around us to examine the boat and exchange ideas. They detailed to us the hardships of their lives—the £l5 license for fishing, the little rest, and hard work they had, while we enlarged upon the qualities of our ship.and the incidents of the voyage. Again the tide is gone, again we are aground, again we are snugly enkennelled and asleep. On the next morning's ebb the salmon-fishers again came down— to draw this time. We saw one large fish caught and despatched by blows upon the head.</p>
        <p>"Man's inhumanity to man, Makes countless thousands mourn."</p>
        <p>Very true; but man has a tongue and can "yell out " when hurt, and often does this in anticipation of that event. But man's inhumanity to his dumb fellow beings is a disgrace to him and his so-called "Christian country." The treatment of this poor salmon is mercy itself compared with the horrors of our foreign cattle trade. The continuance among us of these atro- cities is a proof that the professions of civilization and refinement may be, and often is accompanied by the most barbaric practices. Nevertheless, re- verting to the subject of my text, this quick des- patch of the salmon is far more merciful than leaving it to gasp out its life in the agonies of suffocation; if our imported cattle had but the same mercy meted to them, we English would have lessened by one, the just causes of national reprobation. A beef-steak is very pleasant taking but one does not like the thought of the cruelties by which it has been obtained. Remorse is not a good seasoning for such meat. The fishing boats gradually left us—went below in pursuit of their business—and we were left in solitary possession of that sand-bank; but not for long. A little before low water men with " trudge nets," which they pushed before them while wading, often waist deep in the water, came to enliven the scene. The scene of their labours was a long" flash " left by the receding tide in the bank upon which we were grounded. I he shrimps they told us were not very plentiful, and, to lessen even this small quantty,was the intent of a great collection of sea-gulls, who were wheeling and screaming over one place,render- ing the air vocal in their disputes as to the division of the spoil. And now, down the river, Flint-ward, the odd score or so of salmon boats were making their way up again with the young flood tide, and against the fresh breeze now blowing right down our course to Connah's Quay. Of course we deter- mined to be in the swim, if the good tide would lift us in time. So we prepared by getting the anchor off the bare sand and stowing it,and setting the sails for the start when that " flood " which the "Divine Williams " says there is "in the affairs of men," 'flame and enacted its beneficent part to "lead to fortune"—in our case to Chester. The last boat had passed on some distance ahead ere "Tom," after some impatient yawings, was freed from the indignity of his arenaceous bondage, but, when this did happen, "Old Tom "sped upwards like the spirit of his name- sake. It was a lively gcene with the strong tide and the manoeuvering boats; and " Bowling " did not dis- grace himself in the contest. At Connah's Quay one of our salmon fishing acquaintances told us we should have a railway bridge to pass, a turn-bridge, requir- ing as a signal, a red flag at the mast head. Now we had no mast head to speak of, no signal halliards, and no red flag! But, however, Di. had, in one of his redundantly replenished pockets, a red pocket- hankerchtef. This we lashed to a long shaft— which should have been a boat-hook, but Di. had yet not found the grains in his uncatalogued collec- tion of pocket able paraphernalia. This impromptu substitute, Di. waved with desperate energy on alternate sides of the boat as we tacked towards the bridge, and we hailed that apparently immov- able obstruction. But our "Open Sesame" had either not been seen or, if seen, not heeded. So we wore, and stood back some distance, still flying our flag of danger, which at last had its due operative effect, and the massive iron-work slowly turned upon its axis and the tide carried us through flowingly. I</p>
        <p>rather imagine the bridge-keeper regarded out insistence with the ext..inpere flag to partake of the nature of ti diininuT ike impertinence ; to ask such a large bridge to open for so small a craft to pass. Tarturiunt nzontes, ex ridiculus mus, is what one might imagine him saying, but he did convey a similar sentiment in the words, " You could have gone under, there's plenty of room." At this implied insult Di. broke forth vehemently. " What the blessed Plague''—this is Di's mode of condemnatory denunciation—it saves his conscience and a possible heavy bill for brimstone—" what do these people wean by obstructing the free navigation of this river and objecting, when folks comply with rules of their own making? " Why do they not give, on a gauge, the height from the bottom of their bridge to the water,and then we should us' our own judge- ment. as to the advisability of going under, or otherwise ; and the responsibility would then rest with the boat." "Bother em We now had open to us a long straight reach of some seven miles. With the fast dying flood tide and the very light wind it was a long time ere we reached Saltney, but all the time we were enjoying the beauty of the evening and the novelty of our surroundings. At Saltney began to appear the übiquitous Angle- maniacs who, from here to Eaton, almost line the banks in what my own experience tells rue is— the vain persuasion for the much pestered fish. We reached the outskirts of Chester about 7 p.m. on Thursday and after replenishing the exhausted bread locker, we retired and put the lid on. On Friday morning early we poled her round the Roodee to Chester Bridge, und6r which we found the mast would not go by some six inches. She was then dropped down to Mr. Roberts's boat yard where I left Di., somewhat regretfully,as the solitary tenant of his tub. Having signed articles for the round trip to Chester, my contract was fulfilled, and he had now to wait until the tides were high enough to carry him and his habitation over the weir. This accomplished, his intention was to explore the upper reaches of the Dee, and fish, using the ship as a house-boat.</p>
        <p>Row this was done may subsequently be detailed if the fates are propitious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LANCASHIRE MINERS ON THE AGGRESSIVE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE MINERS ON THE AGGRESSIVE.</p>
        <p>MINIMUM WAGE FOR PONY BOYS AND DA YWAGEMEN.</p>
        <p>DISPUTES AT LANCASHIRE COLLIERIES.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon, Mr. S. Woods, M.P., Mr Greenall, and a deputation of miners waited upon Mr. Campbell, general manager of the Sutton Heath and Lea Green Collieries, St. Helens, relative to certain dis- putes which had arisen. One dispute was as to the fix- ing of the field prices in the New Rushy Park Mine. The men wanted 4d. per ton more than the employers had offered. Considerable discussion took place upon the question, but no agreement was arrived at, and it was ultimately arranged to defer the matter for a week or a fortnight, during which time both parties would recon- sider the position, and see if any compromise could be effected. Another point in dispute was thd removal of thick dirt at the Sutton Heath Colliery. The men had been paid on a rough and readysystem—that is, so much per ton, whether the dirt was thick or thin. This was said to be unsatisfactory, and a graduated scale was laid before the manager, who met the men most fairly, and a settlement was effected on the basis of payment accord- ing to thickness. A third point in dispute was that in the Main Delph Mine the places had been worked 10 feet wide for which the men got a certain yardage and tonnage,but recently the manager had ordered the men to work 15 feet wide, and had only offered them is. per yard more, and nothing extra per ton. The men considered the amount inadequate for the change. Later a leng inter- view, in which the deputation tried to show that the amount had not been sufficiently increased, Mr. Camp- bell said he could not see his way to pay any more, and it was now under consideration whether the places in question should be closed, or whether the men would continue to work at the rates they had been receiving. Mr. Woods, M.P., Mr. Greenall, and Mr. Isherwood, with a deputation, on the same day waited upon the Tyldesley Coal and Salt Company, with whom a peculiar difficulty had arisen. It appeared that for some time the mines manager and underlooker (father and son) had not been regarded in a friendly spirit by the men, and they stopped in a body, with a view to getting the underlooker removed. This was agreed to, and then the mea demanded the discharge of the mines muter. The deputation had a long interview, and the employer said that, rather than discharge the mines manager, he would, on principle, sacrifice the whole colliery. The deputation adjourned to the Miners' Hall, and informed the miners of what had taken place.—Mr. Woods advised the men to accept the arrangement, and said there was a guarantee that the men would be properly treated.— The men were then balloted, but by a majority of eigh- teen they refused to accept the arrangement. Mr. Woods expressed an opinion on Wednesday that the men would altimately reverse that decision. A grievance on the lines of many other collieries throughout the country exists at the Garswood Hall Colliery. On Wednesday night, Mr. Woods and a deputation waited upon the management, and discussed the complaints of the boys and daywagemen, who say they received no advance when the miners got the last two 5 per cents., and assert that no reduction sheuld be made, whereas in some cases quite 10 per cent, and in other cases nearly 10 per cent. reduction had actually been made. If the complaints are substantiated, the matter will probably be brought before the Conciliation Board. Mr. Woods is sorry to say the grievances of boys and daywagemen are general, and many of the Lancashire employers were not, in his opinion, behaving fairly. A proposition was under discussion on Saturday at the meeting of the Lancashire Miners' Federation as to the establishment of a standard wage for boys and day- wagemen, below which there could be no reduction— that anybody's wages below 3s. per day should have no reduction vthatever. The Lancashire members of the Conciliation Board would no doubt press this matter upon the Conciliation Board, as it was unanimously carried by them on Saturday. In many places in his (Mr. Woods's) district daywagemen did not get 20 per cent, of the 40 per cent, advance, and yet 10 per cent. was being taken from tbem. As regards the Scotch levies, while at Thatto Heath Mr. Woods brought before the miners the necessity of continuing the payment of the Scotch levies, and it was unanimously resolved that the whole of the levies should be paid at once. Mr. Woods, in answer to a question,said if the Scotch miners were going to carry on the strike and ignore the advice of the Eaglish Federation, as they had done, it would lead to the English miners withdraw- ing their support. His own feeling was very strong on that point, and he thought most of his colleagues would feel the same. If the Scotchmen decided against the policy laid down by the English delegates to Glasgow, they would feel at liberty to withdraw the levies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>I PARR'S BANK AND ALLIANCE CO. LIMITED. The " Bullionist " has a very interesting article upon this well-known Banking Company and we take the following extract from it : In the days of long ago it was the custom for many merchants to carry on, to a certain small extent, the business of bankers, and many of the most renowned banks of the present time can trace their origin to this source. It was thus that Parr's Bank was founded in ‘Varrington, but the date is uncertain. Enjoying public confidence to a remarkable extent, the connections of Parr and Co. were extended to districts round, and the name of the bank became quite a household word. In 1865 a limited company was formed, with a capital of one million pounds, divided into 10,000 shates of £lOO each (of which £lO per share, or £lOO,OOO, was paid up), to take over the business of Parr and Company. The same year an arrangement was made by the direc- tors of Parr's Banking Company to purchase the old- established business of Thomas Firth and Son, at Northwich, aed two years later the Metropolitan and Provincial Bank, of Macclesfield, was acquired. A call of £5 per share was made in 1871,thus bringing the paid- up capital to £150,000. In 1874 a further call of £5 per share was made, and at the same time 4,500 new shares were issued, the bank having taken over the business of Woodcock, Sons, and Eckersley. In 1875 the paid-up capital was represented by £391,500, and, by sundry issues of new shares, it was raised from time to time until, in 1883, it stood at £560,000, at which figure it remained till 1890, when the issue of 2,000 new shares brought it up to £600,000. In 1891 it was again ' increased to £650,000, and the following year, on the amalgamation with the Alliance Bank, it was raised to its present amount, £1,000,000. These issues of new shares were frequently made for the purchase of other banks. In 1887 Parr's took over the private bank of F. W. Jennings, of Leek ; in 1878 Dixons and Co., of Ches- ter; and in 1883 an amalgamation was effected with the National Bank of Liverpoel. Parr's then rested for a few years, evidently on the look-out for a chance of getting a foothold in the Metropolis. An extraordinary general meeting of the shareholders was held in Warrington on July 25th, 1891, when the Chairman announced that an arrangement for amalgamation had been concluded between the partners of the very old establisked and much respected banking firm of Fuller, Banbury, Nix, and Co., of 77, Lombard-st., London, and Parr's Banking Company,whereby the bank obtained entrance to London. Londoners were sorry for the private bank ; but the Lombard-street bankers knew the advent of Parr into the the city meant another keen competitor for London business, for Mr. John Dun had been known to them for many a long day as one of the smartest bank managers in the provinces. Very soon a further announcement was made. It was patent to everybody that the Lombard-street premises though large enough for the business of Messrs. Fuller and Co., were miserably inadequate for the business connection of Parr's, so it was understood that a further change would be made. But few were prepared for the announcement in 1892, that an amalgamation had been effected with the Alliance Bank. By this step splendid premises and a magnificent brokers and general business were pro- cured, and the deposits were increased by no less than q millions. Early this year an arrangement was made by which the old private bank of Sir Samuel Scott, Bart., and Co. was obtained—a firm known all over the world ; and this year the little private bank of Skrubsole and Co., Kingston-on-Thames, has been taken over. For a longtime rumours have been rife as to another important amalgamation or absorption in contempla- tion and two or three names have been mentioned, but we are unable to learn whether there be any truth or I not in the statements. One thing is certain. Parr's ' have no branch in Manchester, although they extend all round it, and it is clear that their customers' connections with, and transactions in, that city must be both numerous and important. When the first report was issued the bank had branches at Northwich (late Thomas Firth and Sons), Runcorn,St. Helens, Widnes, and Winsford, the head office being, as already stated at Warrington. In 1891 the registered office of the company .was removed to Lombard-st., and when the amalgamation with the Alliance was effected the next year, the Bartholemew-lane premises were duly re- corded as the registered office. The Alliance amalgama- tion gave Parr's a large number of London offices,and at the present time the business of the bank is carried on at sixty-four different centres. We think we are right in saying that Parr's can claim the credit of being the first to pay quarterly dividends. For a long time.L. high rate of 19 per cent, per annum has been maintained, and when one remembers that, in addition to this, large sums have been written off property account and the Consols have been written down to 90, ene can only assume that the management of the bank continues of the same able nature that has characterised it all through. Mr. John Dun, who joined the service in 1865, still retains the reins of power, acting in the dual capacity of general manager and director. MR. JOHN DUN.</p>
        <p>timel the!! ••</p>
        <p>The same journal thus alludes to the General Manager :—Mr.John Dun is well known in city financial circles as the general manager of Parr's Banking Com- pany and the Alliance Bank Limited. He left the Bank of Scotland in 1865 in order to become general manager and secretary of parr's Banking Company Limited, a company then formed to take over the business of old private bankers, Parr and Company. It is only neces- sary for us to refer our readers to another page,on which we give the history of Parr's Banking Company and the Alliance Bank, for them to note the continued and un- interrupted success of Mr. John Dun as a bank manager. In 1891, when the batik got its footing in London, Mr. Dun was unanimously raised to the position of director, though he still retained the managemenz. For many years_ Mr. Dun has been a Fellow of the Royal Statistical Society, and in 1875, we believe, read a paper on British Banking Statistics, which was one of the most elaborate and careful analyses of bank figures which had yet been made. This paper was afterwards printed in book form, and attracted a great deal of attention. When Mr. Goschen made his noted speech at Leeds he used the very same arguments which had appeared in the pages of Mr. Dun's book, which, apart from the question of copyright in ideas, was a compliment of the first class. ACQUISITION OF ANOTHER BANK.</p>
        <p>The following circular has been issued :—" 4. Bartho- lomew-lane, London, E.C., 25th August, 1894. Dear Sir,—We beg to announce that, by arrangement with Messrs. Croxon, Jones, and Co. (Old Bank), Li mited,the banking business hitherto carried on by them at Oswes- try will from this date be conducted by us as a branch of Parr's Banking Company and the Alliance Bank, Limited, under the management of Mr. Joseph Grant, who has been for some years in our service, and under the immediate personal superintendence of F. G. Buller Swete, Esq., as local director. We trust the business will continue to have the advantage of your valued ' support.—For Parr's Banking Company and the Alliance Bank, Limited, ALFRED TURNER, Chairman ; JOHN DUN, director and general manager."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>HOW SABEZ BALFOUR WAS CAUGHT.</head>
        <p>HOW SABEZ BALFOUR WAS CAUGHT.</p>
        <p>An Ipswich man, resident in Salta, in the Argentine Republic, in the course of a letter on Argentine affairs generally, sends an interesting narrative of how Libera- tor Balfour was arrested. He says:—" I cannot refrain from mentioning the interest which Mr. Bridgett, our English consul in Buenos Ayres, manifested in Jabez Spencer Balfour. I don't believe you quite know how Jabez was run down, and it is time I t,4ld you the real story. Some months ago a mutual friend from Salta called at the Consulate to see the Consul, and on his way through the hall saw a photograph of Jabez on the wall. He immediately recognised in it the portrait of a man to whom he had let a house at Salta a few weeks previously, and told the consul of the fact. Mr. Bridgett said very little, but did a great deal, for within the shortest possible time, armed with the necessary docu- ments, he was on his way to SAlta, nine hundred miles northwards, leaving word that if inquired for he had gone down to Monte Video to get married (the only feeble part of the business, as Mr. Bridgett is the most confirmed bachelor that ever walked). Arrived at Salta he interviewed the governor, and before Jabez could stir he was astounded by a carefully planned arrest, which took all the wind out of his sails, and left him nothing but the law court to go to for a possible protection. All this story sounds simple, but Salta is so situated that had Balfour received a hint that he was being looked for half-an-hour would have been sufficient for him to put himself out of reach of danger and pursuit b 7 quietly starting for the Bolivian frontier. Every credit is due, therefore, to our consul."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>FIRE INSURANCE IN AUSTRIA.</head>
        <p>FIRE INSURANCE IN AUSTRIA.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED STATE MONOPOLY.</p>
        <p>The " Standard " Vienna correspondent telegraphEl : It is announced that the Austrian Government is con- templating certain reforms in regard to fire insurance which, if carried out, cannot fail to prove of extreme importance. A commission of delegates from different Ministries is said to have advised that the Government shall take all insurance liabilities into its own hands. It is urged that the State offers far greater security than can be given by any, even the best and longest estab- lished private insurance company, and that profits of insurance business ought by right to belong to the State, since insurance is connected with public, not civil law.</p>
        <p>A Newcastle telegram states that the moulders' strike, which has lasted twenty-four weeks and involved a loss of upwards of £200,000 in wages, is in a fair way to be settled, the men's leaders having suggested a return to work on the old terms pending the formation of a board of conciliation and the employers having accepted the proposal. Tbc Royal Laundresses say Oritchley's " STancit Gr,oS Is unequalled for imparting a beautiful finish to starched Ilnen, ad., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn. *all, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your "Starch Gloss." From Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear sit lam pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Pashas, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 3d., and 6d. by most starch sellets. If not in stock will procure it. T. Oarrcravir. Blickburn. PeoPrietor.—ddst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>EXTRAORDINARY MARRIAGE CEREMONIES.</head>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY MARRIAGE CEREMONIES.</p>
        <p>An account of the Fleet marriages will doubtless be interesting to many readers. Of these, Knight, in " Old England," says :—Parents must have had a great horror and dread of Belsize, and of all such places, on account of the fatal facility that existed for every sharper or ad- venturer whether male or female, to inveigle some young wealthy and credulous partner into a Fleet marriage. What that was is worth showing. No feature of London in the last century was more noticeable, few more im- portant for evil. A person could not then frequently pass along Fleet-street or Ludgate Hill without havinc, such occasional interrogatories put to him as " Would you like to be married, sir?" If he looked in a window in the same neighbourkood it was most probable he saw a card, or a large board with the announcement" Wed- dings performed here !" Even time newspapers spread far and wide the tempting invitation in the shape of advertisements, one of which iS here transctibed verb&amp; tim</p>
        <p>" Marriages, with a licemce, certificate, and a croi. stamp, at a guinea, at the new chapel, next door to the china-shop near FlEet Bridge, London, by a regular-bi. clergyman, and not by a Fleet street parson, as is insinu-. ated in the public papers; and that the town may be freed (from) mistakes, no clergyman, being a prisoner in the rules of the Fleet, dare marry ; and to obviate all doubts, this chapel is not in the verge of the Fleet, bul kept by a gentleman who was lately chaplain on boar one of His Majesty's men-cf-war, and likewise has giu— ously distinguished himself in defence of his King and conntry, and is above committing those little n actions that some men impose on people, being di . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Auction 1 . `Puplio ,arci 'N _aloes, •-loni. itillhe abov</head>
        <p>Auction 1 . `Puplio ,arci 'N _aloes, •-loni. itillhe abov</p>
        <p>mean - ieter; misses colum, nos6oliuupi iéLMyfbotb l' circulates 01</p>
        <p>mined to have everything conduct(--d with the utm,. decency and regularity, such as shall all be supported law and equity." But the most amusing feature of t place was to see the contention that was aroused if a carriage appeared with a. couple bound for the Fleet. Up they rushed, clustering, struggling on each side the window—one shouting, " Madam you want a par- son ? lam the clerk, and registrar of the Fleet !" An- other, "Madam, come with me; that fellow will carry you to a little peddling alehouse !" And yet a third, t` Go with me; he will carry you to a brandy shop." So one is speedily chosen, if only to get rid of the rest, and the pair descend, with the bridesmaid following quick after, as soon as she has released her immense hoop from the temporary restrictions it had experienced. The clergyman—a pretty fellow, we may be sure—advances to meet them,all smiles ; he expects to be well paid from the appearance of the pair. And how is the ceremony performed within ? Let us step in and see. As the party ascend the prison stairs and pass along the gallery, they receive invitations to stop. A coal-hewer is particu- larly pressing : " This," says he, " is the famous Lord Mayor's chapel ; and you will get married cheaper here than in any other part of the Fleet." The chaplain who has got the job looks daggers at him, but receives a horse-laugh in reply. And by-the-by, the pair are fortunate—their worthy conductor is sober to-day. They enter his room. There is a hint about brandy and wine, which the excellent priest deals in as well as wedlock ; and both are called for. And the ceremony now pro- ceeds, and is performed, on the whole decently enough. It is the last bit of professional dignity or pride left in the functionary's heart—the going solemnly through the service; he is even indignant when jokes are played upon him, and tries hard to obtain credit for his rigid sense of the prcprieties. All that sort of thing ceases with the conclusion of the ceremony; and woe betide_ the bridegroom if he has not made up his mind to pay handsomely, even according to the Fleet standard,other- wise he will not soon forget the Fleet parson's lesson in- Billingsgate. This is but a literal description of what was taking place daily and hourly in the Fleet prison or precincts. And most calamitous frequently must have been the results of the unions thus formed. There is an entry in one of the registers full of significance :—" William. and Sarah—; he dressed in a gold waistcoat, like an office r ; she a beautiful young lady with two fine diamond rings, and a black high crowned hat, and very well dressed—at Boyce's. N.B.—There are four or five young Irish fellows, seemed to mP, after the marriage was over (mark the rascal's cunning) to have deluded the young woman." False names, half names, or even no names at all, would do with these m:st liberal gentry ; abd if all that was not sufficient, they would get up a " sham " certificate of marriage without any marriage taking place. A marriage of to-day could be dated back for a twelve month or two-; if bride , bridegroom could not convPniently come, there waS one ready to act proxy. Women who were in debt might come here, be married to a husband regularly attached to the place for the purpose, and as soon as mained part to meet no more,—he quite content for a handsome gratuity to be liable for all her debts ; she able to laugh at her old creditors, and take in new ones. Lastly, if money was short, you might go "upon tick" as the " Register "has it; but then, we presume, the security was unexceptionable. A roaring trade was thus obtained. As far as mere income was concerned, it was almost as good for a clergyman to be put into the Fleet for debt, and become popular there, as to achieve some of the highest dignities of the church. Wyatt was one of these popular Fleet parsons; be received, as we learn from his own memorandum book, £57 12s. 9d. (money of the last century, be it observed) for a single month's marriages. William Dare's average complement was some one hundred and fifty or two hundred couple a month; he had a curate to assist him. The most notorious of them all was Keith, "who had constructed a very bishopric for revenue in May fair, by performing that charitable function for a trifling sum which the poor successors of the apostles are seldom humble enough to perform out of duty." (Walpole). But the glory of the Fleet marriage system was to depart from it at the inexorable fiat of King, Lords, and Com- mons. A new Marriage Bill was brought. forward in 1753, making the publications of banns indispensable, and was passed, though in the face probably of the most determined opposition that a successful bill ever met with. Most ludicrous were the reasons alleged against it. A naval captain remarked that lie had once given forty of his crew leave to go ashore, and the whole returned married; of course, therefore, he was aghast at the idea of a bill that would stop all such proceedings for the future. The last marriages in the Fleet were fixed to take place on the 24th of March, and the utmost use was made of the time afforded. In one day Keith married one hundred and seventy-three pairs; and in the last—the terril,le 24th—we have records of above three hundred marriages having taken place, and even that number may not have included the whole.</p>
        <p>or</p>
