      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>MR. CHAMBERLAIN AT HOME.</head>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN AT HOME.</p>
        <p>" A Travelling Correspondent " of the " Pall Mall Gazette" writes :—" No wonder Mr. Chamberlain in his own locality is thought a great deal of, and that quite apart from the national political distinction he has acquired. I came across a gentleman in Birmingham the other day who gave me the following interesting* information :—lt appears that every year Mr. Chamber- lain, once a week uring the summer months, throws open the grounds adjoining his suburban residence to the visitation of the board school children of the city. The girl children, to the number of 300 or 400 at a time, ride in brakes with their teachers to the entrance to the park, They then descend and roam about to their hearts' content. Before leaving in the evening they are supplied with tea and the usual 'edibles. On departing from the premises a living plant in 4 flower-pot is supplied to each child, both as a memento of her visit and as an object worthy of her care. In this admirable work one or more members of Mr. Chamberlain's family or relatives are always present to add to the enjoyment which teachers and children must not unnaturally feel. One of the daughters of the gentleman who gave me this information stated that on the occasion of her visit a month ago there were 388 other school girls present. The children, I may add, are drawn from all parts of the city, and those of rich and poor parents, for once at any rate, take their games and have their enjoyments on equal terms.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY. There was a largely attended meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon. Mr. J. S. Garland occupied the chair, and delivered a brief address. He stated that he was one of the first who some years ago, strongly advo- cated those Sunday afternoon services at Wycliffe. He rejoiced to see so many of his fellow men gathered together there to listen to the gospel of • Jesus Christ. The speaker then referred to some holiday scenes in Wales which he had had the pleasure of seeing, and drew some lessons to be learnt from them. Mr. Bateman, of Manchester, delivered an instructive address' the sub- ject of which was "Myself." Mr. A. Griffiths was the soloist and be sang a hymn and the solo " The Holy City." Mr. T. Wallington (secretary), and Mr. R. Atkinson (treasurer) also took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>The Recorder of Liverpool on Tuesday quashed an order made by the city magistrates for the destruction of certain copies of a number of " Pick-me-Up," remarking that only very prurient people could find indecency in the pictures complained of.</p>
      </div>
