        <p>_ _</p>
        <p>By Mr. WILLIAM ELLIS.</p>
        <p>THERE was a good deal of inter- The Ship Canal. eating matter in the speech which Mr. J. K. BYTHELL addressed to the meetimg of Ship Canal shareholders in Manchester on Tiles- , day. Mr. BYTHELL was, it will be remembered, a short time ago appointed salaried Chairman of the Canal Company on condition that he devoted all his time atid energy to the management of the Company's affairs and to the quest of that which the Canal is now most in need of—traffic. He therefore now speaks with all the knowledge and authority which an occupancy of that responsible office confers. On the whole the irnprQssion which one gathers from a perusal of his lucid and business-like speech is that he is of the opinion that the Canal is bound to be a commercial success in the end but that in order to make it a ! success there are still many obstacles to be cleared out of the way or smoothed over. The forces which are still in active or passive hostility to the making of a port at Manchester are not only extremely powerful but also very subtle in their operations. It is clear that the railway interest has not yet been conciliated. The Canal</p>
        <p>Company, says Mr. BYTHELL, are still waiting for the exceptional railway rates which are enjoyed by other ports, and, he naturally adds, they cannot wait for ever. They do not want to quarrel with the railway companies, but after spending fifteen millions to make the Canal they will fight for what they claim to be their rights even against the railway companies. Then, again,the steamship companies are rather hard to move. They have established lines and connec- tions, and it needs strong inducements to divert them into new routes. But upon one point, and that the essential one, Mr. BYTHELL was clear enough. Itis that the fortunes of the Canal depend upon Manchester itself. Let the merchants and manufacturers of that great emporium find pro- fitable cargoes for vessels and the vessels will come. We are assured that on the score ef naviga- tion there are no impediments. The criticisms that have been passed on the Canal as a navigable waterway have caused many shipowners to make an examination for themselves and these ship- owners have, says Mr. BYTHELL, been surprised to see how safe and easy was the navigation,and how • good was the accommodations at the docks. It is clear, therefore, that in the last resort the success of the Canal is really in the hands of the business men of Manchester themselves, and when one con- sidirs the tenacity and determination which have been displayed all along, from the inception of •the scheme till the present time, there is little to suggest that the same qualities will not carry the matter to a successful issue, in spite of the very f(Tinidable " lions " that are still to be removed from the path.</p>
        <p>THERE were some notable circum- The Lieensin Sessions. gstances in connection with Warring-</p>
        <p>ton Licensing Sessions last week. In the .first place the magistrates acted with a vigour and an independence vvhich,to say the least the local bench has not often before displayed in dealing with licensed houses. On this occasion there was no question of granting new licenses, or of making bargains with wealthy brewers through which new districts might be " tapped " by the liquor trade. A powerful and, as it turned out, a successful attack was made upon existing licenses. The license of the Swan Inn was refused, chiefly on the grounds of structural unsuitability and of the difficulty of police supervision, and the license of the "Pelican" was deferred pending further consideration. In addition to this several music licenses were refused yesterday. On this occasion the temperance party of Warring- ton have achieved a real victOry and one which is bound to have a salutary effect. Their case was admirably got up and admirably presented to the court. The evidence they were in a position to lay before the magis- trates was in some respects of a startling charac- ter, and certainly tended to justify the allegations of lax supervision of public-houses in Warrington which have been made from time to time. The feeling that must arise in all decent minds on reading the evidence will be that this sort of thing should not have been tolerated, and that it should not have been left to private individuals, acting under a strong sense of public duty,to make the needed exposure. Something was said about the hands of the police being tied by the Watch Committee in their dealings with publicans and any suspicions which may have been entertained in that respect will not be allayed by what was said in court. It seems clear that before any action can be taken against offending publicans the Chief Constable is under the necessity of reporting the case to the Watch Committee and of taking their instructions. This is not a good system. The magistrates sitting in open court are the proper authority to determine offences against the law. The Watch Committee lay them- selves open to the imputation of being biassed judges and in any case their proceedings are liable to the objection of being conducted in the strict- est privacy.</p>
        <p>THE Leicester election, like that The Leicester at Attercliffe,shows that dissension Election. has, to an alarming extent,crept into the ranks of progressive politicians. The two Liberal candidates, Messrs. BROADHURST and HAMILL, have been returned, but not with the majorities that have been the rule in Leicester. In 1885 the Liberal majority was considerably over 4,000 and in 1886 the poll of the lowest Liberal was 4,000, all but five above that of the Tory. In 1892 the Conservatives had not the hardihood to contest the seat at all. Yet on Wednesday if Mr. ROLLESTON, the Conservative candidate had polled 218 more votes he would have displaced Mr. HAZELL. Tito explanation for this remarkable change is fortunately, or unfortunately, not far to seek. In the first place, it would appear that the Liberal leaders did not make the best possible choice in selecting Mr. HAZELL as one of their candidates. Leicester is above all else a working- man's town, and its workingmen are strong and militant in their trades unionism as well as in their Radicalism. Rightly or wrong'y a cor.sider- able proportion of them seem to have formed the opinion that Mr. HAZELL had not in his trading connections, been everything that Trades Unionists could desire. In the second place, the Independent Labour Party put forward a candidate and fought his battle so vigorously that they polled no fewer than 4,402 votes, much the largest poll that that party has yet made in any constituency. On the whole, Liberals may derive some useful instruction from the Leicester election. Like some other recent elections it goes to prove that the growing demand for candidates with pronounced views upon social and economic questions cannot be safely ignored. To now bring forward candidates of any other kind in working-class constituencies</p>
        <p>is simply to provoke dissension and to invite opposition. •In this respect the recent career of Mr. BROADHURST himself conveys an unmistake- able lesson. As a Trades Unionist of the indivi- dualist type and an opponent of eight hours legis- lation he has been twice defeated in his attempts to regain a Parliamentary footing; as a convert to the advanced labour .programme he has been returned at the head of the poll for Leicester.</p>
      </div>
