      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>&amp;ales bp aurtton.</head>
        <p>&amp;ales bp aurtton.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>At the above court on Wednesday, William Dob- son, aged 15 years, .was charged with inflicting bodily harm on a boy named • John White, at Moore on Saturday with a gun-barrel. James Barrow said that on the day in question he waq at Moore in company with i;obson and White, together with some other boys. De- fendant and complainant had been fighting and defendant had gone home and come hack with a carving knife and gun-barrel. A boy named Gandy took the knife from him. Defendant struck complainant on the head with the gun-barrel and ; almost stunned him. Witness accompanied com- plainant home where be is now in bed and under the care of a doctor. P.C. Shatwell said that he apprehended the defendant in Moore on Tuesday night and conveyei him to the Stockton Heath police station. On Wednesday morning he went to see White and found him in bed. He would not be able 'o appear for a week or so. Defenda.At was remanded until yesterday week.</p>
        <p>lIMIMMII</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>SENSATIONAL STREET TRAGEDY AT</head>
        <p>SENSATIONAL STREET TRAGEDY AT</p>
        <p>OLDHAM</p>
        <p>A WOMAN MURDERED BY „HER HUSBAND. Shortly after midnight on Saturday a most brutal murder, the result of jealousy, was committed in Old- ham. It appears that on Saturday afternoon and even- ing a window-blind maker named George Wilshaw Whitehead, residing at 3, Off Moor-street, Oldham. had been engaged looking for his wife, regarding whom he harboured feelings of jealously. He had visited a num- ber of publichouses' but bad failed to find her. At twenty minutes past twelve he suddenly met the woman at Mumps, near the Manchester and County Bank, and it being the Wakes the street was crowded with hun- dreds of people on their way home. Mrs. Whitehead was with her sister and the latter's husband and a Young man. None of the four seemed to have noticed Whitehead, who was observed by a passer by to suddenly rush up to his wife, and before he could be interfered with be caught her by the hair and, drawing the woman's head back, get her throat tight. It was then noticed that he had in his right hand a razor, the steel blade of which glittered in the light from the lamps. It was the work of a moment, for the man drew the razor across her throat. The murderer then released his hold of the woman, who fel! with a heavy thud upon the pavement, her husbana making off at the same time. The large crowds •of people hear were horror-stricken, and did not realize what had been done until Whitehead had made his escape along Beever-street. It was then seen that MrS• Whitehead was bleeding to death, her head being almost severed from her body. In fact it was found on examination by Dr. Martland, opposite whose house the deed was done, that it only hung by a small niece. of flesh at the back. The woman never uttered a word after the attack, nor was the husband heard to make use of any ex- pression either before or after the crime. When the victim of the attack was found to 1e past all aid a search was made for the man Whitehead. An hour later, upon the police breaking into his house, in Off Moor-street, they found him bleeding from a wound in his throat. He was at the time in one of the upstairs rooms. After being attended he was taken to the infirmary in the horse ambulance, and upon inquiry at that institution it was stated that his injuries were not of a serious nature. It appears that after the murder the man went to a mill lodge and threw himself into the water, but, failing to drown himself, went home,ehanged his wet clothes, and then cut hiq throat.. There were found in hi 3 pockets a blood-stained razJr and a knife. The former was quite new and has since been identified by a cutler's shopman as having been purchased by the prisoner on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN MERSEY MISSION DRUM AND FIFE BAND.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN MERSEY MISSION DRUM AND FIFE BAND.</p>
        <p>The members of this musical combination met on Friday evening in the Seamen's Institute, Runcorn, Mr. Wm. Shaw, missionary, in the chair, for the purpose of making presentations to Miss Bowyer and Mr. Wm. Byfield, drum major. The Chairman having spoken a few words with reference to the long and valuable services which both the lady and gentle- man named had discharged for the benefit of the Kw' sion and its band, Mr. J. Wakefield handed to Miss Bowyer two prettily designed boxes ; one for handker- chiefs and the other for gloves. In making the presen- tation, Mr. Wakefield remarked that Miss Bowyer in tin course of her lengthy connection with the Mission, ha° ever exerted herself to the utmost in her own sphere to improve the prospects of the Band, and to meet the con- venience of its members. She had always been ready and willing to fulfil any office, or undertake and carrY through any work likely to advance the status of the band: He had, therefore, the greatest pleasure on behalf of his brother members, in offering for her acaeptance the articles named as slight marks of their gratitude and respect. Miss Bowyer in accepting the gifts expressed her sincere thanks for the, favour shown her. She had worked for the mission with no anticips- tion of such returns. It was quite sufficient reward tO her to know that she was and had been at some use to the cause; and had been the means of benefitting the ' band in the smallQst degree. It was very kind of the members to so acknowledge her services, and she hoped that in the future she would be enabled to he of ever' greater value to the band and mission than hitherto. She accepted the presents with the greatest pleasure and would always esteem the donors. Subs-quently, Mr. J. Owen presented to Mr. Wm. Byfield, drum mdjor, oh behalf of the members, a handsome silk neckerchief. In performing the ceremony, Mr. Owen observed that Mr. Byfield had ever been a pillar of strength to both the band and the mission. His relations with them extended over many years and they had always found in him true friend. His willingness to co-operate with them to the fullest extent in hard work was exemplari; and many a time had he stimulated then when they had felt inclined to lag behind - Mr. Owen concluded by expressing himself as very pleased at having the opportunity, through the rest of the members, in so recognizing the merit of Mr: Byfield as a co-worker and he hoped he would accept the the present as a small token of their esteem. Mr. Byfielu replied that he had always taken the greatest interest in the doings of the mission and the band—not with the' object of any recompense,but simply because he admired the work which the mission was carrying on. It yr° very gratifying to find that one's services were appreciated so much, and they might rest assured that he should always manifest a deep regard for the welfare' of the band. If the instrumentalists would only stick together he said he did not see why they should not make the band more successful every year.</p>
        <p>THE HOWLEY WARD ELECTION. -- MEETING YESTERDAY WEEK. A meeting in support of the candidature of Mr; Jamee Battersby, the labour candidate, was held on Friday in the National schoolroom, Church-st• There was a good attendance. The chair was °cal' pied by the president of the Trades' Council, Mr' Thomas Malkin, and there were also on the plat" form: Mr. Battersby (candidate), Messrs. S. Wright, J.P.; J. Bullock (secretary Trades Council). Lang, J. Garner and others. The Chairman in a lengthy speech contended that theY were entitled to at least one labour representative on the Town Council out of 36 members. The. -licensed victuallers were already too well repre- sented on the Council.—Mr. Battersby dealt with the various items contained in his address to 9e electors.—Mr. S. Wright proposed a resolution In favour of Mr. Batter,by. Mr. Bullock seconded tb resolution, and said that their canvass so far haa been very satisfactory. On being put to the tne6fr ing the resolution was carried unanimously. The polling for a representative on the Warrington Town Council for the Howley ward took place on NlottlaY: The polling station was in the National Schools, a' which the presiding officers were Messrs. T. H. GratiZar; and C. H. Unsworth, end the. poll clerk, Messrs. Del Smith and T. G. Grounds. During the day there Wl' very little interest taken in the election although gessilo Battersby and EYans,the two candidates put forth eVe." exertion. There was almost an entire absence of .31 a mural literature. The pn-I,Y closed at eight o'cl°° and the ballot hoxes were at once taken to the Tol Hall where the votes were counted. A large crowd gathered before the steps of the Town Hall to hear result. and at about twenty minutes to nine, AlderMa'l Wright, the returning officer of the ward, is the absen.c.:, of Alderman Francomb, announced the figures a° follows :</p>
        <p>JOHN EVANS 484 JAMES BA. LTERSBY</p>
        <p>MAjirity for Mr. Evans 340</p>
        <p>The result was received with cheers and eroan° the crowd which quickly dispersed after the figures Wv*" announced. THE ALLEGED OUTRAGE NE/AR $T'</p>
        <p>F</p>
