        <head>HELENS.</head>
        <p>HELENS.</p>
        <p>PRISONER COMMITTED FOR TRIAL. At St. Helens on Saturday. James Doti.han was brought before the magistrates chareed with unlawful./ nti wounding a fellow-workman named John Greater. --Oti the 3rd inst. the men were engaged at a sand was,h„;d, Crank Brow, near St. Helens, when, it was alie(l. Doughan ran a box of sand over Grealer's right 114",..e Be then took up a spade and struck Grealev over tut, head and shoulders, seriously injuring him. H,: had beeo confined to bed with a fractured shoulder I)l3de 313 i, other injuries. Grealy was able to appear on Seurci,3lo, and looked very weak and ill He did tilt retnerlberi;,o assault, but said about three or four years ago r";ne made an attack on him, but was stopped. Mar,. ":nd Sutton, a single woman, who witnessed the prevented Doughan further injuring Grealer, 12'ive dence. When charged with the offence pris",pr. ' "I never went near him ;I never hit him• Brooks, ger- who attended Grealey, said he found him 6u,,a11 , ing from an incised wound on the scalp three and inches in length, penetrating to the bone. The shoulder was extensively bruised, the shoulder ud Go being, fractured. There was also a lacerated w"na * the little finger of the right hand. GrealeY wacikell dangerous condition at the time. A spade %vas thing to cause the scalp wound.—Prisoner said 11,,e,d00 nothing to do with the man whatever. The 'Estt caught him. Be was committed for trial at the 11 Liverpool Assizes. haVe It is stated that the Northumberland mincr-,;,tinv decided by vote by a large majority against alu"-d to with the 'Miners' Federation on the terms pr9rse them—namely, that they should withdraw their.ll orrait :jou to the Elit Iluur Bill and fall intu policy of the Federation on that question.</p>
