        <p>kr. cA: SUCCESSFEL RUNCORN HORSE BREEDER. ChadWiCk. of Crofton Lodge, Higher Run- , Mad Sutton Bridge Farm, has again received valu- ,„ `cognition for animals which he has produced. On al', at the Worselek and District Agricultural t tlie IT' 4,4 exhibit of his, in the class for horses open o asseilin'ted Kingdom, draught or agricultural stallion, It kZa off the first prize. Mr. Chadwick also obtained the-"ed Prize for gelding or mare; and premiums in t48,,,tt tions for brood mare with filly at foot, and filly by tel of Ellesmere's shire bred horse. Alf ft A uO4 ,r, -REEABLE SURPRISE FOR A RUNCORN TOPER. fluths,"esdaY morning before Mr. F. J. Norman, at the toe -to Police Court, a charge of drunkenness in Hal- e tZoad qe the previous evening was proved against be fr,/173' nettled Robert Ridler. A constable stated that tien-ued Ridler in such an advanced stage of intoxica- ts. ,ttiat he locked him up for safety. Ridler was fined teki°d• including costs, but told the magistrates that he 10t0.11.9 Money,and was making up his mind,with a very DO taee te go to gaol, when he was informed by the fC,,,s'oe that &amp;lid. had been found upon him when taken cu.„ maost„,„"_?elY• At this piece of good news, Robert's face Ite "'"eu perceptibly, and as the remaining coppers Atja laanded over to Lim, a broad grin of satisfaction til,e' over his countenance, and he left the dock quicker , abe entered it. 0., A Potasss WOUNDING CASE AT WIDNES. Mr. H. Wade Deacon. a Polish labourer, named 'hors' MODday afternoon, at the Widnes Police Conrt, .1101;:e lt " Gryzpe of Prospect-row, Park-road, St. Helens, me eh 0 %qv, arged on remand with unlawtully wounding Cr Pole named John Vestkavitch, 15, Water-street, tie Iles. on the 20th inst. The prisoner came to Widnes settz4tle date named for the purpose of having a letter Ate) 'which he had received from his wife in Poland. ilt eleven o'clock at night the prisoner and prosecutor the house 17, Water-street, in company with a fet.r of other Poles. A quantity of drink was sent PriVt„," a dispute occurred, and it is alleged that the 4Mie-"," went out of the house, and returned a few /V4les later carrying something behind his back. I.loei„'.'hi3 he struck prosecntor on the head four times, tleetreg a jagged wound and a clean-cut wound, both three inches long.—The hearing of the case i"hDied neveral hours owing te tbe evidence having to les i',,t:erPreted. Eventually the prisoner was committed 141.4 "al at the county sesaions, but admitted to bail. Tihi'4Cl"4" WOREDIRN ENJOY A TRIP TO LIVERPOOL. aeto e workmen employed at the Sprinch-yard,Runcorn, thettitPatlied by their wives and families, the whole of •,Ithe , Iliuribering about 500, embarked on board Idiytel,:„' "agmar on Monday morning on an excursion to I,te o'lo4 which is likely t 0 become annual. They left ' ee, eme's Pier, Bottom Locks at eight o'clock,and after itlN,,asmot sail arrived at Liverpool at about (ten o'clock Lie ae- ai late number of the party found muchlinterest tute4,4esPection of the docks. Others travelled per the Itity.a"saP Railway to various well-known parts of the -IT,' the remainder crossed by boat to New Brighton. tliee ,r4,s sof amusements were indulged in and subse- '"'M :47' a Rand dinner being partaken of the New Brigh- .aphoi itor, made their way back to Liverpool. The toe,: Paity then re-embarked on the Deemer and were Runcorn. Among those who joined the were Messrs. John Dykes (foreman carpen- Mt the'd William Cropper (foreman joiner), The thanks Ilacie2e„so are due to Mr. W. H. Collier for so kindly ii‘Mes tos`Le,e steamer at their disposal, for their k convey- iit: iverpool. -,., •'-• B. BRUNDRIT OF RUNCORN ATTAINS His 1, Ne b" MAJORITY. Zr. inhßinald Ernest Barclay Brundrit, eldest son of ‘, teirre Brundrit, J.P ,of Runcorn, came of age on riieY whilst the. family were staying at their summer ,rt 0.-"„, e." View," North Penmaenmawr. The event was g9is "1, observed in various parts of Runcorn by the ,4)41'48a:t1371:103fm, but was celebrated at Penmaenmawr by „ demonstrations. The quarries belonging to Itittitexi; nrondrit and Company,were festively decorated i,Il th ~.etiog and nags, end at periods volleys were fired "4rdt -clrkings The employ6e of the quarries after- lee i 8 Partoek 'f the hos itality of Mr. John Brundrit, 3r, eeh--Ilthath,e,courose of their entertainments expressed the t:tbes aPpiness and prosperity would i atten..l both son. A large bon-fire was kindled n the 1.-:_tele.'ou;),,o. the summit of one of the quarries and the L._oteap cl b 0 seen for a considerable distance around e,rl,,ln4wr. The workpeople's children then congre- 41)br --`side "North View," and sang various selections to the occasion. In response,. house. Brundrit, tokt,k unressed them from the terrace of toe H F'elle em all for the kind expressions they had ex- nNi towards him' which he very much appreciated. Zareb;,9edrit, junr, who has recently left Clare College, in. tends to follow his father's business as a F.v hoptstroprietor and ship owner. Many valuable h,,Liave been forwarded to him, some being of a rt 4 v-"usorne design. ,SSSEt. or A. RUNCORN CAPTAIN NARROWLY ESCAPES at)t i BEING WRECKED. .0.14riea.,..n_ Rigby, of Runcorn, had rather a disagreeable Capetown on Friday morning. After pass- r" when commarding his steamer, the Dunottar 4 k oßjetn „_ad lo_riant, the vessel encountered dense fogbanks, 5 0 efore f• ''''h nearing Plymouth ran upon the Eddystone h II this, Captain Rigby, calculating that ~ onottar Castle had run her distance, began hp'4r he ad _ r course. Almost simultaneously breakers I,llll:ta;teetren,reported, and the engines were put full bow grazed before the vessel had time to gather grazed the rocks with a dull scraping E ail o_4 wit then the fog lifted, and to the consternation ,4,34eci Nide hoard the Eddystone light was seen right ahead ' tern, he ridge. The engines were kept full speed 1 icled aod the ship gradually, to the relief of everyone, L'lto Pl()„fl into deep water again, and was brought safely b!. ve4.4l,neth Sound where the passengers, 216 in num- 'st 'e ianded Am' onc, them was the Rev. Harry 'itle'eon-i i • - of Mr. Gladstone. The vessel groueded e-ister scone's throw of the lighthouse, and some Por natirm tte_ god, .Prevailed, one excited passenger shouting, 114,,re lqwe '..,s4ke save yourselves; we are lost." Boats 4;1)4 ~,'-reo am a precautionary measure, but they were s. tore-. "et wanted although an hour and a half elapsed ..rt -lt w ' • 1,,, ma known whether the vessel would float or tenet "e Bunottar Castle arrived at Gravesend on aft, tly In, . .1,11:. di.,c, ...eireng. She was docked in the evening.and tt44,:aus...aneaortitnz,%rer wrgnoq was dry-docked for repairs. confined entirely to the fore- 14g abait the chain locker heing intact.</p>
        <p>kttlrda,</p>
        <p>RUNCORN OCCASIONAL POLICE COURT. Before Messrs. James Handley and F. J. Norman, on Wednesday morning, at the Runcorn Police Court, Elizabeth Barker, for not appearing on the previous Monday at the Sessions, to answer a charge of being drunk and disorderly, on the 22nd August, in Bridge- water-st., was sent to prison for 14 days. Defendant had previously been convicted for like offences.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB. A number of the riders of this club collected on Devon- shire Square and started at 2.30 prompt on Saturday afternoon on a spin to Chester, by way of Frodsham and Helsby. The roads were in capital condition and the run was exceedingly enjoyed. Tea was partaken of at their destination, and a pleasant sail down the river Dee was participated in by all the members. Other kinds of pleasure were indulged in and eventually the riders re- turned to Runcorn, covering the distance from Chester in ninety minutes, which is comfortable time. HELP FOR THE CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL.—Special ser- vices were conducted on Sunday morning,afternoon and evening, in the Weston Church on behalf of the funds of the Sunday school connected with the church. The Rev. W.Poole, M.A.,of Walkden, preached in the morning and the Rev. Stanton Jones, M.A., of St. Paul's, Widnes in the afternoon (to men only) and evening. Special selec- tions were rendered by the choristers, the evening anthem being "Lord of all power and might." There were good congregations and the collections were highly satisfactory. ACCIDENT TO A PAINTER AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An accident which ruight have proved much more seriou' s befel a young man named Frederick Spenser, of Irwell-lane, Runcorn, on Wednesday afternoon, whilst he was engaged painting the St. Edwards Catholic Church. , The ladder upon which he was standing suddenly slipped ' and he fell a distance cf about eighteen feet from the lower roof of the building on to the gravel path. He was apparently in great pain when picked up and was imme- diately conveyed home where he was shortly afterwards attended by Dr. McLennon, who found no bones broken. The injured man is now reported to be progressing favourably towards recovery. TEMPERANCE LECTURES IN RUNCORN SCHOOLS. During the present week Mr. J. J. Jakeman, a lecturer connected with the Band of Hope Union, Liverpool Centre, has been lecturing in the various schools of Run- corn on the injurious effects of alcohol upon the human system. Great attention has been paid to the gentleman's remarks by both boys and girls, whose work it is now to write essays on the subject and submit them to Mr. Jakeman. This gentleman will examine each prodaction and for the best ones he will, under the auspices of the Band of Hope Union' present the writers with illumin- ated certificates. The results of this competition are ex- pected to be made known in about six week's time.</p>
        <p>PROVIDENTIAL ESCAPE FROM DROWNING AT RUNCORN. On Wednesday evening, about seven o'clock, a little boy named Charles Fox, six years old, nephew of Mr. Joseph Fox, High-street, Runcorn, experienced a most providential escape from drowning. The little fellow, who was at the time on a visit to his uncle, was playing on the bank of the Bridgewater Canal in the vicinity of the Top Locks. Suddenly he caught sight of a piece of wood in the water, and in his eagerness to seize it he fell head foremost into the canal. An alarm was raised by a companion and after the boy had sunk a second time a passing boatman threw off his coat and reached it out to him. This the boy succeeded in grasp- ing and was pulled safely to the bank. He was subse- quently carried home, where, 'after the necessary atten- tion had been bestowed upon him, he recovered from the shock and soon regained his former spirits,seeming little the worse for his disagreeable dip.</p>
        <p>TRIP FROM RUNCORN TO CHESTER. On Tuesday an enjoyable day was passed by members of the Young Men's Bible Class connected with the Ellesmere-street United Methodist Free Church, Run- corn. The occasion was their annual trip, and the rendezvous was the famous old city of Chester. Starting from Runcorn in waggonettes about nine o'clock they arrived at their destination shortly before twelve o'clock. , They then repaired to the Belgra via Hotel where an excellent dinner was served and to which they did full justice. Afterwards a sail was indulged in on the Dee down at far as Eaton Hall. Returning to Chester about 5.30, a pleasant tea was partaken of at the same hotel and enjoyed. Subsequently the party spent nearly an hour in strolling round the walls of the city and treated themselves with a sight of the many centres of historical interest that are to be found there. Finally, the members were conveyed home shortly after seven o'clock reaching Runcorn at about ten o'clock. The weather throughout the day was superb, and added in no small degree to the pleasure of the excursionists.</p>
        <p>RE-OPENING OF THE RUNCORN SCIENCE AND ART CLASSES. By referring to our advertising columns it will be seen that the science and art classes carried on by the Runcorn School Board with so much success are to be resumed on Monday, October Ist. As formerly, the classes will assemble in the Greenway-road Board school, with the exception of the dressmaking class which will meet in the Bethesda Lecture Hall. It is quite needless for us to dwell on the capable manner in which these classes have been carried on in the past, and the success which has attended them. Such facts are fully known by everyone, seeing that the Classes Committee have received such general and well-deserved recognition. The next session if anything, is anticipated to prove more successful than former ones, and it is to be hoped that all Runcorn students will continue to avail themselves of the advan- tages offered by the classes and show their appreciation of the beneficial work by at once becoming members. THE ALLEGED PARAFFIN LAMP OUTRAGE AT WIDNES. At the Widnes Police Court on Monday, before Mr. H. Wade Deacon, a married woman named Catherine M‘Mullen, living in Millington-st., was charged with wounding Julia Maud M'Kay, a single woman, with in- tent to do her grevious bodily harm. Inspector Keigh- ley said that prisoner went to the house 16,Water-street, occupied by the prosecutrix's parents, and in the course of a quarrel the prisoner picked up a lighted paraffin lamp and threw it at M‘Kay, who was severely burnt about the legs, arms, and body, and even if she did well the prosecutrix would not be able to attend court for three or four weeks.—Gordon Miltay, father of the pro- secutrix, gave formal evidence of the assault —Dr. C. Hutchinson stated that the prosecutrix was very badly burnt on the legs, arms, and body.-1 he Deputy-Magis- trates' Clerk (Mr. Lewin) : She has been in a very dan- gerous condition P—Witness : Yes.—Mr. Lewin : And is still P—Witness : Yes ; but I am in hopes that she will recover, but have a doubt about it.—Mr. Lewin : It will be some considerable time before she is able to attend here to give evidence ?—Witness : Yes.—Mr. Lewin : Not in a week's time P—Witness : No.—lnspector Keigh- ley : Is she out of danger P—Witness : No.—Mr.Lewin : Better remand prisoner here for a week.—Mr. Wade Deacon : You think the woman might die P—Witness : Yes.—Mr. Wade Deacon (to prisoner) : You will be re- manded here for a week.—Prisoner was then remanded n custody. TWENTY-FIVE MILE RACE BY THE RUNCORN C.C. Under the auspices of the Runcorn Cycling Club a twenty-five mile race was run off on Wednesday even- ing. There were seventeen entries, but only six turned up and competed. The course, which measured actu- ally 27 miles, was from a point in Stockton Heath to Mere Corner, on the Knutsford-road, along the outskirts ef Bowdon, through Lymin, past the Dog and Dart, and back to Mere Corner again. The order in which the competitors started was as follows :—Tinsley 7 minutes start from scratch ; F. Crimes, 5 minutes ; W.Mack and C. Johnson, 3 minutes ; whilst the scratchmen were LeCouteur and George Whinyates. As will be seen the course was rather a hilly one, and in some portions of it, the riding proved very heavy. The riders were fortun- ate, however, in not meeting with any traffic, a circum- stance which undoubtedly proved very advantageous. After seven miles had been covered Whinyates gained the leading position and Mack followed close behind, a good second. Before a third of the ground had been spun over LeCouteur and Tinsley gave up. The remain- ing two, however, kept up a spirited race, Johnson soon securing third place. The four riders anived at Mere Corner in the following order and time :—Whinyates, Ist, 1 hour, 25 minutes ; Mack 2nd. 1 hour,36 minutes; Johnson 3rd. 1 hour, 43 minutes; Crimes 4th. 1 hour, 45 minutes ; Whinyates was paced by Messrs. F. Sherratt (Widnes C.C.) and T. H. Hearne (Warrington C.C.) ; whilst Mack was paced by Messrs. J. Dalgleish, and Thomas Jones (Widnes C.C.) on a tandem safety. Mr. W. Wiswall acted as timekeeper and starter. The prizes are respectively, a geld medal,l silver medal with gold centre and a silver medal.</p>
