      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>HAYDOCK LOCAL BOARD CLERKSHIP.</head>
        <p>HAYDOCK LOCAL BOARD CLERKSHIP.</p>
        <p>A special .meeting of this Board was held on Thursday morning to consider the applications re- ceived • for the post of Clerk-Surveyor rendered vacant by the resignation of Mr. J. Matthews, the present clerk. There were 36 applications, and after going through them, the following were selected to appear before the Board on Tuesday next when the matter will be decided, viz. :—Wm. Unsworth, Toxteth Park Local Board; Richard Brierley, junr.. Newton; A.l.ieith, Golborne ; C. D. Thompson, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SUTTON.</head>
        <p>SUTTON.</p>
        <p>SAD DEATII OF AN OLD MAN.—Mr. J. E. Worsley, coroner, held an inquiry on Tuesday afternoon at the Aston Arins,Sutton,relative to the death of a man named Mamas Massey. 77 years of age, and recently residing at Frodsham Bridge, Sutton.—The testimony of the various witnesses showed that the deceased, who had for four years been periodically employed at Marsh Gate Farm, bv Mr. S. B. Chadwick, was in the loft at this farm putting some hay in order. Whilst thus engaged he fell a distance of eight feet through a manhole into a bin. beneath. Dr. Burton was called in and announced that the unfortunate man had broken his back. The occurrence happened at. 1.15 on the 21st inst. and notwithstanding the skill of Dr Burton, the injured man sank and died on the 27th. He possessed a strong con- stitution. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental Death." On Thursday afternoon the remains of the deceased were laid to rest in Aston churchyard, the funeral ceremony being witnessed by a large number of</p>
      </div>
