      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 19 -••-• ‘-' -•• • – THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT MARRIAGE OF MR. R. POSNETT AND I A WEEK'S TOITEt ON THE RUNCORN. I MISS FANNY GARNET. I – I CONTINENT. BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 19 -••-• ‘-' -•• • - THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT MARRIAGE OF MR. R. POSNETT AND I A WEEK'S TOITEt ON THE RUNCORN. I MISS FANNY GARNET. I - I CONTINENT. BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR.</p>
        <p>It would be a matter of interest to many Run- cornians to know the mind of the managers of the Manchester Ship Canal Company concerning their obligation to provide suitable accommodation at Runcorn for the repairing of vessels. On the part of the Company, the question is fast approaching that stage at which it will find a peaceful resting place on one of their shelves and the dust will gather so quickly and thickly upon it that all trace of such a proposal will be lost. This is a result which it is the duty of the Improvement Commissioners to prevent, and for the sake of the future prosperity of the town it is to be hoped that they will so grapple with the company as to produce something more than mere paper from them. We have already had a specimen of letter writing from the Company and that one should be quite sufficient to whet the weapons of our Commissioners. It is a pity that Runcorn does not possess those facilities for repair- ing vesselswhich we all know are absolutely neces- sary, to the growth of a port and the prosperity of a town. At one time she boasted of such accommo- dation in the form of severel yards, slips&amp;c., dotted up and down the river; but these were ruthlessly devastated by the Canal Company on the promise that provision to recompense the shiprights trade should be made within twelve months after the completion of the Canal.</p>
        <p>If the Company intend to act up to their agree- ment they will have to begin working sharp. But there seems to be no such good sign of their wil- lingness to fulfil their promises. There is actually, whilst I am writing, a vessel on the town side of Runcorn Bridge in the river Mersey being repaired by a prominent firm of shipwrights in the town. The workmen are obliged to do what they cam to her whilst the tide is out, or otherwise she would, in all probability be taken to Liverpool or Elles- mere Port for repairs, where proper accommodation is to be procured. A few days ago a large vessel, requiring extensive alterations and repairs was compelled to run on to Manchester to get the work done, because her skipper was afraid that the train consequent upon her being beached at Runcorn might prove injurious and dangerous. Plenty of . other cases could be cited, pointing out the urgent necessity that exists for some sort of provision, but surely these will suffice to show what an amount of money, and consequently trade, the town is los- ing in consequence of the inability to receive ships into dry-dock.</p>
        <p>The report of the Superintendent at the Licensing Sessions of Runcorn on Monday last, shows that we are on the mend as far as concerns habits of temper- ance. The news is very welcome indeed and the remarks passed by the Chairman of the justices were certainly called for. The decrease in drunkenness is chiefly attributed to the Manchester Ship Canal having been completed, but there is not the least doubt that the local temperance organizations have a good claim to some of the credit. Their ptoceed- ings of late have been ca rried on with considerable enthusiasm, and the supporters of the cause are obviously in high feather. If things continue in this groove the town will be able to throw off the title too often given it, of "Drunken Runcorn," and adopt a more elegant one, that of "Temperate Run- corn." May it soon be so.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Technical Institute is every week approaching nearer to completion. Recently the work has been carried on with some expedition and in a very short time the roof will be finished. The subscriptions towards the building fund are increas- ing and the committee only requ ire £25 to place them in a position to appeal to Mr. J. T. Brunner, ALP. for the division to fulfil his promise of a donation of £5OO. It is to be hoped that some one will soon come forward and deposit the required amount, so as to enable the committee to work with a freer hand.</p>
        <p>I The village of Penketh was en fife on Thursday on the occasion of the wedding of Mr. R. Posnezt, of Runcorn, and Miss Fanny Garnett, daughter of Mr. R. Garnett, J.P., of Hall Nook, Penketh. The wedding was fixed to take place at twelve o'clock, at the Penketh Wesleyan Chapel, but long before that time, Chapel-lane was crowded with the villagers eager to see the bride and bridegroom on their way to and from the chapel. Flags were flying at the residence of Mr. Garnett and various other places. The chapel was tastefully decorated for the occasion, with bamboo leaves, etc. around the pulpit and in front of the organ. The public were also very much en evidence in the chapel. Punctually at twelve o'clock the bridal party entered the chapel, the bride being preceded by the bridesmaids, Miss Hilda Garnett, Miss Margaret Posnett, and Miss Ellen Mary Parker, the latter carrying a gold satin cushion for the bride to kneel upon. The bride was dressed in white satin duchesse, with brocaded petticoat, and orange blossoms and veil. She was also wearing a circlet of pearl diamonds, a present from the bridegroom. The bridesmaids wore gold bracelets, and were dressed in eau-de-Neille aura, with hats to match. The bridegroom was accompanied by Mr. Ernest Walker, of Bolton, as best man. The officiating clergymen were the Rev. R. Posnett (father of the bridegroom), the Rev. J. Gunnell, and the Rev. J. Pusnett. Upon theentrance of the bridal party into the chapel, the choir sang " The voice that breathed o'er Eden," Mr. Wigfield presiding at the organ. After the ixteresting ceremony was over, the party left the sacred edifice, and in the lane the school children sang the National Anthem upon their making their appearance. The bride and bridegroom rode off to the residence of Mr. R. Garnett in an open landeau amid the cheers of the spectators, to which Mr. Posnett raised his hat in acknowledgement. The rest of the company also left the chapel in landaus, which were supplied by Mr. E. Connor, Sankey-street. On arriving at Hall Nook, a wedding breakfast was held at which there were present—the bride and bride- groom. Mr. and Mrs. R. Garnett, Rev. J. and Mrs. Posnett, Mr. E. Walker, Miss Hilda Garnett, Miss Mar- garet Posnett, Mr. and Mrs. David Garnett, Mr. Jenks, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Walker, Mr. and Mrs. Bradshaw, Mrs. Fildes, Mr. and Mrs. Wigfield. the Rev. J. and Mrs. Gunnell, Mr. P. Garnett. Mr. and Mrs. W. Garnett, Mrs. Coop, Mr. and Mrs. R. E. 'Garnett, Rev. and Mrs. R. Posnett, Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker, Mrs. Curwen, Mr. J. F. Curwen, Mr. and Mrs. C. R. Doeg, Miss M. J. Moses, Mr. Arthur Davison, Mr. W. Walker, Mr. L. Posnett, and Mr. and Mrs. W. Parker, Mr. C. Posnett. Miss Louie Posnett, Miss Annie Moses. The room was beautifully set out in a unique style, the table being surmounted with bamboo plants. During the afternoon another dinner was held at which a large number was present. The happy couple afterwards left Penketh en retail for the Isle of Wight and the Channel Islands.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the presents :—Silver candle- sticks, Mr. W. S. Walker; carriage clock, Mr. A. C. Walker; salad bowl, Mr. and Mrs. Riley ; silver egg boiler, Miss B. Dutton ; silver sugar sifter, Miss Jenks ; portmanteau, Mr. W. Walker; silver salt cellar,Mr. C.E. Walker; silver afternoon tea service, Mrs. Walker; silver tea service and tray, Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker; silver standard lamp. Mr. W. E. Walker; silver egg stand, Mr. Edward Posnett ; glass jar, Mr. J.G. Withinshaw ; bread trencher, Mr. S.FI. Holmes; volumes of poems, Mr. C. W. rosnett ; silver revolving dish, J. and H. Moses; vases, Mr. and Mrs. Parker; silver serviette rings, Dr. Murray ; silver inkstand, Mr. P. C. Garnett; large silver spoons, Miss C. Bush; silver tea spoons, Mrs. W. Walker; fish knives and forks, Mr. and Mrs. R. E. Garnett; fish carvers, Mr. and Mrs. Jenks; silver teapot, Mr. and Mrs. Bradshaw; bible and prayerbook, the Rev. R. Posnett ; candelabra, Mr. and Mrs. L.W. Posnett ; silver waiter,Mr. and Mrs. Wigield ; china tea service, Mrs. Coop; silver sugar basin, the Rev. J. and Mrs. Posnett ; silver sugar tongs, Miss Good ; blotter, Miss Fletcher ; preserve dish, Miss Davison; scent bottles. Sze., Miss Slater; silver rack and butter dish, Mr. and Mrs. Norman; china dishes, Nurse; china afternoon tea service, Mrs. Nicholson ; china bowl, Miss Moses (Carlisle) ; silver fruit spoons, Mr. and Mrs. E. Parker ; silver casket, Ruth Parker ; cushion H. Mary Parker; book, Mr. Pym ; box of soap, Airs. Tilley; table silver, Mr. and Mrs. D. Garnett; slippers, Mr. Roberta ; silver sugar sifter, Miss Hankin- son ; oxydized card tray, Mr. and Mrs. J. ' Dutton ; hand painted mats, Annie Hurter; teaspoons and sugar tongs, Mrs. Halton ; inkstand, Mr. A. McKecknie ; goat bell, Mr. and Mrs. T. Coop ; fruit spoon, Mrs. Fildes ; smelling bottle, Mrs. Dow ; silver water jug Mr. and Mrs. Rhodes; table, Mr. and Mrs. J. Gaudy; brass cqfee pat, Mr. and Mrs. Curwen; scent bottles. Miss E. Parker ; vase, Mr. and Mrs. Parker; cream jug, Rev. J. Gunnell; china vase, Mrs. and Misses Owen ; tablecloth, Miss Jessie James ; scent bottle, Mr. C. Gandy • thimbles, Mr. Bush ; glass bowl Mr. Bush; glass dishes, Miss Bush ; clock, Mr. Assindsr; picture, Mrs. W. Fletcher; pipe, Mr. Morrell ; clock, Mr. C. Miller ; tray cloth, Miss Brinslev ; book (Palestine), Mr. J. W. Garnett; letter rack and stamp box, Mrs. J. Owen ; silver bowl, Messrs. Eustance ; card case,Mrs. Norman ; silver cream jug and sugar tongs, Mr. and Mrs. Smith; mats, Miss Dobson; china vase, Dr. and Mrs. Wadsworth; china vase, Mr. and Mrs. Fairclough ; china afternoon tea set and tray, Mr. Gray; china vases Mrs. Ockleston ; print Sandhurst," Mr. Wilkinson and family ; clock and workbox, employees of Xlighfield tannery; picture, Mr. and Mrs. Harrison; cigars, Mr. Deynham ; tray cloth, Miss James; picture, Mr. and Mrs. Garnett; table vase, Mr. and Mrs. Carrick; ora- torios,Mr. and Mrs. Dear; gong,Mr. and Mrs. Pierpoint; silver biscuit canister, Dr. and Mrs. Hunter; bowl, Lily Buckley; scent bottle,Miss Nicholson; book, Miss Lomas; picture. Mr. Fildes; table centre and mats, Miss Briscoe; two pictures, Mr. L. Henshaw • China card dish, Miss Bland; silver cati,llesticks,Mr. and Mrs. Cathcart Smith; table centre. Miss Alice Gill; mate. Mrs. Gunnell; mats, Miss Livingston; silver cheese and butter dish,Mr. Arthur Davison; silver bread .fork and knife rests, Mr. Gordon, Parker; vase, Mr. and Mrs. Fitton; silver fruit knives, Mrs. Posnett ; canary in cage, Mr. Sam Howard; silver lamp, Mr. Kenyon; inkstand. Mr. and Mrs. D McKechnie ; silver preserve dish, Mr. and Mrs. It. Cook; book (Ruskin), Miss Moses; book (Tennyson). Mrs. Raper; sugar tones, Miss Raper; china bowl. Dr. and Mrs. Hellon ; glass jug and two glasses, Mrs. E. Walker.</p>
        <p>(BY A CORRESPONDENT.)</p>
        <p>In August of last year, when I had a week's holiday, I set out from home to nowhere in particu- lar and I at length found myself in Rotterdam and The Hague in Holland. I was only in Holland two days, but those two days were sufficient to make me, like Oliver Twist, wish for more. I therefore deter- mined that in the following year I should spend the whole of the week on the continent. The oppor- tunity came on Saturday, August 11th, when a friend and myself left Warrington by the C. L. C. railway to Grimsby en route for Rotterdam. We had ; carefully mapped out our route before setting out so that we should have a definite plan before us. The plan was a very ambitious one for one week, and we were told by many that it would be impos- sible to accomplish it. Whether or no we did, the following account will show. Suffice it to say that the route we had chosen was as follows :—From Warrington to Grimsby by the C. L. C., thence to Rotterdam by the M. S. and L. steamer, then from Rotterdam to Cologne by rail and a sail from Cologne to Coblentz up the Rhine. This we intended to be the Ultima Thule of our journey. We intended to return to England from Coblentz via. Cologne, Aix-la-Chapelle, Brussels—with a visit to the battlefield of Waterloo thrown in--and Antwerp where we would again take the steamer to Grimsby and thence home again. A rather formidable programme without doubt.</p>
        <p>Afi I said itbo-ve, we left Warrington by thE C.L.C. at 2.17 p.m. on Saturday, and arrived at Grimsby at eight e-clock—the train being an hour and a half late! As we still had three hours to spare before the Rotterdam steamer—the Retford—set sail, we took the opportunity of paying a visit to the editor of the "Grimsby Independent," who, it may be of interest to state, was formerly a pressman in War- rington. In company with this gentleman, we had a walk round the docks, and were regaled with the strong smell of fish for which Grimsby is noted. When we returned, we found it was time to go aboard. We had been informed when booking at Warrington,that we should probably have no berths on the Retford, owing to the large number of pas- sengers who were going with her. On getting aboard, we found that our worst fears were realised and that we should have to sail across the North Sea, without the luxury of a berth, and that if we wished to rest, we must lie down on forms, or on deck, or wherever we could find a convenient place. But crowded as was our 'steamer. the Antwerp steamer was much more so, and I sincerely pity the poor passengers who sailed by that boat. We set sail at eleven o'clock, and after clearing the docks, we speedily passed the Spurn Head, and got into the open sea. Everyone now tried to make himself comfortable for the night and get as much sleep as possible, which In many cases was however very little. Towards morning, the sea became much more rough, and the much dreaded mal-de-tner at once made its appear- ance. As I have not been attacked with this com- plaint, I cannot speak from experience as to the feelings of the sufferers, but if their feelings are anything like their looks, they must be bad indeed, for they look the picture of misery,and some of the worst of the afflicted ones do not care whether the boat goes to the bottom of the sea or not. They have also a very decided objection to having their meals and as a result, there were very few who took their places at the table at dinner time. Those who did so however, had an excellent repast, and the steward and stewardess deserve every praise for the manner in which they looked after the wants of the passengers. The voyage to Rotterdam occupied seventeen hours, and on the way glimpses were to be seen of the Norfolk coast. Cromer a fashionable watering place can be seen quite dis- tinctly, and just above the horizon, Yarmouth can also be seen. The -greater part of the journey is however out of sight of land, although a large num- of fishing smacks and steamers can be seen. The first, land on the Continent to be seen is the beach of Scheveningen, the Brighton of holland. It was off this coast where the redoebtable Dutch admiral, Von Tromp, fought his last, battle, and died whilst fighting the British. The Hague, the seat of the Dutch government is soon afterwards in sight and theo the Hoek or Hook of Holland, where the Harwich steamers disembark their passengers, is passed, after which the remainder of the voyage is accomplished on the calm waters of the Maas. The Maas is a very wide estuary formed by the junction of the Meuse with one of the mouths tif the Rhine. The sail from the Hoek to Rotterdam,occu- pies about an hour and a half,and it is whilst, going up the Maas that the first distinct view of Holland is obtained. The south bank is very monotonous and uninteresting the whole of the distance, but on the north bank there are numerous villages. The houses of these villages are very different frim English cottages, and have not so substantial a look with them. A railway runs along the bank, and the voyager is at once struck with the fact that in- stead of dropping the signals to give notice of the approach of a train, they are risen almost perpen- dicular. The engines are very much of the same pattern as the Ship Canal engines, and the rate of travelling is much slower than in England. Just before arriving at Rotterdam, the town of Schiedam is passed. It is said that in this town there are more pigs than human beings, which, however, I think is a little exaggeration. It is also the seat of the manufacture of the famous Hollands gin, and in that place, the price of this liquor is not very much more than" pop" is in England. Immedi- ately after passing Schiedain, the shipping and houses of Rotterdam come in sight, and the Ret- ford finally came to R standstill at the quay, which is known as the Boompjes. Here the custom officers examined the luggage, but we were fortun- ate in passing without even the opening of our bags, and were the first, to leave the steamer, and put our feet on Dutch soil. We lied not intended to stay in Rotterdam, hut to go straight on to Cologne. We discovered, however, that we were just too late for the last train that night, and there- fore took lodgings at the Queen's Hotel. As it was only five o'clock we set out to have a look round the city. Like orthodox Englishmen.as it was Sunday, we made up our minds to go to the Cathedral for service, hut we were astounded to he told that it was not open, but the circus was ! We had thus an early proof that the people on the con- tinent have not a very reverential feeling for the Sabbath. We declined to go to the circus, but had a stroll round the city instead. Rotterdam is built upon a large number of islands, which form a por- tion of the delta of the Rhine and as a result there are at least three or four bridges to be seen at every turn. Almost all the streets have canals passing through them. Some of the bridges are very large, especially the one over which the North Holland railway is laiti. As we could not, go into the Cathe- dral, we had a look at, the outside. It is a very fine building indeed, hut was put, into the shade by other buildings we saw during the week. The post- office is also an imposing structure. The country houses just outside are simply magnificent, both in architecture and in the way their gardens are s et out. There are several things which at once strike the foreigner upon walking through a Dutch town. Instead of keeping to the left side of the road, the vehicles and trains keep to the right, which must be very embarassing to an English cyclist. Dogs are also largely used as beasts of burden. In the quieter streets of the town,chiefly in the residential quarters, the inhabitants are very fond of watching the passers by. They have accordingly put mirrors outside their windows, in order to see who is com- ing along the street before he passes the house. Blinds ore also put, at the outside of the windows instead of the inside. I don't think they dare do that, in England. There are also numerous play- grounds about, the town where the children can enjoy themselves to their heart's content. War- rington could copy this with advantage. We had no time to see the Zoological Gardens, as we left on Monday morning at 930 a.m. We were early astir on Monday morning,and then commenced our long journey to Cologne, Köln, Coeln, or Koehn, as it is variously spelt. The journey took ns right across Holland. We could not speak Dutch, and so could not converse with our fellow- pissengers, hut they all bowed to us with great dignity on leaving the carriage. For miles after i leaving Rotterdam the country is below the level of the sea, and dykes are very abundant, and some of the arms of the sea which we crossed were of enormous width. We passed the town of Breda after ahonz a hoofs ride. This town is of moderate size, and is chiefly noted as the place where a fam- ous treaty which put an end to a devastating war in the last, century, was signed. East Holisnd is v.ry uninteresting. The soil is very poor, and we rode for almost two hours through a district from which nothing is ohtained hut turf. One of the turf stations rejoieed in the name Amerika. Just before reaching Venloo, the last station in Holland, the country improves, end the valley of the Meuse I is very pretty. we had to change and enter the Gerrcan thin at Venleo, and at the next Vatic",</p>
        <p>Kalderkirchen had to submit fora second time to the custom officers. Nothing contraband having been found upon us we were allowed to proceed on our journey. We had to change again at Kempen, but fortunately this was the last change. By the way, I almost forgot to mention the illusage our tickets received on the way from Rotterdam. Be- fore we reached our journey's end, no less than six pieces had been taken out of them, and I verily be- lieve that if we had gone much further than Cologne we should not have had any tickets left to give up to the ticket collector.</p>
        <p>Cologne station is perhaps the first in Europe. It is not of course the largest, but the work which has been bestowed upon it is marvellous. Imme- diately on leaving the station, we found ourselves in front of the Cathedral, one of the wonders of the world. The size of it simply appalling, and it well deserves the claim of being the finest Gothic building in the world. However we had not the time just then to admire the Cathedral, but must secure lodgings. This we did, and a better place I do not wish to find than the Hotel drei Ronigen, on the left bank of the Rhine, whence the traffic on the fine stream which is the pride of Germany, can be seen. After. tea we had a stroll through the city. A description of Cologne would be too long for an article of this kind, and I will therefore content myself with say- ing that a visit would repay anyone, for the city abounds in fine buildings and picturesque surround- lags. Ido not agree at all with Tom Hood who described it as a " city of smells." It has perhaps improved since he was there. We postponed a full examination of the Cathedral until we returned from Coblentz. In the evening, there was a full moon, and we had a walk along the banks of the Rhine. The scene was the most romantic that ever I have seen, for the scenery on both sides of the river is very beautiful. We kept along the bank for some distance, when we were rudely disturbed by hearing a strong gutteral voice telling us in German that we must not go that way. The voice proceeded from a policeman, who like all other con- tinental policemen, carried a sword, and we there- fore preferred to give him a wide berth, and entered the city again. Now that it was night, we found plenty of drinking going on, but no one seemed to get drunk. I suppose the beer is not so strong there as in England. Almost every shop sold beer, so that the temperance party are not of much weight there. We returned to our hotel at ten o'clock, and at once went to rest. Next morning we left Cologne by the 8.45 boat to Coblentz in order to have one of the much talked of sails up the Rhine. Unfortunately the weather was very cold and it was also very dull the whole of the journey, but it was a splendid sail nevertheless. We passed numerous places of interest to students of legendary lore • Rolaudaeck was one of these places with the island opposite upon which Roland's lady love pined away in a monastery. The Siebenburger or Seven Mountains are also of very great legendary interest. One of these mountains is the famous Drachenfels or Dragon Rock, and the statue to the dragon can still be seen. The well-known university town of Bonn is also passed, end the university and various other public buildings can be seen distinctly from the steamer. The scenery on both sides of the river is enchanting. Vines are growing along the hill sides, and reaching almost down to the water. Every now and again, an old castle can be seen, or the portion which has withstood the storms of centuries. Picturesque villages dot the hanks, and the river is also dotted with numerous beautiful islands Hear and there a stream as large as the Mersey joins its waters to Father Rhine, which is itself a mighty river. Dinner was partaken of on board the steamer, and a grand affair it was. We never inquired what the various courses contained, however, for we did not relish the thought of being told that we were masticating horses or other such objectionable diet. Whether this was the case or not I don't know, but we agreed that ignorance was bliss, and this was a case where it was folly to be wise We reached Coblentz at three o'clock. It is at this town where "the blue waters of the lovely Moselle, join the waters of Father Rhine." CoblEntz is a garrison town, and the military formed a large proportion of its inhabitants. On the opposite side of the Rhine is the fortress of Ehrenbreitstein, the "Gibraltar of the Rhine," said to be impregnable, and judging from its appearance I should think it was. While here, we saw an enormous raft of trees from the Black Forest pass down the Rhine. It requires about 40 men to steer these raftt, as they simply go by the force of the stream. After visiting the fortifications, and various places of interest, we left Coblentz for Cologue and had the luxury of a sail by moonlight. An idea of the force of the current of the Rhine can be obtained, when it is stated that whereas it took us six hours and a quarter to go up stream to Coblentz, it only took us four hours and a half to return. It was very late when we ar- rived in Cologne, and we at once went to bed. Next morning, we had a walk round the fortifications of Cologne, and on the way passed numerous interest- ing places, such as the city theatre, the court house, the market, etc. Towards noon we ar- rived at the Cathedral, and were fortunate in being present at a service, and seeing the Arch- bishop. This prelate bad a walk round the church, and one could then see who were Protestants and who were Catholics. As he passed the Catholics I bowed to the ground, while the Protestants remained I erect. The two creeds were pretty equally divided. The exterior of the building is a masterpiece of architecture. There are two spires each about 540 feet high, and lanother about 350 feet. The nave and main portion of ' the Cathedral are also an enormous height, and the building towers high above everything else in the city. , There are also in addition numerous turrets, and figures carved all over it. In fact there are over 700 figures each a yard high about the building. The in- terior is quite in keeping with the exterior. Upon enter- ing, the visitor feels small indeed for the roof towers at an enormous height above him. Pillars in the Gothic style run the whole length of the interior, and every- thing is made up on a gigantic scale. After the service was over we meunted the spire. This is a tremendous task, as there are 518 steps to climb, and then we only get within about 220 feet from the top. At this height, the whole of Cologne can be seen and the course of the Rhine can be traced for miles, past Dusseldorf on one side, and Bonn on the other. After taking a good view from the top, we descended about half way, and then walked round the top of the nave and had thus an opportunity of admiring the numerous stained glass windows, the altar, and other interesting portions of the building. After leaving the Cathedral, we had another walk through the city, and visited several places, after which we left for Brussels. On the way we passed through the district where the manufactures of iron and chemicals are carried on. Some of these places would ahnost rival Widnes for their perfumes. Aix-la-Chapelle, which is very popular with Queen Victoria, is on the border of this district. This town is a very clean one, however, and there is some beautiful scenery aroend. The railway past here) goes through the northern spurs of the Ardennes, and the scenery is extremely beautiful, consisting of alternate hill and dale, with here and there a rippling brook. The Belgium frontier is crossed soon after leaving Aix-la-Chapelle, and our luggage was examined at Vevier, where we also had to change. Our luggage was not so innocent now as it was in Holland and Germany, for we had purchased 12 bottles of eau-de-Cologne at Cologne, and baying the whole of the twelve bottles in one parcel, we forgot to divide them until it was too late, and I had to pass the whole twelve bottles myself. The officer scratched his head and stared at it for some time, but as he could not talk English, and I could not talk French, I suppose he thought it would be too much trouble to explain, and at length he let me go. In get- ting into the train again we met two Lancashire men from Colne in Lancashire, and as the only other twn pas- sengers were a French man and a young French lady, we had a lively time of it. We might have been old frioads the way we behaved,and I am .raid we gave the French passengers an idea that the Enelish were a noisy crew.</p>
        <p>_ We arrived at Brussels at ten o'clock at night, and on going out of the station we were quite dazzled at the scene before us.' Lights of all kinds, but chiefly electric, were shining in every direction, and the square in front of the station was filled with a noisy lot of people. We obtained lodgings at the English hotel, and the next morning we felt as though we would like to have the hairdresser's hands upon us. We acuardingly entered the shop of a coiffeur and were motioned—theY could not tell us—to take a seat. The lather I am sure was iced it was so cold, and they simply put it on with- out rubbing it in after the fashion of English barbers. After the shaving was complete. some eau-de-Portuy al was applied, and then something else—l don't know whst it was.—They then put about six or seven differ- ent fluids upon our hair and then waxed it tia keep it down ; in fact we went in Englishmen and came out Frenchmen. For this transformation a franc was required. After the performance of this operation, we made our way to the Midi station to take train to Brain de l'alleud in order to see the field of Waterloo. On the way we passed the Hotel de Vine with its enor- mous spire. This is the building in which the Duke of Wellington was present at a. hall when the news of the advance of the French army was broueht to him. The Bourse is close by. and is a fine building with stone columns in front. Arrived at Midi station, we were soon on the way to the famous battle-field. The railway journey occupies about half an hour and is through a beautiful country. The mound which was thrown un with the lion at the top, ran be saen some time before the train arrives at Brai'. do l'alleud station, and is never out of sight when walking towarda it, so that there is no need for a ("nide. When we left the station. we wer- surrounded by men " genuine" oak sticks made at Waterloo—so they said—bat most. probably they were made at Birmingham. After running the ea untlet, of the stick sellers, 7e had next to face the guider. " Take you to the field for a frtne each," said one. We shook our beads. "Well a fvr the two of you." till we</p>
        <p>shook our heads. "Well then, for half a franc." We were still inexorable, and our tormentors left us. We did not see the fun of paying anything to be shown to a place which we could see straight in front of us. When we got to the battle-field, we at once ascended the mound, which necessitated upwards of 220 steps being climbed, but when the top was reached,a fine view of the country round could be obtained. Mont St. Jean, the headquarters of the English army, Le aeye Sainte and Hougoumont, scenes of terrific struggles, La Belle Albance,where Napoleon was during the battle,and where afterwards Blucher and Wellington met, could all be seen. We afterwards visited each of these places he detail. roe place has now a very peaceful look, and it requires some effort of the imagination to picture a san- guinary battle having taken place here. On the top of the mound is a lion looking towards France, and show- defia.uce to that country. While on the top, we had a most interesting sketch of the battle by an English officer who keeps the Museum Hotel at the bottoms. We spent about eight tours in Waterloo, and while near La Haye Sainte we were caught by a thunderstorm, so that we could hear the thunder of the elements, if not the thunder of guns. We returned to Brussels in the after- noon,and the evening we spent in a concert ball. Every- thing was of course in French so that we were not much wiser when we came out. The next day (Friday) we, spent in a last look round Brussels, when we saw the Botanical gardens, the Royal Palace, and the Reyal Park besides les, important places. The guards at the Royal Palace are about the shabbiest looking soldiers that I have ever seen. Our impression of Brussels was that it is a very fine town indeed, fully equal to its reputation, but we did not care for the people at all. Tney appeared to be a very under-handed lot. Before leaving Brussels for Antwerp, we had an opportunity of repaying a grudge to a waiter at the hotel we were staying at. It appears a law had been passed whereby after Aegust Ist of this year, certain money is not of legal tender. There is a lot of this bad money about, and the waiter in question had passed one of these coins in change to us. It is very difficult for a foreigner to distinguish them. We found it out soon after receiving it however. When the time came to leave, our waiter found plenty of work to do close by us. He was of course waiting for his" tip." We therefore bad our revenge upen him by tipping him with the bad money he had given us. He looked daggers at us but did not say anythine. He saw that he was paid in his own coin in a very literal sense. We left Brussela on Friday afternoon, and arrived at Antwerp about four o'clock. We obtained ledgings at the Hotel d' Auvers, and then went off to the quest to obtain a berth for the voyage on the following Saturday night. Again fortune was against us for every berth had been spoken forond another voyage like the last was before us. Friday evening we spent in tee exhibision,which is a very fine one indeed. Enough has been written about this, so that it need not he described here. While we were on the grounds we visited Captain Boyton'e famous aquatic troupetand a very pleasant evening was spent. The next day being our last on the continent, after visiting the Cathedral—which is no doubt a noble building, but much dwarfed by Cologne Cathedral—and the Zoological Gardens, we packed up our trappings and took them aboard the " Ashton " which was to be our dwelling place for 22 hours. We then had a laid look round the city, and at six o'clock in the even- ing went aboard ourselves. The Scheldt was at that time at full tide, and at half past seven o'clock we set sail for Old England again. We arrived at Griznsby after a very cold voyage a litee after four o'clock on Sunday afternoon, where we had to ply duty on our Eau-dt-Cologne. This was too bad, and altogether they were more strict here than at any of the oth,r three custom stations we had passed. However we bottled our rage bemuse we were compelled to, and catching the excursion train,we arrived in Warringt-n at 10.30, after a most interesting, instructive, and pleasant weex's holi- day. Had the weather been more tavourable we should without doubt have had even a still better tour. Last year when I was at Rotterdam, the cholera had made its appearance in the city. The consequence was that our names and addresses were taken on the reture jeurneV, and the medical officers of the various towns advised,t and the next morning after my arrival, I received a visi from Dr. Gornall. This time, there was no cholera at the ports but there were several cases of cholera at Cologne while we were there, yet our name were never taken. Of course we have not had any signs of cholera upon us, but it does not appear to matter much so long as the cholera does not come to the ports.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. —Before Messrs. A. Thomas, W. Mortimer, and T. Rigby. CHARGE• OF UNLAWFUL WOUNDING. —James Corker, labourer, Was charged in custody on remand with biting and wounding John Ray, of Frodsham Lordship, on the 21st July.—Mr. Frank Turner, solicitor, of Chester, pro- secuted,and Mr. A. Linaker, solicitor, Runcorn, appeared for the prisoner.—Mr. Turner, in prosecuting, stated that he had been usable to get the doctor there to give evidence that day, so that under the circumstances he would agree to the charge being reduced to one of assault. On the day mentioned above the two men were workine, together on the canal at Eastham discharging timber and in the evening they met at the Commercial Hotel, Frodsham. They had previously been on the best of terms, but though this was the case when Corker met Ray coming out of a "convenience" outside the hotel he threatened to strike him. Ray replied that he had better not, whereupon Corker caught hold of him and brutally assaulted him. Ray was found in an unconscious state in the "convenience" and carried in the Commercial Hotel by John Brown and Margaret Caldwell. In addi- tion to a wound on the hip there was one on the top of his head. His face was bathed and Dr. Selby was summoned. Ray was incapacitated from employment for a week.— Complainant bore out his solicitor's statement, and in answer to Mr. Linaker said he had never employed the defendant to do any work. Neither had be boasted on the day of the occurrence that he could fight any two men. The quarrel was not owing to any difference between them.—Margaret Caldwell, servant at the Commercial Hotel, testified to seeing the complainant, Ray, brought from the out-building and to assisting to carry him into the hotel. He was bleeding from the head and face and she noticed several teeth marks about his hip.—The man Brown did not appear to give evidence.— John Taylor, in giving evidence for the defence, said that on the night alluded to he saw Ray go into the outhouse followed by Corker who said "Jack what do you mean." Ray did not reply. They both then "pitched into one another for life and death." Witness was glad to get outside of the "convenience" but stood at the entrance. It was fair fighting and he did not see Corker bite Ray or use a belt.—P.C. Price stated that the defendant was of a quiet disposition.—Mr. Linaker having addressed the bench at some length on behalf of his client, the de- fendant was fined 5. and £1 ss. costs or an alternative of one month in gaol. DRUNK AND DISORMIRLT .—William Davies, of the Brow. Frodshain Bridge, was fined ss. including costs for being drunk and disorderly on the 18th August at Frodshain Bridge. UNVACC Jones,of Saltport, was sum- moned for neglecting to have his child,Rose Ellen Jones. born on the 22nd January, 1894, vaccinated in a=ord- ance with the Vaccination Ant, 1867. The offence hav- ing been proved, it was stated in defence that defendant was not opposed to vaccination. He had been to doctors and ordered them to call and vaccinate the child, but they had called whilst he and his wife had been away from home. The magistrates adjourned the case for a month, to allow the detendaut to comply with the law.</p>
        <p>TOOK. THE LAW INTO LIN OWN HANDS.—WIII. Roberts, Woodhouse, Frodsham Bridge, was fined ss. and Bs. 6d. costs for assaulting Thomas Ainsworth, of Frodsham Lordship, on the Ist August. The complainant stated that defendant met him and struck him in the mouth. The defendant pleaded that the complainant had pro- voked him by his conduct towards his son. UNSUSTAINID CHARGE OF ASSAII LT.—Emma Ainsworth, of Bradley,was summoned by Eliza Ainswarth,of Kings- ley, for assaulting her on the 22nd August. Complainant stated that the defendant entered her house and struck her in the mouth. She also used threatening language towards her. Defendant denied the offence, and the ease was dismissed, the magistrates thinking that the affair was too trivial for a Police Cond.</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT TOPER.—Charles Miller,of Frodsham Bridge. was summoned for being violent and refusing to quit the licensed premises of Joseph Faulkner, at Frodsham Bridge, on the 18th August. The circumstances were related by Mrs. Faulkner, and the charge having been proved, defendant was tined 53. and 84. 6,1. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>BOWLING MA.Tol.—On Wednesday afternoon on the green of the Norton Arms Hotel, a bowling match was completed between T. Dean, of Runcorn, and Fred Hay- wood. of Halton. The first portion of the comp,tition had been played on the green of the Bridge Hotel, Run- corn, on Monday afternoon, and the two players, wh." are well-known iu Runcorn and district, met on Wednes- day afternoon to play off the final. The venue was in splendid condition, and some good play was witnessed. Ultimately the game resulted in a win for the Runcorn- ian, 'l'. Dean, the respective scores being 82 to 55. The prize awarded to the cham pion was a cup, valued £4,</p>
        <p>FAREWELL OF TIIE REV. JAMES IJANBY.—On Sunday evening the Rev. James Hanby, who for the last three rears has been the pastor of the Wesleyan Church,Halton, bade farewell to his conaregation in an eloquent sermon, the last of which was taken from I. Thessalonians. V. 25, " Brethren pray for us." Both the Rev. J. and Mrs. Hanby have been the recipients of several ha fi,lsorno presents in commemoration of tlriir connection with Halton.</p>
        <p>At a largely attended meeting in Hyde on Wednesday, organized by the local. branch of the Independent Labour Party and presided over by Mr. Tom Mann, Mr. C. S. Christie, of Nottingham was chosen as the Independent I,:Louz cAuclidate .or iz. IJth Fr diviiQc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>PRHBENTATION TO TUB DRIDN AND BRIDRGIZOOIt.</head>
        <p>PRHBENTATION TO TUB DRIDN AND BRIDRGIZOOIt.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. 13. Posnett, manager of the Higbfield Tannery Company, Runcorn, and Miss Fanny Garnett, his bride- elect, were each the recipient of a present on Friday evening (last week) at Runcorn. The employ&amp; of the Tannery Company all assembled on the evening men- tioned in the warehouse, where on behalf of the work- peeple, Mr. Randeil, foreman, presented a lady's com- panion to Miss Garnett,and a beautifully designed marble timepiece to. Mr. Posnett on the occassien of their coming marriage. Mr. Randell,in performing the cere- mony, delivered a fitting address, in the course of which he alluded to the kind manner in which Mr. Posnett had always dealt with them,and the respect in which he was held by the men. The presents were but small tokens of the esteem in which the employei held their manager and his prospective bride: and they hoped that both would ever be the possessors of happiness and prospe- rity. (Applause.)—Mr. Posnett, in accepting the gifts on behalf of himself and Miss Garnett,thanked them for their kind expression of appreciation, and assured them that their presents would ever be very highly prized. He hoped that the good feeling which had always existed between employers and employed would never die out,but that as time rolled on they would be brought into still closer union, one with the other. He again thanked them all very much.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SCOTCH STRIKE.</head>
        <p>THE SCOTCH STRIKE.</p>
        <p>The result of the ballot among the Scotch miners WaS declared on Thursday. There were 25,417 votes in fav- our of the National Miners' Federation proposal that the men should submit to a reduction of sixpence in their wages and return to work. The counter-proposal that the pitmen should remain on strike till the shilling advance was conceded obtained 20,942 votes. Inasmuch as there is a majority of 4,475 votes for the Federation suggestion, it is hoped that the dispute will soon termin- ate. A telegram from Glasgow states, however, that the masters are unable to agree ta the Federation offer because the coal contracts usually made in the autumn have teen placed with English coal-owners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>EXCURSION TO CHESTER. -On IVeinesday morning the members of the Weston Church Bible Class,numbe,r- ing w-arly fifty, had their annual picnic, the place se- lected being Chester. They were conveyed thither in vehicles provided by Messrs. Yarwood, a start being made shortly before nine o'clock. The Rev. W.H. Stables accompanied them, and after an enjoyable ride the an- cient city was reached at half-past eleven o'cl,4clc. The remainder of the day was spent in a variety of ways, some went for a sail on the Dee ; whilst others visited the Sports.which were in full swing after dinner. Most of the historic parts of the old city were also eagerly sought out and inspected by the visitors, who altogether spent a thoroughly errioyable day. The return journey was begun at 7.15 and completed at 10.30.</p>
        <p>The most wretched man on earth is probably a monarch, Norodom, • King of Cambodia. He has a gorgeous palace furnished according to the most expen- sive ideas. but he adheres to the customs of his ance,tors, and sleeps on an ancient carpet in a kind of shed that has not been cleaned since the creation. He is a miserable victim to hypochondria, and all day long he heaver sighs of utter avretchednoss. This monarch is a short, fat person with one eye. A disastrous fire f,ccurred iii ManchPster on Wednes- day evening. 'Dr: premises of Messrs. Meyerhof, Marx, and Simonsen. velvet and veivet:ren manufacturers Dickinson-street, Portland-street, were burned out, and the fire extended to soveral warehouses adjoining. A vast amount of valuable merchandise was destroyed. The origin of the fire which was burning till nearly mid- L.:. act !;Cef.</p>
      </div>
