      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ANNUAL BREWSTER SESSIONS. FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL BREWSTER SESSIONS. FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning the annual licensing session for the Frodsham district of the Eddisbury Divlsion wag held in the Frodsham Police Court, the magistrates being Messrs. A. Thomas, W. Mortirw.r and T. Rigby. ANNUAL REPORT. Superintendent Johnson presented his annual report which showed that there were, in the Frodshain section of the division, 17 licensed victuallers, 8 beer-sellers,and one off license, making a total of 26, which gave an average of 258 of the population to each license. No licensed victualler had be•mn proceeded against during the year, the guile being the case in the proceeding year. One beer-seller had been summoned and con- victed, being an increase of one in the previous twelve months. 46 persons had been proceeded against for , drunkenness during the year, 45 of whom had peen con- convicted,as compared with 43 persons proceeded against land 39 convicted in the previous year, showing an., increase of 3 proceeded against and 6 convicted. From the list it appeared that Joseph Faulkner, of the Weaver Tavern, Frodsham, had been fined 40s. and 16s. 6d. costa for permitting drunkenness. Continuing, the Superin- tendent stated that the justices were going to serve notice of objection with reference to the case of Faulkner and it would be beard at Oakmere on the 2411 Septem- ber. With regard to the Bridge Inn it had been sug- gested to adjourn the application for renewal until the license could be made out in the name of the new tenant.</p>
        <p>The Chairman remarked that he could not understand- why the Weaver Tavern case should be adjourned to- Oakmere which was eight miles away. Surriy If the Frodsham magistrates were able to hear applications for licences, they ought to be competent to deal with the Weaver lavern.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ford who represented Mr. Trafford, the clerk to the licensing magistrates for the Eddisbury division,ex- plained that the applications for the renewal of al/ liceaces in the division were previously heard at Otic- mere, then adjourned to Frodsham and Over, and then back again to Oakmere on September 24th. The justices would doubtless have adjourned the one in question to Frodshain but for the tact that there was not time to give the necessary seven days' rmtice of objection. The Clerk (Mr. Ashton) thought they should deal with their own licences.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ford suggested that the suprintendent should give his notices of objection earlier in future so as to avoid delay. The Chairman considered that there should be some alteration.</p>
        <p>Superinteodeat Johnson remarked that he could not give his notices of objection until he received instructions from thr bench.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rigby observed that if the feeling of the Frodshatn Magistrates was conveyed to those of Oak were perhaps the latter would adjqurn the case ta FrodAiain. Legiti- mately it should b.: heard at Frodsham but the notice had not been served sufficiently early. The Clerk said that if their worships at Oakrnere could see their way to adjourn it to Frodsham it should be done.</p>
        <p>The licenses were then all renewed with the exception. of the two named.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ST. HELENS</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS</p>
        <p>A PRESCOT FATLIIRE.</p>
        <p>A receiving order was made on Tuesday by Me. Registrar Cooper, in the Liverpool Bankruptcy Court, against Edward Murphy, of Eceleston-street, and Market-place, Preseot, grocer and provision dealer. The order was made on a creditor's petition, presented by Messrs. Symond and Elwards, solicitors.</p>
        <p>A BLACKSMITH DROWNED. At the Sr. Helens Town Hall, on Tuesday afternoon, Mr. Brighouse, county coroner• held an ingnest on the body of James Lawton, a bl4cksmith, uho was drowned in the cAnal 'm Sunday. The evidence showed that deceased had been atidic!ed to drink, but his relatives had no reason to thirdy that ho would commit suicide. An open verdict of s• Found dr)wned " was returned.</p>
        <p>TRE COLLAPSE OF A RAILWAY TUNNEL. • The Cropper's Hill tunnel, on the London. and Nc)rth- West,ri liatkvas-• at St. Helens, v.hih rollap t... 11 about thn e months since, has now heen rep,ired and made perfectl.), secure. the bole has bepn iilled up, and all that remains to be done is the. rebuilding of the out- houses and walls of the yard which gave way when the tunnel COP.:aped. NEW COUNTY MAGISTRATES FOR ST. HELENS. It is stated that inforation has been received in St. Helens that the Chancellor of the Duchy is prepared to add the naims of Alderman M`lfechnie and Councillors J. Forster and J. Beecham, of S. Helens, to the county magistracy list.. Several other names have also been mentioned. Alderman M'Kectinie, who is an ex-Mayor : r of St. Helens is a retired alkali manufacturer and metal extractor, and in politics a Liberal Unionist Councillo J. Firster is an engineer, and Liberal candidate-elect for St. Helens; and Councillor lifecham Li a Pill manufac- turer and a Conservative.</p>
        <p>DAMAGING TUE NEW RAILWAY. At St. Helens. on Monday, Mary Roberts. Eiz.-ibeth Roberts. Alice Branton,Mary 0' foole.Catherine Alice Fazakerley, Jane Stott, and Ellen Stoti., all living in the neighbourhood of Merton Bank-road, were summoned for damaging the embankment of the new St. Helens railway. Mr. Swift, who prosecuted on be- half of Mr. Pilling, said practically a. wall of stone had been built for sleepers to be laid down ; but the stones were carried away for use as rubbing stones. Damage to the extent of £5 or £lO had been dune during several - days preceding those on which the defendants were caught. The stone "vas. epyirried at Ashton purposely for the woik. Defendants were each fined 6. /d.,incluci- ing costs and damage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
