      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>VALUABLE DISCOVERY.</head>
        <p>A VALUABLE DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>Most readers have had some experience of sick rooms, either as patients or attendants. How m iny such are there who have not felt the weakness or dhilitY which follows the sickness, more discouraging than even the disease itself ? Though the disease may have given way before the timely application of proper remedies, and though, in consequence, no immediate danger is to be apprehended, the slow progress towards convalescence is very disheartening to patient and attendant alike. How often do we hear people say :—" Yes. The fever has left him. He is free from an: immediate danger. But he is so weak. If he could only regain strength !" This debilitating effect of fevers and other diseases is indeed a great source of anxiety to medical men as well as to th&amp;r patients ; and anything which would tend directly to shorten the period between the departure of the fever and the complete recovery of strength would be naturally welcomed by both doctors and patients.</p>
        <p>This is one of the reascns why Gwylym Evans' Quinine Bitters are so popular,why doctors prescribe !t so largely, why chemists sell it so ,generally, why patients have recourse to it so readilv, and why everybody has a good word to say for it. N.)t only -does it attack the disease in its stronghold, not only does it drive the evil from the system, but it gives bark directly. the strength of which the system has been deprived by sickne,s ; it infuses new life into the blood, and the recovery of health arid strength, instead of being, as it otherwise Would he, discouragingly slow, intermitt-nt, and uncer- tain, becomes, when this excellent medicine is resorted to, encouragingly rapid, reguli'r,•and certain. It is, therefore, confidently recommended as an unrivalled tonic medicine,and may be safely used by all who suffer from any form of weakness or debility from whatever cause it may arise.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Young, of 107; Laxton-st., New Brompton, Chatham, writing to a friend, says :</p>
        <p>"I thank you very- much for sending me the GWILYIII EveNs' Birrmas ' handbill-. I have taken two bottles, a Zs. 9J. and a 4s. 6d. It Alas done me more good than anything I had ever toed before, and, I am so pleased with it that I shall recommend it to -every one svho comes to see me." , • . GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS, PRAISED BY EVERY BopY. AGEN 1:6 EVERY-, , Sold in BAttles at 1/4,42/9, arid 4/6 Beware of Imitations! Sae the , ot4. " G.WIL.IOI • EVANS " on Label Stamp and pane.. - SOLE PROPRIETORS QUININE BITTERS NIANUFACTURIVGI CO., LTD., • LLANELLY, SOU 111 .WALES.</p>
        <p>2, Wharf-street, discharged pith a caution ; Jams Leonardy, labourer, 18, Haydock-street, on Pinner's Brow, on the 25th ult., and also on the 18th ult., 23. 6d. and costs in each case ; Robert Dakin (76th appearance) at Market-gate, on the 25th lilt., ss. ; Etizabeth Ryan, Dolman's-lane, in Dolman's-lane on the 26th ult., 53. and costs ; Kate Ann Jones, 8, Ashton-street, on the 18th ult. in Winwick-street, ss. and costs ; Jana Tickle, 3, 'fun-ter-street, on the 18th ult. in Turner-street, ss. and costs ; Catherine Maguire, 2, Ikrry's-yard, on the 19th ult., in NVinwick-street, 2s. 6]. CHIMNEY ON FIRE.-Forqhis offence in Bridge-street, on the 22nd ult.. Catherine Ann Stirrup was fined a shilling. , MAINTENANCE ORDER.-Samuel Smith was ordered to contribute is. 61. per'week towards the maintenance of a son on the ship 1' Clio." TUESDAY.-Before Mr. R. Garnet:: (in the chair) and CoUncillor H. Roberts. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.-Jane Quinn for being drunk in Scotland-road en Monday night was fined ss. and• coists ; Henry Aspinall drunk and disorderly in Dial- street, on the same date, 53. and costs. Mary Garritty for the same offence committed this morning (Tuesday) was fined 103. and costs or 1.11. days. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASES.-Mr. G. Riley prosecuted in the following cases e,F persons summoned for the nen- attendance of their children at school :-Edmund Pye, bricksetter, Cartwright-street, 53.; Mary Bennett, lodg- house keeper, Pierpoint-st., 53.; William Burton, forge-, man, Edward-street, 5.3.•' John Delooze, chimney-sweep- Silver Square, 23. 6d.; James Garse filecutter, Porter- street, 2s. 6d. ; Joseph Green, forgeman, Winwick-road, 2s. 6d. ; John Holloway, forgeman, Kerfoot-street, 2s. 6d.; Walter Lund, forgeman Bewsey-road, 23. 6J. ; Patrick Kilmarten, labourer, Old-road, 23. 6d.; William Wright, baker, Old-road, !2s. 6. ; Leah Briars, char- woman, William-street, 23. 6d. ; Frederick Beachill, forgeman, Bank Quay, WEDNESDAY.-Before Messrs. John Fairclough (in the chair), J. Geddes and J. H. Fleming. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.-Martha Walsh, LFthgOeS- lane, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street, on Tuesday night. She was fined 23. 6. and costs. NEWSPAPER THEFT.-Thomas Knight was charged with stealing from Bank Quay station on Tuesday morn- ing, three copies of the "Liverpool Courier," and three Of tile " Liverpool Daily Post," the property of John Thomas Daniels, newsagent, ankev-street. The papers were valued at 6d. Mr. Hunt (chief constable), stated that ‘,the prisoner was aged 11 years and the son of 4ohn*Knight, Silver Square. On Tuesday the prisoner went tank Quay station at half-past seven in the I morning and asked a porter for Mr. Daniels' papers. The porter gave prisoner two parcels addressed to Mr. Daniels. Soon after, an inquiry was made for the same papers. The matter was reported to D.S. Monoghan who apprehended the prisoner at 11.30 the same day in Museum-street. D.S. Monoghan trought him to Bank quay, station, where he was identified as the lad who came for Mr. Daniels' papers. When charged with the thef the prisoner replied tint he had only sold two of the papers and had left the others at a shop in Bewsev- street.-Allan Moore, the porter,corroborated Mr. Hunt's stlteinerit-Th4 prisoner's father said that his son was l(exhilf atveriarrol.lf-flere considerable amusement was createel.byr the prisoner saying to the magistrates in a tearful voice-' ) I'll be satisfied if you'll send me away." He as remanded till Friday so that arrangements wiul!! be made for his being sent to an industrial school. TAITELSDAY.-Before Alderman T. Sutton (in the chair) and Mr. J. H. Fleming. ,DAUNKENNESS.-Ann Dolson, a stranger in the town, was pharged with committing this offence in Key Fold at n3,idnight on Wednesday. It was stated that defend- ant hadlwerii, quarrelling with her children, who reside in Warrington. .he was bound over to keep the peace in her own redognizances.</p>
        <p>'NEWTON PEITY SESSIONS. ,SSATURD'A. Y. —,Before Captain Withington (chairman), Messrs. T. J. Gillespie, R. Stone, W.Mitchell, and T.D. Grinike Drayton. DRUNKENNESS. —The following were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs for drunkenness—Daniel Green, Richard Mell- ing, William Okell, Thomas Carlin, and Joseph Dearden, all of Newton. John Navin of Golborne was discharged. ' UNMUZZLED Doos.—The following were each fined 7s. 6d. for allowing their dogs to be at large unmuzzled : —Samuel Faircloug,h, Burtonwo,d ; John Slee, Newton ; Simon Hazelton, Newton ; and Henry Fowler, Newton. R.S.P.C.A. CASES.—Samuel Bennett, of Ashton, was charged with using obscene language in Newton on August 13, and was further charged with travelling a horse while in an unfit state on the same day. Mr. Stuart dafended. The evidence for the prosecution was that Inspectors Thorne and Luff,R.S.P.C.k. were on duty at Newton on the day in question, and they noticed a blacit horse tied up outside the Oak Tree Inn. It ap- peared to be very lame, and upon examining it they foupd it suffering from ringbone, and the coronet was also very tender. Upon making inquiries, they found that it belonged to the defendant. When asked to give his name and address, he told them Ashton but P.C. Dixon who. was also present said that he did not think that was the right address,whereuponthe defendant used She language complained of.—Mr, Stuart said that the language which defendant used was not nearly so bad ,as was stated by the officer, and defendant was natur- 'aljy irritated when the officer doubted his word. He did not deny that the animal was brought from Ashton to Newton, hut it was only suffering from temporary lame- ness owing to being "caught " by another horse. It had nothing else the matter with it except sidebone. The temporary lameness could not have been serious inas- much as the horse had been sold for £7, and no coin- plaints had since been received from the purchaser.— Mr. Stuart called several .witnesses to support his etate- ment, and the teench decided to dismiss the charge of cruelty, but fined defendant 2s. 61. and costs for using obscene language. John Berry, St. Helens, was charged with causing a horse to be travelled while in an unfit st4te on August 13, and l'hnmae Stubs was charged with travelling the horse. In4pector Luff in proving the case, said that the tent4ons of one leg were severely strained. and the horse was practically walking on three legs. It had been brought to the Newton Horse Fair. P.S. Longworth cor- roborated the inspector and the defendant Berry was fined 104. and costs and Stubs 24. 61. includihg (:0•47.5. Patrick Harrington was fined 54. and costs for work- ing his horse while in an unfit state. Inspector Luff and Longworth stated that the defendant had brought the horse to Newton on August 13, and it was lame owing to the hock being inflamed. It was unfit for work. Henry Tranter was fined 10s. and costs for causing his horse to be travelled in Newton on August 13. Inspector Thorne and P.C. Dixon proved the case. BREACH OF THE PEACE . —TWO hr-there Frank and Joseph Rowley, were each bound over to keep the peace for three months for fighting in Golborne on August 18. IT WAS NOT A POKER. —Bridget Maddock was fined 10s. and costs for assaulting Elizabeth Allen on August 18. The two women are neighbours, anti reside in New- ton. The evidence of Mrs. Allen was to the effect that she went to the defendant to complain of her conduct whereupon the defendant gave her a blow in the face with the poker.—A witness called by Mrs. Maddock said that it was not a Piker that was used but only an iron rod. (Mr. Gillespie: "What is a poker but an iron rodr). The witness further said that the defendant only "tapped" the complainant in the face with the iron rod., The "tapping" was evidently not of a very mild charac- ter, for the cemplainant's face was a pitiful object, and the magistrates expressed their sense of the tapping by inflicting the above fine. TURNIP STEALING—WiIIiam Bibby, Winwick-road, Warrington, was fined 2s. 6d. and costs for stealing tur- nips from the field of William Manning, Orford, on the 18th inst. Mr. Manning said that he had lost a consider- able number of turnips recently. AN INCORRIGIBLE LITTLE THIEF. —A girl named Mary Elizabeth Stockton, of Newton, was charged with steal- ing a coat from the shop of Richard Burtley, High-street Newton, on the Bth inst., also anottier coat on the 9th inst. from the same shop, and a pair ef hoots the pro- perty of William Hill, Market-street, Earlestown on the 13th inst. The prisone/ had been brought before the magistrates three times previously, and had been dealt with under the First Offenders Act, but the bench con- sidered that they could not let the preseet case pass over in a similar manner, and they ordered her removal to a reformatory for three years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRANES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRANES.</p>
        <p>A LUNATIC AT LA/IC. At the Warrington Borough Court, on Monday, a middle-aged man named Thomas Newton, was charged with being found wandering at large in Buttermarket- street at ten minutes to three that morning; suprOscli be of unsound mind.—Sergeant Cheney "'stated that at the time named he met the poisoner who was mutterFngo to himself. He thought it advisable-tevilsektonrup. Orr examining his clothing he found the. Prestwich Asjolum stamp en his shirt and waistcnat. Fleortold witness that-trt' got away the previous morning:—Mr. Hunt saidlthat they had communicated with the authorities at Prest- wich Asylum, and they stated that an officer would be sent for the prisoner. Thel magistrates accordingly remanded the prisoner to await an officer from the asylum.</p>
        <p>A POACHER IN TROUBLE. On Monday morning at the Warrington Borough Court, Michael Burns. labourer' 6, Carlisle-street, was charged with having on the 20th August, wheh searched by P.C. Turner, had upon him nets, two pegs, and two,l rabbits. Defendant pleaded guilty. P.C. Turner stated that at 4.30 a.m. on the date mentioned, he was near the Boat House, Wilderspool Causeway, when he saw the defendant in company w ith another man. On seeing witeffs they ran in cliff, rent directions. He gave chase to the defendant and after running about 150 yards he caught him. He found upon him the nets and pegs pro- duced, and also two rabbits which were warm at the time. He took the defendant to the Police Station, and got his name and address. He was then allowed to go home. The rabbits were sold by Inspector Spinks. The defendant stated that he was out of work at the time and wanted to earn a shilling or two. The magistrates recorded a conviction against him, and forfeited the nets, which were stated to be worth about £2. THE BANK NOTE THEFT. At the Borough Police Court, on Tuesday, Peter Higgins, who was remanded the Tuesday before, was , again charged with having stolen four £5 notes, a ' sovereign, and three gold studs, the property of Mr. Hampson, ex-Conservative agent, 11, Academy- street.. The evidence was similar to that given in our columns the previous week. There were severitt witnesses present, hut they were not called upon to give evidence. It transpired that the note found at iflam hal been sold by the prisoner t 6 a farmer there for £2, and who had immediately cashed it for £5. The fourth, note was in the possession of Annie Ryan, otie of tht womerein company with Mr. Flamponn and the prisoner at the time the notes were lost. Ryan cannot be found. Only four notes out of the. Six lost "have been found a?td traced to the prisoner. Two of them he had placed in charge of a waitress at a restaurant in Deansgete, Manchester, from whom they were recovered' by Detec- tive Harris, of the Manchester police. Prisoner had been convicted for felony once before. After a short consultation the magistrates sentenced the prisoner to three months imprisonment with hard labour. THEFT OF A SHIRT. On Wednesday, at the Police Court, Martin Hart was charged with stealing a man's shirt from his mother's house on Tuesday.—Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—Mr. e Hunt stated that the comp'ainarft was Mary Hart, the mother nf the prisoner, and rcsiding at 14, Friars Green. She took in lodgers and washing. The shirt in que- tion belonged to one of themelarnes Fitzpatrick. On the theft being discovered information was aiven to D.S. Monaghan who upon making enquiries (Es- .. covered that the shirt had been pawned at Mrs. Leslie's shop in Mersey-street, by a man named John Maerath.. Monaghan met the prisoner in Mersey. street, drunk, and charged him with the theft. The prisoner said his mother gave him the shirt on Tuesday. He sent Magrath with it to pawn because he wanted a drink.—Mrs. Hart stated that the shirt belonged to Jemes Fitzpatrick, one of her lodgers. She said that on the day in question she gave the prisoner a shirt to put on but the one produced was not it. The shirt was safe on Sunday. Mrs. Leslie said that on the clay named'a man brought the shirt into her shop and asked a shilline for it. The prisoner was not the man. D.S. Monaghan deposed that on Tuesday night at about six o'clock Mrs. Hart came to the police station and reported the theft. He corroboneed Mr. Hunt's state- ment as to the subsequent recovery of the shirt and the prisoner's arrest. The magistrates committed the prisoner to goal for one month's hard labour. THEFT OF A BICYCLE.</p>
        <p>At the Bnroueh Police Court on Wednesday. Arthur Hankinson was charged with stealing from the stable of Frederick Pendlebury, 10 Winwick-street on the 12!-h August a bicycle and a key value £5. Tile prisoner plEaded guilty to the charge. Mr. Hunt stated that the complainant wag George Gould, the manager of the furniture shop, 10 VVinwick-street. Defendant was aged 17, and resided at 25 Ashton-street. He was engaged on the 2nd August to look after a horse for Mr. Pendlebury, and left on Saturday night. On Monday, Mr. Gould went into the stable at the back of the shop, where his machine was kept,and found that it had disappeared. He immeniately sent the errand lad to the prisoners resid- ence to a.k him about it. The lad did so and the defen- dant replied that it was all right, and he was going to see Blr.Gould. This he did not do, so Mr. Gould reported the matter to Mr. Pendlebury who sent word to the police. Sergeant Rowbotham. in consequence of the information he received, went to 25 Ashton-street and saw the defen- dant who told him that he did not know anything about the ticycle. The sergeant then saw the defendants mother who stated that there was a bicycle at the house of Samuel Webster, brother-in-law to the defendant, Bostock-street. Rowbotham again saw the defendant who told elm that the machineat Bostock-street!was the one in qustion, and was identified as Mr. Goulds. The key was also found, Sergeant Rowbotham brought the defendant to the police station, where he con- fessed that he took the key from the desk in Mr. Gould's office.—Mr. Hankin.son the father of the defendant said, that his son told him that Mr. Geuld had sold the machine to him. Defendant had also told Mr. Webster Bnstock-street, that he had bought the machine Jut of his wages. In consideration of his youth the magistrates agreed not to committ defendant, but fined him 255. including costs.</p>
      </div>
