      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN PETIY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETIY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Beforeiessrs. James Handley (in the chair), Samuel Taylor, and Thomas Handley. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Jane Oxen, of Massey's- court, was fined 2s. 6d. including costs for being drunk and dissrderly on the 18th August in Massey's-court.— Catherine Craig, of Mersey-square, was charged with a like offence on the same date and near her own house. The evidence in support of the charge having been taken, defendant was discharged on promising a refor• melon in her conduct.—Bridget McMullen, of Liver- pool was charged with a similar offence in High-street on the 25th August but discharged on promising to leave the town. OssrnucTromsTs. —Four youths named Ernest Nicholls, John Thomas Limb (Lock-street), William Jones, and William Haddock, were summoned for obstructing the footpath in High-street, Runcorn, on Sunday night, the 19th inst.—P.C. Booth stated that at about 6.15 in the evening on the date named he was on duty in High-street and saw the four defendants stand- ling on the footpath opposite the offices of Mr. Knight in such an obstructive manner as to force persons going to divine service to go into the road in order to pass them. When they saw witness they dispersed, but as soon as he had turned his back they returned to their former position and continued the nuisance. Upein seeing them commit the nuisance a second time the con- stable took their names and informed them that he intended to report the offence. Upon being questioned</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
