      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 1, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 1, 1894.</p>
        <p>the officer stated that the defendants loitered on the footpath for quite fifteen ininutes-.—P.S. Len and P.C. Richardson corroborared the testimony of P.C. Booth.— Superintendent Atherton remarked that the offence was very aggravating. It was very unpleasant for people going to church or chapel to be compelled to walk in the road especially on rainy nights. He 'had received complaints from Mr. Hazlehurst with reference to the nuisance created by youths in the locality. Their con- duct on some occasions was extremely disagreeable, not- withstanding that they had often been warned by the police.—The magistrates agreed that the offence was very annoyine to pedestrians, and in piew of the age of the defendants a tine of 61. and 9s. 6d. costs would be imposed upon each of them. In default of payment they would be committed for seven days to prison. A PUBLICAN AND HIS DOG.—Peter Coyle; landlord of the Weaver Hotel, Weston Point, was summoned for , ,llowing his dog to be at large, unmuzzlecl and un- collared on the 21st August at Weston Point.—The de- fendant admitted the offence stating that another person ha, without; his (defendant's) knowledge, permitted the animal to stray out of the yard.—Superindendent Atherton alluded to a dog which had recently been caught by the police in Widnes and shot owing to its suffering from rabies. The animal had no mark upon it by which its owner could be identified. The magis- trates would therefore perceive the danger of allowing dogs to wander in this manner.—Defendant was fined is. and 83. 6d. costs. WARRINGTON LURRYMEN AND THEIR HORSES.—TWP men engaged as carters by Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co., brewers, Wilderspool, and named William Broadhurst, Laurence's Brow and Henry Harmer, Chester-road were summoned for obstructing High- street, Runcorn, by leaving *;heir lurries—to each of which two horses were attached—for an unreasonable time with no one in charge.—P.S. Lee testified to seeing the horses standing outside of a public house opposite Messrs. Unsworth's shop in High-street for fully twenty minutes without anyone in charge of the anlini.ls. When about to visit the Bull's Head in search of the drivars they came out of the bar of that public house. The men had apparently finished their work when first witness saw the lurries as the vehicles were laden with empty barrels.—The defendants denied being away from their horses the length of time stated by the officer, and further said that when they were seen coming from the bar of the public house, they had just completed their business there. They were not allowed to have anyone to take Charge of their horses whilst doing their work inside houses.—Mr. Handley remarked that the pro- prietors ought to provide assistance for their men when they were compelled to remain so long absent from their horses.—The Bench requested Superintendent Atherton to represent to Messrs. Greenall that their lurrymen had no assistance on this occasion but were forced to do their work and took after the horses at the same time, a state of things which should nor exist. In view of this fact the magistrates dealt as leniently as possible with the defendants and fined them ss. and the costs 9s. 6d.</p>
        <p>HAD TO STEAL TO GET FOOD.—An old man namel Thomas O'Niel was brought up in custody on remand and charged with having stolen 641bs of iron value ls.3d. from the works of the Manchester Ship Canal Co., at Runcorn on the 23rd inst.—P.C. Ridgway deposed to seeing the prisoner in Leinster Gardens on the day named with a bag upon his back. The contents of the Lag appeared to be very heavy, and cmsequently wit- ness stopped him and enquired the nature of the load- Prisoner replied that it was rags and bones that he was carting mixed with a few pieces of old iron which he Lad got from kt bat. Witness inspected the iron and taxed the prisoner with having taken it from the Ship Canal. Finally, lie admitted that he had done so.—ln answer to the magistrates prisoner said he was sorry for what he had done, but he was very hungry at the time and took the iron in order that he might get some bread.—Superintendent Atherton said the prisoner was a hard-working and respectable old man. He had been out of work for twelve months and was not at all of a drunken nature. He the superintendent believed that the old man had taken the iron really for food.—The Bench bound him over in a surety of £5 to come up for judgement when called upon.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. W. Long (chairman), and Alder-</p>
        <p>man Niela.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DlsoftnEray.—Thomas Hart was fined 103. and costs for being drunk and disorderly on the 26th May at Appleton. —John Conway, Warrington, was fined ss. including costs for a similar offence at Appleton on the 27th August. OCCASIONAL LICENSE.—Mr. Speakman was granted an occasional license to sell wines and spirits at Wilderspool on eptember Ist on the occasion of the Warrington Elorse Show.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—William Bailey was charged with having on the 21st of August at Dutton cruelly ill- treated a horse by working it in an unfit state. A con- stable stationed at Dutton deposed that on the date named he met the defendant on the canal bank at Dutton in charge of a horse attached to two narrow bats be- longing to Messrs. Simpson Davies and Company, Run- corn, contairng 60 tons of stone. He examined the horse and found a large wound under the collar about three inches long, and about three-quarteas of an inch deep. There was a considerable amount of matter on the collar, and the horse was in very poor condition. A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed. _ . _</p>
        <p>Doci AT LARGE—For allowing his dog to be at large untnuzzled on the 10th August at Walton, Wm. Berkett was fined 23. 6d. P.C. Shatwell proved the case.</p>
        <p>A POACHER FlNED.—lsaac Smith, Warrington, was charged with having at Walton Superior committed certain trespass by entering land in possession of Sir Gilbert Greenall in search of game, without permission to do so. Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, Warrington, prose- cuted on behalf of Sir Gilbert Greenall. He stated that the defendant was a Warrington man. On the 22nd of July, John Nam, gamekeeper in the employ of Sir Gilbert Greenall, concealed hims-elf in a corn field, and saw the defendant and another man go into the fields almost opposite Messrs. Monk's woiks. On getting into the third field they saw a hare and were about to catch it with a " fly net " when Mani shouted to them. The two men then ran away but he shouted to the defendant " I know you Smith, you have no need to run away."—John Irlam, corroborated Mr. Browne's statement.—Mr. Bailey, head gamekeeper stated that the defendant had no right there.—Mr. Jones, gamekeeper for Sir Richard Brooke, stated that he had chased the defendant from land in the possession of his employer. For the defence the de- fendant called Alfred Groves, glass blower, 48, Lowe- street, St. Helens, who stated that on the 21st and 22nd of July the defendant wl,s at St. Helens.—Gamekeeper Ham identified the witness as the one in company with the defendant on the date named. The magistrates fined the defendant 10s. and costs. - -</p>
        <p>THEFT OF TURNIPS. —Two lads named Alfred Clare and Jack Taylor, of Warriagton, were charged with having on the 6th August, at Walton, destroyed certain roots growing on land in the possession of Mr. Weir. The complainant stated that he was at Lower Walton on the date named, and caught the two defendants pull- ing turnips up on his land. Both lads had turnips in their pockets. They were swede turnips, and about 20 yards square of growing turnips had been pulled up by lads. He did not wish to press the case against the. de- fendants. The magistrates warned them and dismissed them on payment of costs.</p>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE R PILLS.</p>
        <p>Cure Torpid Liver, Bile, Sallow Complexion, and Sick Headaches promptly: and curb them so as to stay cured. Chemists, is. lid.</p>
        <p>CHICKET.</p>
        <p>• GEO. G. BUSSEY &amp; CO-. are the leading makers of requisites for Football, IL ckey, Golf, Baseball, Gymnasia, and all Sports and Games. 1111ustrated catalogues may be obtained from the local dealers, or the Sports Manufactory, Peckham, London. Trade &lt;CCCEt Mark.</p>
