      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TELE MURDEROUS OUTRAGE ON A WIFE</head>
        <p>TELE MURDEROUS OUTRAGE ON A WIFE</p>
        <p>AT PRESCOT.</p>
        <p>COMMITTAL OF THE ACCUSED.</p>
        <p>Thomas Jones, 34. collier, residing in Moss-street, Prescot, was brought up at the Prescot Police Court, on Monday, before Mr. J. Birch all, charged with unlawfully wounding his wife, Rose Jones, by cutting her on the chin with a table knife on Saturday last. Rose Jrimes deposed that she was the wife of the pri- soner, and lived at No. 5 house, I court, Moss-street, Eccleston. About half-past one o'clock on Saturday afternoon prisoner came home. He had had some drink. Fie accused her of going with other men, and struck her about the face threv or four times with his fist. She screamed, and he then seized her by the throat, threw her down, and nearly choked her. He then fastened the front door with his pick, and took the knife off the table, saying, "I will finish you before morn- ing. I would kill you now if Willie (meaning the eldest child) was in." She then screamed again, and he cut at her throat, under the chin, which bled very much. Her screams brought the neighbours to the door hut they could not get in. She ran upstairs and called for help thrcugh the window. Dr. Barlow said he was called to the Police Station,and there saw the complainant, who was suffering from a recently-inflicted wound under the chin, an inch and a half long, which cut through to the bone. There were abrasions on the throat, and her face was covered with contusions.</p>
        <p>Police-sergeant Walker said he apprehended the pri- soner, and on charging him with unlawfully wounding his wife with a table knife he said, "I never did it." Me knife he found covered with rags under the window in prisoner's house. The prisoner was committed for trial at the next Liverpool Assizes.</p>
        <p>--.....--.</p>
        <p>At Elinburgh on Tuesday Ernest Hassberger, jute merchant, of Dundee, was Fentenced to eight years' penal servitude for forging bills to the amount of £112.000.</p>
      </div>
