        <p>" PRESIDENT'S DAY."</p>
        <p>CYCLISTS TRIP TO CRANAGE.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday was the annual "President's Day of the Warrington Cycling Club. Once a year the president of the calla and vice-president are taken by bus' to some country place, escorted by the cyclists on their machines.- On Saturday the Presi- dent (Ir. T. Morris, F.G.S.), eccompaniea by Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town c'erk), T. Birtles (photographer), Ed. Pickavance (sub-captain), Garnett (secretary), J. Dooley and a few others, proceeded by bus to Cranage. The party started from the Norton Arms Hotel at 2.15 and were followed by the cyclists shortly after. An hour and a half's pleasant drive 'brought the party to Knuts- I ford where the thirsty members alighted for refreshments. A fresh start was made at 4 o'clock and the whole troop numbering 63 arrived at the Swan Farm, their destina- tion, Cranage, at 5 o'clock. BaTore tea the party were formed into a group and Mr. Birtles,after two ineffectual attempts, owing to the hilarious conduct of some of the members, succeeded in taking a photograph. A very substantial meat tea was then partaken of in a laree room at the farm. During tea a telegram from Mr. 11. Piervoint, M.P.. who was in London, was read, in which he expressed his disappointment at not being present and also wished the trip every success. Mr. William Pierpoint and Mr. Percy Davies were also unable to be present. After the inner man had been satisfied all adjourned to another room where a social evening was spent. Songs of a humorous char- acter were rendered by Messrs. W. Whittle, (of the Marquis of Granby), J. Whitham and J. Dooley, Mr. T. Morris called on Mr. Birtles to propose the health of the c,7clists. Mr. Birtles in doing so said that this was the first time he had met so many cyclists and he was very pleased to propose their health. Be thought they showed good judgment in coming to such a pretty place as Cranage where they were so well looked after by their host and his family. It was his hope that prosperity would attend the club and its president, whom he had known many years and who, he hoped, would live to be president of the club, for many years to come.—Mr. J. L. Whittle endorsed the last speaker's remarks about Mr. Morris and had very great pleasure in seconding his proposition. He thanked the members of the club for their kind invitation and hoped that this would not be his last meeting with them. Many years ago he was a member of the club and rode one of those machines now know as "ordinary's" and every cyclists ambition then was to have the highest machine. His ambition was 52 inches. He hoped that the club would increase in numbers and long retain their present president.—Mr. Morris said that,in the name of the club, he begged to tender his thanks to Mr. Birtles and Mr. Whittle for their kind remarks about the club and him- self. Be thought that cycling had a good moralizing effect on young men. It kept them away from the publichouses in the town and from loafing about the streets. He had never seen a cyclist with too much drink. Instead of playing with their thumbs they could ride through miles of pleasant country and then come for tea to a fine place like Cranage. He knew of no better exercise or way of enjoying themselves than cycling. Seeing pictures, he thought, was nothing to the pretty scenes which they saw on their rides. Besides having a sobering influence cycling made them provident. To procure their machines they had to save up, and in getting them they gave employment to thousands of people who would otherwise be destitute. Altogether 29 different trades were upheld by the manufacture of the cycle. He would be pleased to be the president of the club as long as they thought as well of him as he thought of them.—Mr. Dooley made some reference to the absent members and said that the club was proud to have the company of Mr. Morris and friends.—The captain of the club, Mr. Davies, said that what the club was most in need of was a track to hold their races on. Many towns of less importance than Warrington had one.—About 8 o'clock the party said adieu to their entertainers and com- menced the return journey. On reaching Knutsford machines and conveyances were again stabled and the whole troop entered the " Red Cow" for the purpose of having another " social," at which songs were sung by Messrs. G. Smith, J. Whitham, Mounfield, J. Dooley and Wm. Whittle. A few remarks were made by Mr. Ed. Pickavance (sub-captain) in which he eulogised the ser- vices of Mr. Mores in connection with local athletics. Mr. Morris thanked Mr. Pickavance for his remarks, and also made a short speech. Several toasts wcre pro- posed and taken with musical honours. Mr. Garnett (the secretary) in responding to the toast of his health, said that he hoped that this would not be the last time by many that they have such an outing. The final start was made at ten o'clock, Warrington being reached soon after half-past eleven. On the return journey the cyclists kept behind the conveyance all the way, and with their lamps lit, made an exceedingly pretty scene. They were strung out like will-o-th-wisps for half a mile or more, and with their dancing lights they made an ever varying show. Whenever the waggonette slowed down in going up the hill, the wheelers passed on in front and then forming in two rows, one on each side of the road, they cheered the conveyance as it passed through. This is the first time that the club has been able to provide the members with a free tea, and judging by the success which has attended the venture, it is CO ,be hoped that it will not be the last. The waggonette 1, used was supplied by Mr. Connor, of Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>A. PRISONER RE-ARRESTED IN THE NEWTON POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>A. PRISONER RE-ARRESTED IN THE NEWTON POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>AN UNRULY DEFENDANT.</p>
        <p>A peculiar case occurred in the Newton Police Court on Saturday. The usual Petty Sessions were being held, and one of the persons tried was Michael McGrail, of Golborne, who was summoned for being too demonstrative after indulging in liquor on August 17th, at Golborne. During the time the police officer was giving his evidence, Michael kept continu- ally interrupting him and even went so far as to swear at him. The magistrates tried to put a stop to this kind of behaviour, whereupon the defendant treatA them also with choice oaths. This was too much, and the magistrates told him to stand down from the dock, and they then ordered an officer to re-arrest him and place him into the dock again. When this was done, McGrail was charged in addition with being drunk and dis- orderly and also with misbehaving himself in the court that morning. This did not appear to abash the prisoner in the least, for he expressed his opinion of the proceedings in a most profane manner. It was quite evident that he had had something stronger than water before he entered the court that morning. The bench determined that he should have an opportunity for reflection upon the evils of intemperance and sent him to gaol tor 14 days without giving nim the option of a fine.</p>
        <p>At the licensing sessions held at Ni olverhampton and Newcastle-under -Lyme on Tuesday the justices refused to renew six licenses. In one case the license had been in existence for two centuries.</p>
      </div>
