      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>DEATH OF THE REV. CANON HERON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV. CANON HERON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon Heron, who has resided at Moore for a considerable number of years,thed on Monday, at an early hour, at Moore Hall, at the age of nearly 90 years. He has not enjoyed the best of health for some years,but has been able to go about pretty frequently. About a year ago a return of his old malady, dropsy, confined him to the house. For some weeks his condi- tion was critical, but a vigorous constitution, careful musing, and the skilful treatment of Dr. White, of War• rington, enabled him to recover his strength somewhat, and he was afterwards taken about the village in a bath chair. The rev. Canon, who had outlived all his rela- tives, was unmarried. He was the son of General Peter Heron by his wife Caroline Hopwood, one ol the Hop- woods of Hopwood, and, therefore, allied with the an- cient Stanley family, the Earls of Derby. His grand- father was Peter GOER Heron, of Daresbury, sheriff of Cheshire in 1777, who married Rebecca Rutter, the daughter and heiress of Richard Rutter, the last of the line of Rutters of Moore, anciently the knightly family ot Le Roter of Thornton and Kingsley. It was by this marriage that the estate of Moore and Halton passed into the family of the Herons of Daresbury. In its turn and in the person of the deceased Canon Heron, the direct male line of the Heron family becomes extinct, the only brother of deceased, Harry Heron, a captain in the army, having also died childless. A sister of the deceased married Mr. John Smith Barry, of Marbury Hall, Cheshire, and became the mother of the present owner, Mr. J. Smith Barry, M.P. He was vicar of St. George's, Carrington, for a period of 40 years, during which time he was distinguished for the kindness and liberality which he displayed towards the parishioners, winning for himself the highest respect of all in the village. he took a great interest in the church, choir, and Sunday schools, which he liberally supported out of his private means ; indeed, the parish- ioners never knew the full extent of his genero- sity until after he had left the neighbourhood. Canon Heron was the founder of the friendly society known as "St. George's Guild," which is still in a flourishing condition, and the membership is large The guild is doing a great amount of good in the neighbourhood ,and it is seldom that the founder of such a society has lived to see such gratifying results attending his work. When vicar of the parish, Canon Heron was always ready to counsel and guide the young, and many men who now hold responsible positions owe their progress and advancement in life to his fostering care and influence. He was an honorary canon of Chester. Disconnection with the Warrington branch of the Eng- lish Church Union is well-known. It had, in fact, no warmer supporter, and the members of that body will hear of his de4th with deep regret. In 1891 he accepted the office of president, although he was, of course,unable to take any active part in the work of the Union. In other ways, however, he manifested his deep interest in it, and the compliment which was paid him by electing him president had been thoroughly earned by his ardu- ous labours during the earlier history of the union. The deceased was also one of the governors of the Warrington Grammar School. having always manifested considerable interest in educational matters. He had for many years lived a comparatively retired life. One of his oldest friends, the Rev. Dr. Durnford, Bishop of Chichester, a few years ago paid him a visit to Moore Hall, and although he was himself nearly 90 years of age, took advantage of the opportunity to visit the Ship Canal works at Acton Grange. His lordship, however, lost his way. and returned to Moore Hall quite exhausted after a walk of nine or ten miles. The Canon was very fond of relating this as well as other interesting incidents which had come under his observations during his long life. He was a highly educated man, and a good con- versationalist, and with a mind stored with a variety of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>LATHERS FREELY IN THE HARDEST WATER.</head>
