      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <p>useful knowledge, he never failed to interest as well a instru et his hearers. His last public appearance vvas a Moore on Thursday, February 26, when he opened the new Church Institute in that village. He was taken to the building in a bath chair, and delivered what cannot be regarded as other than an able and vigorous speech, when his great .age is taken into consideration. The evenerable Canon was very outspoken on tht drink ques- ti on. He had, he said, never been able to reconcile himself to the hard and fast lines of the teetotaller. •If a person found himself giving way to the bad habit of intemperance, he was as a Christian man bound toleep away from the drink, but in the case of other.. persong who could reasonably draw a distinction between the use of the thing and the abuse, he had never been able to find it in his heart to deny to the workingtnan the restorative pleasure and benefit of a glass of good matt iquor at the close of his day's work. Tnere were per lons who would exclude the very name, the very thing sut they had not done so in that Institute, because the? bad taken c% hat precautions they could to prevent 13aY hvils arising out of the use of drink. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PROFITABLE PETS.</head>
        <p>PROFITABLE PETS.</p>
        <p>PUSSY AS A LUCRATIVE INVESTMENT. In these days when most of us are ever seeking new ideas for the accumulation of that excellent thing which, paltry poets describe as paltry dross, a new idea is al- ways welc-.me, though one would scarcely expect the humb'e grimalkin to be a source of wealth: Neverthe- less, " Sylvia's J urnal," for this month shows us that nice little sums ale to be made out of the careful treat- ment and breeding of cats. One of the most successful amateur cat fanciers is Mrs. Buller, who has a " cattery " at her picturesque country licuse in Surrey. In reply to " Sylvia's " inter- • viewer Mrs. Buller showed that pussy can be made a profitable as well as an affectionate pet. " If properly managed," said Mrs. Buller, " cats can be made to pay well. A friend of mine made £240 IN TWO YEARS by prizes, sales, and stud fees. Of course, you must start with pedigree animals, and these are costly. The best plan is to choose one or two breeds, and try t° perfect them. lam endeavouring to produce unmarkell orange Persians,and am succeeding beyond my expectv tions. You shalbsee my lovely Marigold, with her superb • brush ; Cowslip, too, and Buttercup, for I name then 2, - all after golden flowers. They are worth a considerable, sum, orange Persian ktttens fetching tiiree guineas fo females, and four guineas for toms. Long-haired cats are the most popular and, in try opinion, the most a tractive. You must come and see ray pets, althoug they all appear at a great disadvantage just now. It is the cats' inAulting season, and every one of them is out of coat. Besides, a dreadful epidemic of influenza has been right throug,hmy"cattery,"and the poor things are, only just recovering. Some were so ill that my maid and I took it in turns to sit up all night with them. Then Zulu was introduced, a splendid fellow, whose tint to the initiated is light blue, but to the common ' mind more like state colour. Afterwards followed Migs Betty, a beautifully-marked tortoiseshell who took first prize at Croft's Aquarium Show, and a pair of royal cats of Siam, from whom great things are expected. "Of course," continued Mrs. Buller, "I have to take great care of my pets. THE SECRET OF SUCCESS is judicious feeding and an avoidance of coddling. 111/. • kittens spend the day in garden whenever the weather permits. The flower beds are covered with cocoanut fibre, in which they frolic about. You must never let Persians be out of doors in the wet. Their thick coati; ' will not dry easily, and they get chills and die. I lost a superb torn from that cause a little while ago." Another enthwiast in matters feline is Mrs. Herringi who has quite a little zoo, attached to her house in.lKent• Parallel with the residence rims the cattery, containina well-arranged pens of Match-board and wire, irreproach' ably clean and comfort able. "Not my ideal cattery." said Mrs. Herring. "A large , field with severathouses, so that the animals can be shifted from one to the other, is what I should like. "Here you see some of my prize-winners. Tba'' black, short-haired Malay cat is the famous Lady Curly' Tail. The black smoke cat near her is The Countess. sister to Candice, the Crystal Palace medal-winner last year, when The Countess was highly commended. The blue Persian over there won the challenge vase and medal, and this pretty little cat, Lady Jap, hails froin Chrysanthemum Land. You see, I give the ladies their freedom ; the gentlemen are kept in a separate catterY behind the rockery." Mrs. Herring ssems to regard thirty cats as a veri moderate number to have, and she advises us to keeP only a few if we would KALE A PROFIT. Then followed a dissertation on the various species of cats, which include among the short-haired tortoiseshell tabbies, ve hires, blacks, greys, and creams. Long-haired cats are the Persian, Angora, Indian, French, Russian. and Chinese. Other varieties are Siamese, Abyssinian. Malay, Japanese, and Manx cats. Some of these breeds are subdivided according to colour, especially Persian. The article continues : "I must show my lovely Siamese Queen Rhea, such a sweet and gentle creature, except when she has kittens, aud then woe betide any cat that comes near her." _ . _ _</p>
        <p>" Rolled in flannel Rhea's kittens were brQught down. mewing loudly with indignation, pretty little creamy' coloured creatures with chocolate ears and muzzles. Then Mrs. Herring's pet Persian, The Countess w 35 hurried out of the room before Rhea made her appearance in the arms of her proud mistress. " Of course you have heard of the great import&amp; Tiam. Qoeen Rhea is his daughter, the best Siamese female at Croft's show. Look at her beautiful blue eyeS• Those kittens are</p>
        <p>WORTH SIX GUINEAS EACH, so do you wonder I give them arid their mother a rooluo at the top of the house instead of a pen in the cattery ?,, The cat seems to be heir to as many ills as man, tul' cold is one of his greatest enemies—barring the foV terrier, of course. Pussy's lovers may be glad to knovi that the best treatment for cold is a drop of camP.b.° daily in half a teaspoonful of water. For bronchitis. Mrs. Herring prescribes, with delightful feminine vague- ness, a drop of aconite three times a day. I fear that aconite taken by the drop would cure feline colds alnaost as effectually as a fox-terrior.</p>
        <p>• BARBARIC CONDUCT OF GERMAN AUXILIARIES IN THE CAMEROONS. T. ALLEGED CANNIBALISM. The reports which have been received in Hamburg of the unsatisfactory state of affairs in the Germ-al settlernebV of Cameroons,West Africa, received confirmation by the last English mail arriving in Liverpool. The trooPs which the German Government have drafted into the Cameroons are mo,,tly Soudane,e, and are said to be ree° much more to be feared than the Dahomeyans who re-; volted last year. The natives in the place are in fear ot the men, and it is alleged that the sloudanese are carry matters with a very high hand, causing many distur- bances among the peacefully-disposed people of the place. As an illustration of their semi-wild state, it isf said that they have tried to buy the children of some the natives for chops ;" or, in other words, to eat. They offered is. or its equivalent for them, and wbe° the parents refused to sell, the Soudanese invariablY attacked them.</p>
        <p>j</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>NEW PRIVATE POST-CARD.</head>
        <p>THE NEW PRIVATE POST-CARD.</p>
        <p>It is now stated on authority that to-day (Saturdaici the public have the privilege of send through the inlan post, as post-cards, private cards bearing halfpeoal. adhesive stamps. The regulations provide that the ca,_ I.ru: must be composed of ordinary cardboard not thick than the material used for the official post-card. "`"L maximum size, having regard to the variety of for' must correspond as nearly as may be to the size of the ordinary inland post-card now in use. The minirnul size must not be less than 3iin. by 2±in., and cards rno9 not be folded. With regard to the address side,the rules differ very little from the rules which relate to the Pre; sent cards. But the modifications introduced in the ne":, warrant give rather more latitude than is permitted. I), the warrant which it supersedes. On the address Sicl.iej which must bear the postage stamp, the sender maYs,uo the words "immediate," " forward," " local," and al may attach by gum or paste a small label as at preseo Nothing else may be attached on the address side ogee% a postage stamp, and on the reverse side nothing buts° receipt stamp. The arrangements of course applY al to reply post-cards.</p>
        <p>Great heat prevails in Central Europe, and irell Austria-Hungary many casualities from sunstroke a reported. In Silesia there has been a cyclone. RUPTURE CURED WITHOUT OPERATION.—S. J. S. ' Hernia Specialist, 64, Chancery-Lane, London; 26, street, Maucheste ; Book on rupture and evil of 035— post tree 7d —A alt. The Earl of Albemarle, K.C.M.G., died on Tuesda • morning of paralysis, aged sixty-three. His success°' is his eldost son, Viscount Bury, M.P. for Birkenhesu where it vacancy will consequently be created.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
