        <head>PUBLIC-HOUSE EJECTION.</head>
        <p>A PUBLIC-HOUSE EJECTION.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS CHARGE.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening, the 17th, a man named Louis Napoleon Bonaparte Crowley. a musician and piano tuner, was found in 1131 d-st., Warrington,near the Woolpack, in an unconscious state. He was taken to ti.e Infirmary, but he never recovered Con- sciousness and died on Sunday. It was alleged that the man's injuries had been caused by his being forcibly ejected from the Woolpack. Mr. Brighouse opened the inquest lastMondayweek,but after formal evidence, the inquest was adjourned until the 24th, when the inquest was again opened. Herbert Honing worth, the lisensee, and Arthur Gleave, the barman, and Messrs. Peter Walker and Co. the owners were represented by Mr. Arthur Browne; Messrs. P. Walker and CO. were also represented by Mr. H. Greenall ; and the widow by Mr. Forshaw. Previous to the opening of the inquiry, it was dis- covered that the woman who had passed as deceased's wife was not sco, but that his wife lived in Birkenhead. The witness were all ordered out of court on the appli- cation of the jury. Aunie Crowley who was the first witness called, said she lived at 3, Brook-square, Birkenhead. Deceased was her husband and was a musician and pianoforte tuner. Her husband was 38 years of age. It was over two years since she saw him alive. She produced a photograph of her husband. She knew that deceased was living with another woman in Liverpool. She also produced a certificate of her marriage with deceased. She had not been allowed anything for two years by her husband% _ _ _</p>
        <p>MUSIC LICENSES IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>SEVERAL LICENSES REFUSED.</p>
        <p>Yesterday-week was the day appointed for gran- ing and renewing music licenses in Warrington. There were present on the Bench :—Mr. John Fair- dough (chairman), Alderman Wright, Councillor. 11. Roberts, Messrs. W. Pierpoint,J. W. Hayward, J. H. Fleming, R. J. Guest, and S. Wright. The CHAIRMAN asked the Chief Constable whether he objected to any of the existing license,, to which he replied that he did not. The Chairman said the magistrates made a recommendation the previous day that the Hop Pole music license `,e refused. Mr. Fowler • (Messrs. R. Davies and Co) said he appeared in support ,of the renewal of the music license of the Hop Pole. The Chairman: Are there any objections to the renewal of music licenses, with the exception of the Hop Pole ? There was no answer. Mr. Fowler said their worships were on the Bench the previous day and heard the evidence adduced against the Hop Pole license. They and the other magistrates after some discussion renewed the license but recommended the refusal of the music license. Nothing would have induced him to come there that morning to ask for the renewal of the music license but the fact that the landlord would suffer con- siderable loss, and without that license the house was worthless and might as well be shut up. Two large rooms were made in the place when it was built, for the purpose of concerts, and these weuld be useless without a music license. The words in toe song which it was alleged the previous day were sung at the time of the Rev. D. W. Roberts' visit, were harmless, as would be seen from the music he now produced. If the license was refused they might as well refuse the whole of the music licenses in Warrington. The Chairman said that he didn't see, without they had some very strong evidence, after the decision of the magistrates the pre- vious day, how they could do anything else but refuse it. Mr. Fowler said he didn't propose to adduce any evidence, but asked the magistrates to give him a two days' license for Wednesdays and Saturdays. He would call the pianist to prove that the song produced was the one sung.—Mr. Chapel, pianist at the Hop Pole, stated that he was in court the previous day and heard the evidence of Mr. Roberts with regard to a song sung. The song produced was the one, and it was a song that was sung in Music Halls. The words given the pre- vious day were not correct. The magistrates reserved their decision. Jas. BURRROW, landlord of the Queen's Hotel, Sankey- street, applied for a music license only. Mr. Hunt said he had no objectiois to raise. WILLIAM GREEN applied for a music license for the Shakespeare Inn. Mr. Hunt said the house in a great measure had been thoroughly cleaned and the sugges- tions made by the magistrates with regard to the exter- ior and the interior had been carried out to the ,letter. The licensee was a man of irreproachable character. He thought it was a case where a music license could be granted. JONATHAN BROADHURST applied for a music and dancing, license in Jones' Old Foundry. Mr. Hunt said the appli- cant appeared before them two years ago and he was then in concert with another man. The room in question was 58ft. long, 381 t. wide, and 6ft. high at the lowest point. The magistrates recommended alterations to the room, but none had been made. He, in company with Inspector Spinks visited the place and found it in a dilapidated condition. He opposed the application. The magistrates retired, and after a some deliberation returned, the Chairman stating that the following music licenses had been refused : John Allen, "Pelican "; Ann Balshaw, "White Bull"; E. A. Ccop, " Hop Pole " ; Jas. Burrow, "Queen's Hotel " ; Wm. Green," Shakespeare " ; and J. Broadhurst. The Chairman added that they had had a discussion about the music licenses, and it had been a very great question as to whether they should he refused wholesale. The magistrates felt that there was a state of things creeping in through these music licenses that had not been contemplated—these professionals being employed, and otherwise a state of things which they would not allow.</p>
        <p>Jane Christian Kenny said she lived at 27, Balckstone- street, Liverpool, along with deceased. She last saw him alive on Wednesday three-weeks between seven and eight o'clock in the morning. He was on a tour through several towns in search of employment. She was present at his death on the 19th inst. He made her promise never to tell anyone that he was not her husband. She had never gone through the form of marriage with deceased. David Hickman, compositer, 11, Haydock-street, said that on the Friday night he was coming upßold-street to- wards Sankey-street at about 11.2 when he saw three men appear at the doorway of the Woolpack Inn. Two of them appeared to be waiters but he could not identify them. The other man was the deceased who was in a helpless state from drink. He saw the men push the deceased out into the roadway with some force,from the top step of the house. He came "flying out" into the street, and fell on the back of hi ,s head. The two waiters immediately turned into the house and the door was thrown to. Witness went to the man whose head was in the gutter and his feet towards the door, and with the assistance of others the man was picked up and placed on the step. The man did not appear to bc violent or quarrelsome.—By Mr. Hunt : Deceased could not have simply fallen out with the force he came out.—By the Coroner : He was thrown out like a sack of coal, and he considered there was more force used than was necessary.—By Mr. Browne : Witness was 20 or 25 yards away when the incident occurred. The waters bent forward when they pushed the man out. Deceased was never kicked by the police, although someone said that he would report the police.—By the jury : Deceased was unconscious when witness got to him. Frank Garrett, manager for John Harrop, 122 Bridge-street, said that he was in Sankey-street on the Friday night a little after eleven. He was at the end of Golborne-street.. He had a view of the Woolpack entrance in Bold-street. He heard a scuffle and this .drew his attention,and he then saw three men,two with- out coats, and the other with a coat. The two men got hold of the other man and deliberately threw him into the street. He fell upon -his head, and witness drew the attention of Mr. Mawson by saying, " 0 my God, look how they are throwing that man out." He then went to the man's assistance and a policeman also came up, and deceased was lifted to the step. Dr. Braide was then sent for and ordered his removal to the Infirmary. sergeant Smith subsequently had a conversation with one of the men who threw the deceased out, and point- ing to Herbert Flollingworth, the licensee, he said that that was the man. He considered that more force was used than was necessary. John Mawson, tailor and draper, Sankey-street, stated that on the Friday night he was in Sankey-street at the time of the occurrence. He saw the deceased just as he was falling on the ground. He could not say whether' anyone used violence to him. He thought the man was killed at first. Thomas Clark, 27, Laira-st., said that he was in Bold- street on the Friday night. Be saw the deceased fall when he was in the middle of the street,oppo.site the Woolpack door. He did not see anyone push deceasedout, but he saw the doors closed by somebody. George Whalley, 28, Napier-st., said that on the Friday night, he was in company with the last witness. He first saw the deceased man on the top step. He saw no one else. but he saw some " hands " push deceased out, and then his satchell was thrown out after him. Deceased fell on his head in the street. He was certain that the deceased was forced out of the house. P. C. Williamson said that he heard a man fall into the street out of the Woolpack on the Friday night, and also the sound of doors being closed. He went there and found deceased lying upon his back. He was quite unconscious, and had a slight wound on the back of the head. He was taken to the Infirmary. Witness did not see him fall out of the , house. He heard P.S. Smith ask the manager of the house to look at the deceased, and the manager said that he did not know anything about the man. The mana- ger did not come out of the house. P.S. Smith stated that on the night in question, be was in Bold-street. He found P.C. Williamson attending to the deceased's injuries. He knocked at the Woolpack Inn, and asked Hollingworth concerning the deceased. Hollingworth replied that he knew nothing about the man, and had never seen him on the premises. Witness aeked him to come out and have a look at him, but he did not come out. The next morning witness discovered that Gleave had been with Flollingworth on the previous night. He then went to the Woolpack and asked the manager about the affair. Hollingworth . told him that he ought to come at the proper time. Witness told him he had a duty to perform, and he had come to obtain information concerning the affair. Be replied that he knew nothing about it. He also said be did not know where the barman lived. A bottle of whisky was beund in the deceased's coat pocket when taken to the infirmary. Dr. Bowden said that the deceased was brought to the Infirmary about 11.30 on the Friday night. He was uncon- scious, and had a small wound on the back of the head, and a black eye. He made a post mortem examination on Tuesday. The scalp wound did not reach the bone and was only a small one. There was an extensive frac- ture involving the base and right side of the skull about 10 or 12 inches in length. The brain on the left side was very much lacerated. The cause of death was injury to the skull and brain. The skull was thinner than normal. He thought a fall would inflict the injuries. Edward O'Toole, 6, Stone-yard, Bank-street, stated that on Friday he went for some ale and passed deceased in the Woolpack. When he came back, deceased was lying on the floor. Mr. A. Browne then called James Davies who said that the deceased was quite sober on the night in question just before eleven o'clock. Joseph Henry Florrabin, 4, Mount-st., stated that he was in'tbe Woolpack on the Friday night. Near eleven o'clock, he was in the vestibule talking to Mr. lolling- worth. One door was half closed. The deceased came out and while stepping out neither Flollingworth nor Gleave ever put a hand upon him while witness saw him. He (witness) went up the entry of the Woolpack and when ne again passed Bold-st., he found the matt lying on the footpath. He never heard any quarrel be- tween the deceased and the' manager of the public- house. Herbert Hollingwoith, the landlord of the Woolpack, was then called.He said that he had kept licensed houses since 1883 in Warrington. On the night in question. Weave and himself were serving. He saw the deceased for the first time in the hmse at about nine o'clock when he had some beer. About five minutes to eleven deceased had some whiskey with the witness Davies. When eleven o'clock came 'Time" was called, and the half of the door in Bold-st, was closed. While witness was there, the deceased went out. Weave closed the door after him. He did not know that the deceased had fallen to the ground. Shortly afterwards P.S. Smith knocked at the door, and upon opening it, the officer ask NI him about the deceased who was lying upon the ground. He said at first that he knew nothing about him, but he subsequently saw that deceased had a bag with him, and then noticed that it was the man who had gone out some time previously.—Cross-examined : He had never paid anything for breaking any man's collar-bone previously. He did not know how the man came by his injuries. Tile steps were dangerous. and persons had fallen down before. Arthur Gleave. the barman, said that he remembered Friday night. Be corroborated Mr. Hollingworth's statement. The Coroner, in summing up, said that the case was a question of fact and there was very little law. The question was did the deceased come by his death by the injuries he received near the Woolpack. There was no doubt about that he considered. They would then have to say whether the injuries were accidental or whether they were the result of violence. The question was "Is it safe to say from the esicienee that violence had been used." There was a mass of contradictory evidence. He th-ught the safest course was to give is verdict to the effect that deseased died from injuries received, but. whether accidental or wilfully inflicted there was no evidence, to how. • . The jury after 50 minutes censultation returned, and the foreman, Mr. A. H.Rathbfale announced that twelve of the filt,,esjuyymen had iiereed upon a verdict that the deceased ha I eied from Inj•iries received by being ejected from the Woolpack, but under what ciscum- stancee he was ejected there was not sufficient evidence to show. , eal • et • • 1 RUPTURES... ,TRUSSES. WOODEND9 PATENT TRUSSES, the most comforta'lle truss ever invented. A boon:to wearers, having no .spring .at back. Single 7s. Ad.. noable 12s, Car,isc::e vaid bead sir...l in inches tc J. ViOtiLtail), (Won:alit, 22, Plttr4treetl.liverpool.-4dvt. 41.,x44</p>
