        <p>Ingers. ,ADFORD.</p>
        <p>A ST. HELENS BIGAMIST IN COURT.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Monday, James Harrison, of 25, Peasley Cross-lane, was charged with being £2 14s. in arrear towards the maintenance of his wife, Mary E. Harrison.—Mr. Mearns, for the defence, said Harrison was unable to work, and had only worked three months during the past year. He suggested that an arlimrn- meut he taken.—Mr. Riley, who represented the coni- plamant, asked that the case should take its ordimiry course. The defendant', .who during the inter vat of , separation committed bigamy, seemed determined th it his first wife should not have a penny. The question of being unable to work was always brought forward, but it wtis a remarkable circumstance that he was actually arrested at work. He owed altogether about £23. Comp!ainant, in answer to Mr. Mearns, said OA did not want her husband to go to gaol, but she wanted some money.—Mr. Merle :if the case goes on he Will to ,!a.l, and you will Vse all your money. Complainant : Very well ; he won't be wotking for anybody else then. ! (I.aughter.)—Mr.• Mearns : If ,he goes t ) gael for this, and is quite willing--Complainant : Well, him go to gaol.--The Chairman (Mr. Orp,nlie)rn) 'I here is 311 ohhtzatinn for him to supp.rt his wife.—Mr. Mearn, : Unfortunately ho iaa got two wives. (Laughter.)— Harrison was committed for twenty-one days.</p>
