      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. LIVERPOOL CRESCENT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. LIVERPOOL CRESCENT.</p>
        <p>This match was played on Thursday last in the Margaret-st. Baths, Liverpool. The game through- out was in favour of the visitors,and ended in a vic- tory for Warrington by 3 goals to 1. At the com- mencement of the game Liocker,for Warrington,was first to•the ball,and he passed back to Turner, who shot at goal, but hit the cross bar. On a fresh. start being made there was a neat bit of passing between Turner, Locker, and Scotson, ending in the latter scoring the first goal for Warrington. The ball was again captured by the Warrington team,and several ineffectual shots at goal were made. Fouling was very frequent among the Liverpudlians, so much so that they had to be cautioned by the referee. At half time Warrington led by 1 goal to nil. In the first half Warrington defended the deep end, and VI the second half the Crescent team attacked the shallow end. Soon after half time, through some smart passing among their backs, the homesters secured their first goal, which made the two sides equal. Turner and Locker, for Warrington, played into one another's hands very well,and after more good passing the former shot another goal for Warrington. This event enlivened the Liverpool team, as time was nearly up, and they made strenuous efforts to augment their score. Their efforts were futile however, for, after a smart rush by the visitors, Scotsun shot his second goal and the third for Warrington. Hardly had this bz,,en accomplished when the referee's whistle sounded and the match was ended. The Warrington men played an exCellent game, and there was a marked decrease in the number of fouls on their side. They kept their places very well, and each Warringtchian " marked" his man on the opposite side, thus showing a great improvement on their play with the Crescent at the Warrington Bath some time agcy.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON. THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week ending 18zb August, 1894. During the week, which was the 33rd of thr current year, the following births and deaths were registered</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths. Warrington Latchford</p>
        <p>Total 5O</p>
        <p>The excess of births over deaths was 35. The death- rate from all causes was equal to 139 per 1,000, against 15 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 24'5 the corresponding week of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zyrnotic diseases was equal to 18 per 1,000, against 2-7 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 9 4 the corresponding week of 1893. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-60 60 up. 6 2 1 4 2 15</p>
        <p>The deaths included-2 from diarrhcea, 1 from phthisis, 3 from brain disease, 1 from heart disease, 1 j from, lung disease. The following cases of infectious disease have hen reported during the week :—Erysipelas 1 case, searlet ' fever 1 case. The rainfall measured 89. inch. The general direction was N.W.,the reading of the Anemometer deuoting 2085. miles. JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
