      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Eaten; to the Obttor.</head>
        <p>Eaten; to the Obttor.</p>
        <p>- _ THE REPUTATION OF DOLMAN'S LANE, WARRINGTON. Sir,..-On opening my "Examiner" on Saturday last, I was surprised to see on reading the report of the Licensing Cases that a certain neighbourhood in which 1 live was described as low—viz., Dolman's-lane. Now, sir, as one whe has lived in the neighbourhood for 15 years, I would like to say a fow words on this. Ido not say the neighbourhood is a respectable one, but this I do say, that if the public houses were taken away it would be as respectable as any other part of the town. There are three public houses all within a stones throw from one another, but the people do not want them and have let asked for them. I will take, for instance, one of these houses, and I know from my own personal experience that out of every seven who frequent this house six come from other parts of the town. So you see this house is not wanted, as the people in the neigh- bourhood do not go there. And now, sir, in conclusion I would like to tell those who put this neighbourhood down as low,that there are people living here who value their reputation equally as much as any lord of the land.—Yours truly, ONE WHO LIVES IN THE NEIGHBOURHOOD. August 28th, 1894, Warrington.</p>
        <p>FOREIGN COMPETITION. Sir,—Reading with much interest and pleasure a Speech delivered by Mr. T. Morris in which he referred to the long hours and low priced labour of Germany, I began to wonder how the workingmen of this country could best prevent themselves being injured by it. I know that tradesmen have been told by customers that if they sold German goods those customers would no longer buy from them. Persons offering themselves for public positions have been reproached for selling German goods, and Messrs. Crosfield and Sons have published a letter making known to the public that they do not employ German labour in the manufacture of their soap, think- ing I presume, that if people generally thought that they did, the sale of their soap might be injured. From these :ircumstances, it seems t. me that the loss of wages in Germany is a great injury to the manufacturers and workmen of this country, and that they may possibly greatly retard the comfort of civilized mankind. We cannot interfere with the Government of the German Empire, or directly with the long hours of its low priced labour, but cannot the workingmen of this and other countries, connot we and other countries prevent our- selves being injured so much by it, and ought we not to do so. If respectable tradesmen would lose customers by selling German goods, if manufacturers in this country would prejudice the sale of their manufactures y employing German labour, if sweated German labour s an injury to this country and perhaps to the civiliza- ion of the world, ought not the Governments of this and other countries to place a prohibitive tax on goods manufactured by that sweated labour. This subject is too important to be mixed up with party politics, and the hope that others with more time and greater ability than myself may take it up,is my excuse for writing this letter.—Yours S. KENYON. August 1894.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON BREWSTER SESSIONS. Sir,—There are two points connected with the recent Brewster Sessions to which I would like to call the at- tention of your readers, viz.- Ist, the conspicuous absence of any action on the part of the clergymen of the Church of England and Roman Catholic persuasion. 2nd, the creditable conduct of Nonconformist minis- ters in endeavouring to lessen the temptations for ex- cessive drinking in the town. The Temperance party raised objections to four, and the police to one of the licensed houses. Of these, one, raised by the Temperance party, and that objected to by the police, have been adjourned to 27th Slatember. To show whether there was due cause for the interven- tion of all Christian ministers, I propose with your kind permission to summarise the reasons given in evidence why the licenses should not be renewed ; these were : Congested areas ; affording undue facilities for secret drinking ; difficulty of police supervision ; objectionable and insufficient sanitary conveniences ; low class neigh- bourhoods ; indecent singing and dancing; use of filthy language ; employment ef young girls as waitresses ; solicitation ; former conviction. Can any minister of Christ conscientiously say he has nothing to do with such matters as these, or that he is ignorant of the existence of them; or is the non-inter- vention to be traced to the gratitude of the Church for the .various donations from brewers? With the tongue ef the Church bridled ,and the hands of the police tied, it is no wonder that Warrington has sucia an un- enviable notoriety for drunkenness and other vices. • That we are privileged by having in the town Non- conformist ministers gifted with such a deep sense of their duty to their Master in Temperance matters as to lead them to go and witness disgraceful scenes with a view of removing the cause, is something to be devoutly thankful for, and I hope the total abstinence men and women of the town wilLshow those gentlemen who are not ashamed to stand' bpldly up for Christ even in the lace of scorn and conlaiely, that they are deeply grate- ful for their spirited action. This gratitude cannot be better expressed than by attempting to purge the borough Council of all connected with the liquor traffic, and thus enable a Watch Committee to be appointed which shall not interfere with the due carrying out of the laws on licensing matters.—Yours truly. VERITAS. 27th August, 1894. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>-VALEDICTORY SERMON AT THE ST. PAUL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>-VALEDICTORY SERMON AT THE ST. PAUL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS TO THE DEPARTING MINISTERS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. Wilkes, who has superintended the Runcorn Wesleyan Ministerial Circuit for the past three years, bade farewell to his flock on Sunday evening in the St. Paul's Church, Runcorn. The congregation was a very large one, and the most intense interest was manifested in the rev, gentle- man's farewell words. He founded his discourse upon Revelations 11. 10—" Be thou faithful unto death, and I will give.thee a crown of life." The preacher drew the attention of his hearers to the period when Jesus Christ appeared in his glory before John in Patmos, and directed him to write certain letters to the different churches. In these letters Christ required faithfulness unto death : consent of will to serve Him; fidelity to lid cause; and an undying attachment to Hie person. In return Christ promised them a crown of life. The rev, gentleman asked them all to work zealously for the Master's cause in spite of all opposition, and success would certainly be the result. Concluding he compli- mented the choristers upon their exquisite singing, and urged the congregation to continue to practise that devoutness which had characterized them whilst he had had the pleasure of being their pastor. After the ordin- ary service the congregation met and partook of the Sacrament which was administered by the Rev. T. Wilkes, assisted by the Rev. W. Arrowsmith. On Tuesday evening Mrs. Wilkes was made the reci- pient by a few personal lady friends in the circuit of a silver tea kettle mounted upon a silver spirit lamp. kinong other presents she has received a brown leather Satchel from one of her personal friends. The Rev. T. Wilkes has also been given several menentoes of his con- nection with Runcorn among the number being a com- bination inkstand and stationary cabinet, from the members of the Camden Mothers meeting class; &amp;quan- tity of valuable stationary, a brown leather prirtman- teem, and a substantial sum of money in notes from other friends in the town. To Mrs. arrosesmith has been given, by a few of her lady friends, a silVer fruit stand and sugar. bowl with sifter. The Rev. Arrowstnith has 101811,10 received presents from various friends in his district. Both ministers left "sir Runcorn abodes on Wednesday evening, the firs amord for his new super- lataridency at Rochdale; and t !ast-naMed for a sirrsilar ,sbostitten It Rt. Helens.</p>
        <p>NOTIIS ON GAMES AYD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The two professionals, Bates and Clark, were very fortunate in the weather they had on Saturday, on the occasion of their benefit match. Clark, of course, is getting well established in the Warrington team, this season being the fifth in which he has kept the wickets for Warrington. He is deservedly popular for his cleverness behind the " sticks." In fact, he is a very ,mplished "stumper." Bates has now concluded his first season for Warrington, and he has done so well, with both bat and ball that he has been re-engaged for the next season. He has established himself a great favourite with Warringtonians, and his re-engagement will give general satisfaction.</p>
        <p>accoi</p>
        <p>It is estimated that when the Werneth team coin- I menced their innings there were 2,000 spectators pre- sent, so that a good "benefit" was assured. The Wer- neth players did not do anything sensational in their innings,their highest score being 22 off the bat of Lowe. Hall, who "came off" in the first match, was got out for only nine,a matter upon which the Warrington players naturally congratulated themselves. The fielding of the Warrington men was very smart,with the result that all the Werneth team were out for 79. Bibby and Bates secured eight of the wickets, while Connor obtained one, the other wicket being lost owing to a too great eagerness to score.</p>
        <p>Bates and Connor started the Warrington innings,bu while the beneficiare was the last of his side to come ou his first partner was bowled before a run was scored. Wadsworth stayed until 24 was obtained, and then it appeared as though Warrington was going to be put through the mill as they have been so frequently dur- ing thepresent season, for half the wickets were down for only 37, or less than half their opponents' score. L. A. Voisey,however,came to the rescue and stayed until 57 had been scored. Warrington were not yet out of dan.er,but Bibby now came in, and before any chance was offered, the game was saved, when Bates bad a narrow escape. This took place when the totals were equal. Bibby was successful in compiling 22 before he fell a victim to the formidable Hall. The remaining batsmen did not add much to the score, and upon Bates being caught, the in- nings closed for 108. Bates excelled himself on Satur- day, and with the score of 52, he made his highest con- tribution of the present season.</p>
        <p>The cricket season is fast approaching to a close, and perhaps some mention might here be made of some of the individual players in the Warrington team. Of Bates and Clark we have mentioned their merits above. S. M. and J. J. Crosfield and also R. Smith have not been in the field as frequently as one would wish during the season, and this has been a source of weakness to the team. G. Clarke has done yeoman service during the season, and on many occasions has saved his side from ignominious defeat. Among players who are compara- tively new to the team, the first to attract attention is E. Connor, who has dome some capital bowling perform- ances this season. W. Wadsworth has also been very brilliant with the bat, as also has J. Callon. Bibby has not had many chances of distinguishing himself in the first team, but he has not done at all badly. His per- formance on Saturday was exceptionally creditable, for after having the best bowling analysis of the afternoon ,he came in at a very critical period of the game and kept in until the result was beyond a doubt. The season has not been a very successful one, but still it has been better than at one time seemed possible.</p>
        <p>The two Sankey teams were each unsuccessful on Saturday, the Wire Mills being defeated by Arley Park, and the Wesleyans by St. Helens Primitives. They can therefore mutually condole themselves upon the uncer- tainties of cricket, and each hope that the same thing will not occur again. There will not be much chance of it this season at any rate.</p>
        <p>The St. Elphity could not beat the Weston 2nd on Saturday, but they succeeded in making a tie, each team scoring 36. It would have gone hard with the St. Elphins team had Gilbert not played with them, for he captured seven of the Weston wickets for three runs. He also carried his bat out for sevent runs, being the sixth to go in. Scarisbrick was the top scorer of his side with ten.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The football season has come round again, and to-day it will be in full swing. Although twice as long as the cricket season, football enthusiasts keep up their inter- est right-to the end of the season. Football is without doubt a much more exciting game than cricket, as the players are continually on the move, and the fortunes of each side are so variable, whereas cricket is compara- tively slow, especially if a " stone-waller " is in.</p>
        <p>Rugby football in Lancashire has received a great impetus during the past year or two by tlae formation of the Lancashire Rugby League. This league only got into full working order last year, when the Oldham club gained the championship. Warrington began last season in a very discouraging manner, but towards the end they gained hand over hand, and finished the third in the last. In fact, had they not been beaten by Oldham, which was the only league match they lost in the last part of the season they would have carried off the champion- ship. It is to be hoped that they will commence in a winning vein, and then they will have an even better chance of gaining the Lancashire Cup this year than last. There is a formidable list of chits to meet in the league, the following being the teams :---Gldham, Swin- ton, Wigan, Tyldesley, Rochdale Hornets,Salford,Broug- tonßangers, St.Helens,and Leigh. Last year, however, they beat every one of these clubs except Oldham and Leigh, the latter of which was not engaged. Their players this year are not very different from last season. They have Webster again, and his great speed will be useful at three-quarter. Heesom still retains his position as full-back,and the two Bates as half-backs. The front rank is a heavy lot,and will stand some pushing. Taken as a whole, the team should show up well in the best of company.</p>
        <p>-- The League matches do not commence until the end of the month, and the first two or three matches will be " friendlies." " Friendlies" have not yet come into such disrepute in Rugby as in Association football, and there will be without doubt a good "gate" to-day when War- rington will meet their old antagonists, the St. Helens Recreation. The calibre of this club will test the War- rington team, and Warringtonians will have an oppor- tunity of judging, to some extent, of the kind of stuff their representatives are made of.</p>
        <p>The following is the team which will play for Warring- ton to-day against St. Helens Recreation team. Full back, Heesom ; three-quarter backs, Webster, Carey, Potts and Evans; halt oacki, J. Bite and J. T. Bate; forwards, Turner (captain), Jolley, Berry, Buckley, Houghton, Taylor, Deakin and Burrowes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WAIL FROM THE PARK GATES.</head>
        <p>A WAIL FROM THE PARK GATES.</p>
        <p>(First Angel.) Phew! Is this where I've got to eternally dwell? Pooh! shan't we go higher than this? They've surely mistaken and put us in-- well I thought we were journeying to bliss. (Second Angel.) There are railings and furnaces close on my right, Fire and smoke are away at my back, As for smells, for a delicate angel of light, I think I have more than my whack. (Third Angel.) Ah me! worse than this; stink and flame I might bear, But the last straw of direful disgrace Is heaped upon us, for look over there- A Conservative Club in my face! • (Chorus of Angels.) 0 never have seraphim fallen so low, We could gaze on your tan-yard or pub. Your Whitecross, youi Dallam, intercepting depot, But oh I a Conservative Club!</p>
        <p>• (Fourth Angel.) From a palace of crystal to this horrid site 0 how can they sleep in their beds These wretches of councilors! 'twould serve them all</p>
        <p>right, If the four of us fell on their heads. (Chorus) Heigh-ho! Angels never have fallen so far, Overwhelmed with sneer and with snub. Give us fire, give us smoke, but there's one thing we bar, And that's a Conservative Club. (First Angel) I shall droop, I shall die, I shall fade quite away, What a hopeless and horrible plight! My pale sisters are sighing and moaning all day, The pail system annoys me at night.</p>
        <p>(Chorus) - Ah yes l it annoys us at night,—But oh! Besides this dread tale of a tub, We've a mountain of sorrow, a river of woe. Why, 0 why, this Conservative Club! Come droop your heads, sisters, like angels of night Let us wear a funereal air, We're a qusrtette of petrified damsels Wight. With trappings of woe and despair— Of gloomy distreaa and despair, ' A medley of miserable maidens that might, Never more a beatitude wear!</p>
        <p>BNoDO II ASS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
