      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY SEPTEMBER 1. 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY SEPTEMBER 1. 1894.</p>
        <p>Mr. Win. Edward Milner, who was at that time in</p>
        <p>business at the shop which Mr. C. Jacksem Holmes at present occupies in Bridge-street. He served a seven years' apprenticeship at this shop, and in 1842, he went to Mr. John Thompson's shop at Hitchin, for about seven or eight months. He returned to . _ . .</p>
        <p>Warrington, however, in 1843, and in February 1846, he took over _Mr. Miler's business. At this _ _</p>
        <p>time he lived in Friar's Green,. along with Mr. T.</p>
        <p>Smithson and Mr. John Harrison. He entered</p>
        <p>public life in 1847, when he was appointed one of the police commissioners of Warrington, and he is the only survivor among those who signed the transfer of the old commissioners' powers. to the</p>
        <p>Corporation of Warrington. At about 1850, he was an overseer of the poor. In Aprir 21, 1852, he mar- ried Elinor, second daughter of Jonathan and Eliza- beth Jackson, of Calder Vale, near Crarstang, and in</p>
        <p>the same year he became a member ot the Warring- ton Board of Guardians of which body he was chairman for two years. He was connected with the Board of Guardians until 1856. He first entered the Town Council in 1853, when he was returned for the S.W. ward at a.. bye-election. He only . .</p>
        <p>remained in the Council Until the following November when his term_ of office expir-</p>
        <p>ing, he contested the S.E. ward against Messrs. William Reynolds and Josiah Whittle. He</p>
        <p>• was unsuccessful in this contest, but was only de- feated by the narrow majority of five votes. He remained outside the Council until 1856. During this interval Mr.ilelmes was closely concerned with</p>
        <p>a most extraordinary dispute which arose between Coroner Driffield,and the Mayor and Town Council, and a large body of the inhabitants of Warrington. The dispute first arose on the occasion of the in- quest upon the body of a woman named Mrs. Burn. Mr. Holmes was appointed to be the foreman of the jury, which was composed of a number of respect- table tradesmen of the town. Mr. Holmes being</p>
        <p>called upon to take the oath, as foreman of the jury, he stated that he was "one of the people called Quakers," and would make affirmation instead. The coroner upon hearing this, requested another foreman to be named. Mr. Holmes thought that un- less there was a legal objection he should not be deprived of the position of foreman; he did not care twopence for the post, but he did for the principle. The coroner said teat he was perfectly willing that Mr. Holmes should act. His objection to him rose on the grounds that the oath of the jury referred to that of their foreman. He would be obliged to Mr. Macmichael (the chief constable of Warrington),not to put any member of the Society of Friends as fore- man of the jury in future. This action of the coroner aroused great indignation in the town. It was asserted that his reason was a" lazy " one. If a Quaker be foreman of a jury, the oath taken by the other jurymen had to be altered from "having heard the oath of your foreman," to" having heard tha affirmation." Tne Town Council was also urged to take the matter up. The in wn Council did take the matter up,with the result that a scene occurred at an inquest, the like of which probably never has occurred since. The inquest was on the body of James Hough, and was neld at the Roebuck Inn, Bridge-st. It is the invariable custom for the man at the top of the list of the jurymen to act as foreman. On this occasion, Mr. Holmes was again on the top of the list, and another Quaker, Mr. Nield, was at the bottom, so that whichever way the coroner turned the list, a Quaker turned up at the to. The coroner not to be outdone, passed over Mr. Holmes, on the ground that he wished to affirm and came to the next name on the list, that of the late Alderman Webster, asking him to be foreman. Mr. Webster, however, refused to be sworn, and proposed that Mr. Holmes be foreman. This was seconded by a large number of the jury in unison. An angry disputation now occurred between the jury and the coroner, many jurymen asserting that they would go to prison before they would serve under the circumstances. The coroner ordered P.C. Edgar to go out to find some fresh men to serve upon the jury. That officer went out and brought in Mr. Woods, pawnbroker, who upon being informed by the jury of the circumstances of the case, utterly refused to serve and walked out. The upshot of it all was that the coroner discharged the jury and ordered the Chief Constable to call a different jury, and not to let the body be interred. A strong protest signed by the jurymen was the result of this arbitrary conduct, and it was signed by several well-known names such as Messrs. C. J. Holmes (the subject of our sketch), S. M. Webster, G. Blackhurst, and W. Nield. Two days afterwards another jury was brought together,but lo! a Quaker was again at the top and another at the bottom ! The coroner, almost wild with rage, declined to allow either of these gentlemen to be foremen, and the jury were equally resolved that one of them should. After an angry scene, the coroner ordered the police to turn the jury out of the room, but the police refused, and in this they were supported by the Mayor. The coroner then said that he could hold no inquest, and walked out of the room. The jury, however, followed him, and when the coroner got opposite the Lion Hotel, he found that he had forgotten his overcoat. He returned for it, fol- lowed by his faithful jury, who said that they would not leave him until they were lawfully dis- charged. Having received his coat the coroner again headed the procession, accompanied by Mr. West, who, like a priest accompanying a malefactor to execution kept reading from a book the duties of coroners, and the privileges of jurors, every now and again exhorting Mr. Driffield to repent. But Mr. Driffield was inexorable. He marched on accompanied by Mayor, jury, Chief Constable, and reporters. When opposite the bridewell he asked the Chief Constable for a private room. A room he could have but alas! no privacy. The jury declared that until they were legally discharged, they would follow him into any room. He then determined to go to Padgate. Then once more</p>
        <p>Away went coroner, and away Went jury at his heels; They little thought when they set out Of being such roving chiels. The dogs did bark with sheer affright And up the windows flew, The neighbours certain they were gone, A murdered man to view. At the borough boundary another confabulation took place, but no agreement could be arrived at, and Mr. Driffield departed. Needless to say the fight for the religious rights ended in a victory for the Quakers, and Mr. Holmes is to be congratulated at the part he took in it. At the November elections of the following year, 1856, Mr. Holmes was returned to the Town Council for the north-east ward, and from that year to the present, he has been continuously a member of the ruling body of the town. He had not been in the Council many years before he was elected to the aldermanic dignity. He first became Mayor in 1863, lafter a very exciting debate. The retiring mayor, Councillor Hepherd was nominated again by some of the members of his party, very much against his expressed wish in the Council. Alderman Holmes was proposed as a candidate for the mayoralty by Councillor Pickton seconded by Councillor Robin- son. There was an excited debate, the chief objec- tions to Mr. Holmes being that he would not go to church. Despite an appeal from Councillor Hep- herd to withdraw his candidature, his proposer and seconder declined and the two names were put to the vote,with the result, that Alderman Molmes was elected by 17 to 13. Alderman Holmes was the first member of the Society of Friends to occupy the civic chair in Warrington,and he declined to attend church in state on the ground that it was only a public show. During his mayoralty in 1863-4 the dreadful Bradfield flood, near Sheffield, took place in whichmore than 200 lives were lost. Aid. Holmes by virtue of his office of Mayor called a meeting to raise subscriptions for the relief of the bereaved families at Sheffield, and worked hard and success- fully in that direction. He won golden opinions during his year of office, and when he retired he was generally congratulated on the success of his mayoralty. Alderman Holmes was again elected to the office of Mayor in 1869, and his continued re- fusal to attend church, was the cause of recriminat- ing articles in the local press. In 1870 he was ap- poitted by the Crown to be a magistrate for the borough, and he is one of the oldest of that body at the present time acting. Aldetman Holmes' principal work has been in the Council and among his early aims was an endea- vour to purchase the water works. The scheme of purchase had been agreed to by the Corporation but was vetoed by the ratepayers. The water works have recently been acquired by the Corporation,but the terms of purchase have not been nearly so favourable as they were at the time when Alder- man Holmes first advocated their acquirement. He was also very prominent in the negotiation for the purchase of Bank Park, and was one of three mem- bers of the Council who waited upon Colonel Wilson Patten to complete the purchase. He was the lead. , big spirit in the purchase ot the gas works during the, mayoralty of the late Mr. Webster. Upon the -death of Alderman Hepherd in 1879, he was ap-, pointed chairman of the Gas Committee, a position which he held until two years ago. He was also ap- 'pointed,chsictnan of the Finance Committee in 19,63, and has served upon the Watch. Committee, General ,Purposes Committee, Parliamentary' Committal,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>PRESENTATION OF TaE FREEDOM OF THE BOROUGH,</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF TaE FREEDOM OF THE BOROUGH,</p>
        <p>. An interesting event in the history of Warrington occurred onThursday week when the freedom of the borough was conferred upon two townsmen—Alder- man Holmes and Major Pickmere. Owing to the condition of Alderman Holmes' health it was found advisable for a small depWation to wait upon him at his house in Be wsey-road, and there present him with the casket containing the certificate of the freedom of the borough. Accordingly on Thursday morning a deputation consisting of the Mayor (Alderman E. Greenall), Alderman Blackhurst, Coun- cillors Shaw Green, Monks,Flood, and Smethurst, Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk) and Mr. Fairhurst (borough treasurer) proceeded to the house, and were received by Alderman Holmes, Mrs. Holmes, Mtss Jackson, and Mr. Jackson Holmes. Letters of apology for non-attendance were read from Alderman Wright, Councillors R. T. Faircloug.h and Henry Roberts.—The Mayor in suitable terms made the presentation.—Alderman Blackhurst made a few remarks and the casket was then formally handed to Alderman Holmes who was much affected by the action of his fellow townsmen. He asked his son to reply on his behalf.—Mr. Charles Jack- son Holmes suitably replied, referring to his father's long connection with the town, and stated that his father would not much longer be able to come amongst them. An interesting souvenir of the occasion was made by Mr. Birtles, who photographed the party on the lawn. Light refreshments were afterwards partaken of. The casket, a description of which has already been given in the "Examiner," bore the following inscription :—" Borough of Warrington. At a meeting of the Council of the Borough of Warrington, holden on Tuesday, the sth day of June, 1894, as a special Council under the Honorary Freedom of the Borough Act, 1885, present Edward Greenall, Esq., Mayor, and a full Council, it was unanimously resolved that Charles John Holmes Esq., justice of the peace, be and is hereby admitted an honorary freeman of the borough in recog- nition of valuable and eminent services rendered by him to the borough.—EDWAßD GREENALL, mayor; J. LvoN Wnerria3, town clerk." There was a similar inscription, with the exception of the name, on the casket. presented to Major Pickmere. Mr. Alderman Holmes, J.P„ after the presentation, mentioned that before the Corporation was formed he was qualified as one of the self-elected commissioners and was one of sixteen who transferred the properties of the town to the new Corporate body. He was the last survivor of that group of citizens of that time. At the Town Hall at three o'clock a meeting was held te confer the freedom of the borough on Major Pickmere. The proceedings took place in the Council Chamber and there were present : Major Pickmere, the Mayor (Alderman E. Greenall), Alderman Hutchinson, Council- lors Shaw Green, Flood, Smethurst, Hill,Monks, Woods, Davies, W. J. Shaw Plumpton, Skelton,Cannell.Longton, Mr. R.Pierpoint, Captain Withington, Mr. Travers Pickmere, Dr. Urornall, Mr. J. Lyon Wnittle (town clerk), Mr. S. Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer). Letters of apology were sent by Alderman Harrison, Councillors R. T. Fairclough and H. Roberts. The Mayor said he had been asked to apologise for the others engaged otherwise on the Bench and unable to attend. The MAYOR said they were there that day to perform the pleasing duty of presenting to Major .Pickmere the silver casket on the table containing the Freedom of the Borough which the Council unanimously conferred upon him in June last. In many ancient towns the right to freedom existed, and generally depended upon birth, servitude, or marriage and each Borough had its own peculiar customs and bye laws regulating the admission of Freemen. The right of freedom in those Boroughs carried with it the right to vote at Parliamentary elec- tions and to share in corporate benefits such as rents of common lands and to exemption from certain tolls or dues. The custom gradually sprung up of admitting public men to be "honorary freemen" in those Boroughs as a mark of distinction for eminent services rendered to the Borough. It was an honour which modern towns could not before 1885 confer, in- asmuch as they had no Freemens' Roll. He did not think the Council could have selected two gentlemen more worthy of the honour, or two who so fully answered the qualification required by the Act, for they were two of their mostdistinguished townsmen and each of them had rendered eminent services to the town. (Applause.) The Mayor afterwards presented the casket to Major Pickmere. Alderman HUTCHINSON said he took it a great pleasure to be there that afternoon on that occasion of the pre-- seutation to their old and valued servant and friend of the town, Major Pickmere. He had rendered great and valuable services to the town as mayor, alderman, and ' councillor, and he thought they could not bestow the freedom of the borough on a more worthy citizen. The speaker referred to the valuable service Major Pickmere had given in the Volunteer movement in the town. He had known Mr. Holmes quite as long as Major Pickmere he was as worthy of the honour conferred upon him as any man in Warrington. He had been in the harness something like 40 years. and had been a model for them to follow in his steps. Be was a most amiable man and he was very pleased that they had conferred upon him the highest honour the citizens could bestow. Councillor P. DAVIES said he spoke as the only relative present of one who had had a similar honour conferred upon him. He referred to his father, and he felt very sorry that he was not there that day because there was no one in the borough of Warrington who would have had more pleasure to welcome his friend, Mr. IPickmere. It Was a very pleasing duty to be present on that important occasion, and he hoped that others in the Council might attain that distinction. (Applause.) Councillor FLOOD said, as a young member of that Council he could hardly express his feelings on the matter. He had had a long connection with Major Pickmere. On first coming to Warrington he came early into contact with him. No two men in the long record they had had in Warrington, dating back over two centuries, were more worthy of the esteem of their fellow citizens than the two they were honouring that day. Councillor SHAW-GREEN said the honour they were conferring that day was one in exchange, or return for peryice that had been done. He expressed his admira- tion of the services which Alderman Holmes and Major Pickmere had rendered to them. (Applause.) Mr. ROBERT PIERPOINT, M.P., thanked the Mayor for his kindness in inviting him to be present that afternoon on that interesting occasion. He regretted exceedingly that he had not had the good fortune to meet them that morning in order that he might have had another op- portunity of being present on a similar occasion which took place, on the conferring of the freedom of the borough upon Alderman Holmes. If the Mayor would tell Alderman Holmes how much he regretted it he would be greatly obliged to him. Alderman Holmes was certainly one who had worked hard for the good of the town—a man very worthily deserving the honour he had that morning received. As to Major Plcktnere he knew that he (Mr. Pierpoint) highly esteemed him. They knew perfectly well the good work Mr. Pickmere had done for many years in the town. They had been told that he had served the citizens as mayor on five different occasions. He took it that no man deserved better of his fellow men and women than the man who served the municipal offices of his town. He thought thay ought to congratulate themselves in England that they had so many men will- ing to serve their town without guerdon. He had had some opportunities of talking to Americans and he had found very often that Americans were very properly justified in grumbling at the way their municipal work was transacted. There was no doubt that there was corruption in American municipal affairs. (Hear, hear.) He had told them that it served them perfectly right, because they were so eager in making money that they had no time .to give to municipal matters. He trusted that the example that Major Pickmere and Alderman Holmes had set would find worthy followers, not only now but as long as the good old town existed. (Applause.) MAJOR PICKMERE in responding said they would all agree with him that that was a most trying occasion. He sincerely thanked them for the honour they had con- ferred upon Mai by presenting him with the freedom of the borough. Be need hardly say that it gave him much pleasure and satisfaction to think that it had not been conferred upon him without deliberation on their part. He was only sorry that his old friend, Mr. Alderman Holmes was not present. If there was one man more than another who deserved the honour of the freedom of the borough, he thought that man was Alderman Holmes, who had done his duty well. The memento would be handed down in course of time to his children, by which they would see that their father was of some service to his native town. (Applause.) The MAYOR moved that the proceedings of that meet- ing be recorded on the minutes of the Council. Alder- man HirrcninsoN seconded and it was carried unanim- ously. Those who were present were afterwards photographed by Mr. Birtles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>ALDERMAN HOLMES :</head>
        <p>ALDERMAN HOLMES :</p>
        <p>AN INTERESTING CHAPTER OF WARRINGTON HISTORY.</p>
        <p>In connection with the presentation the follow- ing details of the :ife of Alderman Holmes will, no doubt, be of interest to our readers. Mr. Charles John Holmes was born on October 7, 1820, at Haddockstone Grange, Markington, near Ripley. Yorkshire. Mr. Holmes received the earlier part of his education at Preston. but when about nine years of age. lie went to Ack worth School, which is the principal school in connection with the Society of Friends, of which body he is a member. He after- wards went to Joseph Edmundson'e school. in Penketh, the house at present occupied by Mr. (4. H. Bolton, where he remained a sear and a half. From this School he went to George Edmundton's house at Blackburn for twelve months, afterwards' returning home to Orren. near Wigan, where his fatherthen resided. In March 19, 1835. Mr. Holmes, obtained a situation in Warrington as aPpretitide to</p>
        <p>MMI</p>
        <p>MEM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <p>Open Spaces Committee, and Fire Engine C•mmittee. lie was also a member of the old Mechanics Institute, and was secretary to a soup kitchen fund when there was a disastrous state of things in the town in 1847, during which time he visited nearly every cottage in the Warrington dis- trict. Mr. Holmes is, it is needless to repeat, a staunch member of the Society of Friends, and he is on the Committee of the Friends School at Pen- ken, an institution in which he takes great in- terest. Indeed, Mr. Holmes has always been in the front rank of educationalists in Warrington, and he has been a member of the People's College Commit- tee-for a long number of years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MAJOR PICKMERE.</head>
        <p>MAJOR PICKMERE.</p>
        <p>Is the eldest son of Mr. John Richard Pickmere solicitor, Bank-street, Warrington, and was born in the premises now used as an office by Mr. A. Browne, solicitor. He ultimately succeeded to the business of his uncle Mr. Ralph Randall Pickmere, wine and spirit merchant, Bridge-street, Warring- ton, and successfully conducted the same business for a considerable number of years. He has been married twice: his first wife being Mary, the eldest daughter of the late Mr. William Hunt, surgeon, of Stanley-street, and his present wife was a Miss Brown, of Rainhill, sister-in-law to the late Mr. John Anderton, wine and spirit merchant. Major Pickmere has been mayor of Warrington five times from 1873-4, 1879-80-1-2, and 1887. He was made a justice of peace for the borough in 1885 and a magistrate of the West Derby Hundred for the County of Lancaster in March, 1891. The Town Couucil has several times owned him as a member, both as councillor and alderman. He is a member of the managing committee of the Infirmary and was one of the earliest, of the Rifle Volunteers in Warrington, but he retired from active connection with the corps, several years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SHIP CANAL. -- SHAREHOLDERS' MEETING. THE PROSPECTS OF THE CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL. -- SHAREHOLDERS' MEETING. THE PROSPECTS OF THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>The shareholders of the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany held their eighteenth ordinary meeting on Tuesday, Mr. J. K. Bythell, the newly appointed chairman, pre- siding. In moving the adoption of the report the Chairman said that after making provision for all the expenditure incurred up to June 30, including interest on debentures and for the estimated cost of new works ordered, the remaining bAlance of unappropriated re- sources amounted to £512,000. That sum included the remainder of the last £500,000 of the loan from the Manchester Corporation, the bulk of which had been received since June 30. As regarded the payments to be made out of that sum,they had first to furnish £50,000, the cost of dredging during the current half year. The expenditure of that money woud not remove the whole of the silt from the canal, but it would cut a broad ; channel threugh it to the depth of 26ft., sufficient for the safe navigation of any vessel. Having given some interesting figures relating to the revenue and expendi- ture on the canal, the Chairman spoke at length upon the prospects of the undertaking. Ile said that, in round figures, 2,200,000 tons of traffic had been moved on the canal during the six months, and yet the universal opinion about the canal was that there was practically nothing doing on it That showed what capabilities the canal had for traffic as soon as they were able to get held of it. Some people might be disappointed, but when he looked back upon the difficulties the competition, and the inertia with which they had had to contend, his opinion was that they had made a very good beginning. They were a little disappointed with some of their friends whe had derived benefit from the canal, who did not seem to act in a very liberal spirit, and who seemed to think that before putting their traffic on the canal they must squeeze the shipowners, the Canal Company, and every- body site as much as possible. That was a suicidal policy. He pointed to specific instances of savings effected by using the canal, and urged that, in view of the expected development of facilities for conducting the traffic, shippers shculd not allow themselves to be tied down by conferences in future. With regard to the American cotton trade, the Company were urging spinners to buy only for shipment to Manchester. If they obtained their proportion of the enormous cotton traffic they must have cotton stored In Manchester, and arrangements must be made for selling it on equal terms with cotton stored in Liverpool. If terms could not be arrangea with Liverpool, the only thing to be done would be to form a Cotten Brokers' Association in Man- chester, independent of Liverpool. His opinion was, however, that the true interests of both ports were that in this matter they should work together. In conclusion, the Chairman emphasised the fact that the success of the canal depended upon the importers and exporters of this district. The Lord Mayor of Manchester. (Sir A. Marshall,seconded the adoption of the report, which was agreed to. HALF-YEARLY REPORT.</p>
        <p>The report of the directors of the Manchester Ship Canal, for the half year ending June 30, was issued at the end of last week. It shows that the receipts amounted to £40,123. The efforts for the devolopment of the traffic are being vigorously pushed forward, and will in due course bear fruit. The protracted negotia- tions with the railway companies for the working of the railway traffic to and from the Ship Canal had resulted in arrangements which, though not yet complete in de- tails, would it was believed, give the port of Manchester the full benefit of its; geographical position. The com- pany were gradually issuing through and inclusive rates from Salford and other districts. The revenue from the Bridgewater Undertaking for the half-year was £20,524. The condition of the water of the canal is referred to asi a reproach to the local authorities of the entire water- shed, and as having involved the company in light expense. There was a probability, however, of great 1 reforms in this matter, as in a short time the sewage of Manchester would be prevented from entering the canal. The directors were concerned, however, by the prospect of a continued heavy deposit. Appended to the directors' .report is a report as to the progress of the works by Sir E. Leader Williams, chief engineer. This report states that the dredging of the canal to the full depth of 26 feet has been actively carried on and will soon be completed. The construction of the Walton Dock and the dredging of the River Mersey between that point and the iron and chemical works near Bank Quay,Warrington, are still in progress. The dredging is a troublesome matter, owing to the continued silting. These works had to be carried out to enable large barges from Bank Quay and Warrington to use the Ship Canal during low tides when they could not pass down the River Mersey to Liverpool. The</p>
        <p>Ship Canal Company will receive a smalltoll on the cargo in these barges when the work is completed. Not- withstanding the fact that nearly all the steamers using the canal have not previously navigated a similar channel the trade has been carried on in a satisfactory manner, steamers using the canal regularly by day and night. The hydraulic machinery at the docks,. swing aqueduct, bridges, and locks continues to work in a satisfactory manner.</p>
        <p>THE OLD RIVER AT WILDERSPOOL : TO RE USED FOR FLOATING TIMBER.</p>
        <p>With regard to the accommodation provided on the canal for floating timber,Mr. Marshall Stevens,the mana- ger of the Canal Company, has sent out a circular letter the following terms :—" I have pleasure in informing you that we can now deal with floating timber in the old river course alongside Wilderspool Causeway, near Warrington, where there is accommodation for many cargoes. We will receive the cargoes from the ship and place them in the pond, redelivering in the. water at loading berth for is. per load, merchants pro- viding any moorings required for mooring the timber alongside the river-banks, and holding us free from lia- bility for loss of timber. The pond is within a mile of Warrington Station, and besides being convenient for inspection, is admirably situated for the distribution of the timber by rail, barge, or cart. When the timber is , delivered to railway, the cost of loading into railway w.aggons will be borne by us out of the railway rate,and when delivery is required by cart or barge, merchants can arrange for the purchaser to do the work him- self, or if we are to do it we should charge actual cost of labour plus 10 per cent., 'or a fixed rate of is. per load. Rent at the rate of a id. per load per month will be charged from the time the timber enters the pond. We reserve full right to shift the timber in the water as and when required. Should the construction of works require it, the Com- pany reserve the right to have the timber put on land at the merchant's expense subject to three months, notice. lam arranging with the railway companies for special rates for the conveyance of this timber, and the rates when quoted by us will include Ship Canal tolls and loading charges. The pond is situated in Section Bof the canal (as per plan enclosed), the canal tolls being 2s. 3d. per load; but if timber taken into the pond is afterwards conveyed up the Ship Canal to Manchester by the merchants for distribution there the tolls would be 3s. per load, and if taken down the Ship Canal to Section Aor into the river Mersey, Section A tolls of is. 6d. per load will only be charged. A good discharging berth is available at the entrance to the pond, but when the lower masts of vessels will not permit them to pass under bridges 75ft. above water level we will receive the rafts from alongside ship at Seltport or Runcorn and tow them to the pond without extra charge. I think if you will go into this matter you will find it worth your while to use the pond, into which a cargo is now being floated."</p>
        <p>The result of the Leizester election was declared at midnight on Wednesday. The two Liberal candidates were returned, the figures being :—Mr. Broadhurst (L), 9,464; Mr. Hazed (L), 7,184 ; Mr. Rolleston (C), 6,967 Mr. Burgess (Labour), 4,402. World-famed as timekeepers. The new quick-wind watche In gold, gold-filled , silver, oxidised and niokel cares, both open•face and hunting, in aU required sixes, bare met with universal favour, 17s. M. to 130 s. Illustrated catelcigue from Waterbury, 7, Snow Hill, London, B.V. Sold by all. VatobuiOrers.—.ddef. (4)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WARRIN CT TO N.</head>
        <p>WARRIN CT TO N.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A. At the above meeting on Sunday afternoon the chair was taken by Mr. James Bennett of Bewsey. An excellent address was given by Mr. Douglas of Manchester. Solos were sung by Miss Wheatley and there was a very large attendance. Mr. Hedge- cock and Mr. William Roberts also took part in tne meeting. ST. ALZAN'I3 ROMAN CATHOLIC CHURCH. The annual sermons were preached at St. Alban's Roman Catholic Church, Bewsey-st., on Sunday,the preacher in the morning being the Rev. A. 0. Coyle, of the Church of the Holy Cross, Liverpool, and in the evening the Rev. J. A. Wilson, of St. Peter's, Liverpool. There was a large attendance at each service.</p>
        <p>SOARING RAIN PROOF OVERCOATS, yet porous, 555. C. J'Acicsox.HOLMES, General Outfitter, Market Gate, Warring-</p>
        <p>ton.—Advt.</p>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S GOSPEL TEMPERANCE MISSION.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening an open air meeting was held behind the British Workman Coffee Tavern,Bewsey- st., conductod by Mr. John Drury. Addresses were</p>
        <p>given by Messrs: R. Storey, .1. Fe-arnley, and A.llor- rocks, who spoke of,the evils of drink. There was a large audience.</p>
        <p>CROFT HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the members of this society was held at the Board Schools on Friday evening week, when there was a moderate attendance. Mr. J. Shaw presided. It was announced that the Rector, who is the president of the society, had con- sented to open the show with the assistance of Captain Withington, of Culcheth Hall. Messrs. W. Fazakerley and T. Fairhurst were appointed to inspect the members' gardens with regard to the cut flowers and vegetables to be exhibited.</p>
        <p>SEWING MACHINES, Hand £2 18e. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. Dumorr, 16 and 1, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Advt. CHOIR TRIP TO BLACKPOOL.</p>
        <p>About 30 members of St. Paul's choir visited Black- pool on Monday, on the occasion of their annual trip. They left Warrington at about six o'clock, and reached Blackpool soon after nine. The Vicar ac- companied them, and they were met there by Mr. Chantry, and Alderman Sutton. The party after visiting the principal places of interest and enjoy- ing the various sights left about seven, and arrived back at Warrington soon after nine o'clock. The day was beautifully fine, and the visitors appeared well satisfied and delighted with their outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO A. WARRINGTON A OMAN.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A. WARRINGTON A OMAN.</p>
        <p>As P.C. Clough was walking along Chorley-street on Saturday night he heard a smashing of glass in Scott-street, and hastening there, found a woman named Barbara • Lowndes, the wife of James Lowndes, of 40, Scott-street. bleeding profusely from three severe wounds in her right arm, which had been inflicted by the glass of a broken window. The constable rendered first aid to the injured woman. Assisted by Mr. Scott he took the woman to Dr. Hall's surgery in Winwick-street, who upon examination, found that she had severed the main artery in her arm. He did all that was necessary, in the way of tying up the artery, &amp;c., and the woman was afterwards enabled to return home. TENNIS REQUIBITEB.—GARLAND, 51. Brldge-otreet, War- rington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CHILDREN'S LEAGUE OF PITY.</head>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S LEAGUE OF PITY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, Miss Broadbent, secretary of the Warrington branch of the Children's "League of Pity," entertained the members at the Hollies, Latchford. The afternoon was fortunately fine,and the children assembled at 330, and thoroughly enjoyed games of all descriptions in the field, atter which tea and fruit were provided, and prizes given to those who had won in races and other games. The Mayoress (Mrs. Greenall) was prevented from being present owing to indisposition. his expected that this meeting of children will be the means of deepening the interest felt in the Society for the Prevention of Cm u elty to Children, and that many new members may join the League of PiLy. GlanDmr Smers.— GARLAND, Ironmonger, Warrington and Earlestown.—Advt.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A CROFT GENTLEMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Priestner, of Springfield House,</p>
        <p>Croft, died at his residence On S—anday. The deceased, who was 63 years of age, went to the Warrington Infirmary nearly five weeks ago to undergo an operation by the advice of his medical attendants, and having somewhat recovered he returned home on Saturday. On Sunday he experi- enced great difficulty in breathing, and died at one o'clock in the afternoon. He had long been a sufferer from bronchitis. He was some years ago a member of the Leigh Local Board. For the past six years he has been one of the church-wardens of Christ Church, Croft, and he was also elected a member of the School Board for the district of Southworth-with-Croft at the last triennial election in October. The funeral took place on Wednesday at Leigh Cemetery.</p>
        <p>SEWING MAcarN us Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome walnut cover.—.T. and W. DUTTON. 16 and 18, Sank ey-st., Warrington. —Advt. LATCHFORD 600 D TEMPLARS AND THE RECENT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>LICEN SING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LICEN SING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>An open session of the Bonehill Branch Lodge of the Independent Order of Good Templars was held in the Temperance Hall, Latchford, on Monday evening under the presidency of Bro. B. Walker, C.T. There was an excellent attendance, including friends and visitors. A letter of acknowledgment of flowers sent for the benefit of patients was read from the War- rington Infirmary. Bro. F. A. Jenkins, gave a report of the Quarterly Session of District Lodge, held at Northwich. The following resolution was moved and carried unanimously :—" That this Lodge wishes to publoly thank the ministers and members of the Temperance Federation and Social Questions Union for their earnest endea yours at the Brewster Ses- sions to reduce the number of licences in the borough, and to offer our hearty congratulation on the success they have obtained."—The meeting was closed by singing and prayer, after a very pleasant and profitable evening's entertainment.</p>
        <p>ALL HOUSEHOLD LINEN', hemmed and marked, free of charge.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-at., Warrington .—Advt. A WARRINGTON CAB PROPRIETOR IN TROUBLE. At the Altrincham Petty Sessions on Monday, Felix Maginn, cab proprietor, Warrington, and Thomas Morris, poultry dealer also of Warrington, were summoned for being concerned in cruelty to a horse. According to Inspector Luckings,R.S.P.O.A., Morris hired a horse and waggonette on Bank Holi- day,and drove from Warrington to Altrincham. The officer found old wounds on each side of the animal's back under the saddle, wounds under the crupper, wounds en the forehead and over the eyes, and the fetlock joints were bleeding where the horse had " brushed" coming along. There were eleven wounds altoge:her, and the animal was totally un- fit to work. Morris said he hired it on the under- taking of Maginn that it would be all right. Maginn stated that the animal was in good condition, the waggonette was a light one, and the wounds on the head and other parts were caused by the friction of new harness. The bench fined Maginn 20s. and costs or 14 days, and Morris ss. and costs or seven days. Mr. Jesse Haworth, who was on the bench, said Maginn ought to be sent to prison for sending a horse out in such a condition. ALL HOUSEHOLD LiNEN, hemmed and marked free of charge.—J. and W. DUTTON, iiankey-.t., Wareington.—Adit. EFFIGY OF LORD WINBIARLBIGH.</p>
        <p>There is being placed in the Warrington Parish Church a life-size effigy of the late Lord Winniar- leigh, erected at the expense of the Hon. Misses Wilson-Patten. The work has been admirably exe- cuted by Mr. H. H. Armstead, ILA., a well-known sculptor. It is chaste in design, and gives a most life like picture in Carari marble of the late Lord Winmarleigh. On the sides of the plinth on which the effigy reclines is the following inscription in Latin :—" John Wilson-Patten, Baro De Winmar- leigh, vixit annes xd. dies Lxxv obit xi die Julii, ' Ann° ulnas MDOCCXCII in Christ°. In manna tuaa commendo spiritum meum nam redemisti me Domine." The English reading is:—" John Wilson- Patten, Baron of Winmarleigh, lived 90 years 75 days, died 11th July in the year of our Lord 1892. In Thy hands I commend my Spirit for Thou hut redeemed me,o Lord." The head rests on a pillow on which is cut the monogram of the late nobleman and his wife, while at the foot of the effigy is their united coats of arms. The effigy has been on exhi- bition at the Royal Academy, and only arrived in Warrington on Monday. It is being placed on the memorial tomb the late nobleman had erected to the memory of his wife, Anna Maria Wilson-Patten, by Mr. Armstead's workmen, and will probably be opened to public view at the beginning of next week.</p>
      </div>
