      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Sr. HELENS PLATE-GLASS TRAD-g</head>
        <p>THE Sr. HELENS PLATE-GLASS TRAD-g</p>
        <p>A STOPPAGE. The men employed in the St. Helens works of the London and Manchester Plate-glass Company finished work on Tuesday for an indefinite period and the works whk oeewpr tee ovcnel eat dhoaretdnet thri erteogl ya s of keepnu rtnWhaece glass Inaaetssssd pots. yottt. so understand,nsloVvvaekoel.s s being wcausesiilnotthbee hidopleevteorybeloexn gpressed that the SuttoannViVotrhikss SHAREHOLDERS' MEETING AT MANCHESTER- An extraordinary general meeting of the shareholders of the London and Manchester Plate-Glass Company was held on Thursday, in the Memorial Hall, Albert- square, Manchester, for the purpose of consideringithe following resolution :—" That it has been proved to the satisfaction of this meeting that the company cannot, by reason of its liabilities, continue its business, and that it is expedient to wind up the same voluntarily." Mr' William Hawkins, chairman of the company, Pteci sided, and there were upwards of one hundred shareholders present, including Colonel Gamble, C.B. Alderman Bishop, Alderman M'Kechnie Gasket', Councillor Foote. Mr. Amos Hanson, Mr. France' Mr. Smith, Mr. Thomas Walker and Mr. John S. White, secretary, all of St. Helens; and Mr. Saul Moss, of Liver- pool. The proceedings were conducted in private. The report of the directors stated that in the past year, as in the two previous ones, the company had unfortunate't ' , sustained a serious loss, amounting to £60,132. At WO 25 per cent. of it was directly attributable to the star Ipage of the ordinary supplies of fuel, during the great coal strike of 1893, and an additional 12 per cent. to the 'further decline in the selling price of plate-glass. there been no stoppage in the fuel supplies and nl‘. decline in the price of glass, the loss would et!: have been serious. With a reduction in &amp;mat' there was a still keener competition, and though effor'' had been made from time to time to effect an under' , standing between continental and English makers, those efforts had so far proved unsuccessful. The directo„,rs who retired on the present occasion were Messrs. "1 Barlow and Frederick Taylor, who.offered thetriselvl for re-election. The vacancy created by toe death oi Mr. Bedell had been ,filled by the appointment of birci Henry Grendon Tippet, a gentleman largely intereste,. in the company. The balance-sheet showed the'' about three years ago mortgage debentures Of the amount of £200,000 were authorised (but this £llB,OOO was not issued) for the purpose o.l' putting down new and perfected machinery RI, the manufacture of plate glass. A sum of £85,80 was stated to be the value of the glass and material and stock. The loss last year amounted to £60,132, an this added to a loss of £82,933 in the two preceeding years made a total loss ot £143.000. After two hours' deliberation the meeting paSeed resolution, and appointed Mr. H. A. Binney, toe gerng-f I manager of the works ; and Mr. Crokson, of the firm°, IJones, Crookson, and Company, of Manchester, a' liquidators ; and the following gentlemen were ar pointed a committee of inspection : Sir T.Bazley,Bal; Aldermen Msßechnie and Bishop, of St. Helens John Jeffries, of Manchester ; Mr. Henry Tippett, Mr. Hawking, the chairman.</p>
